{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T02:38:43.548Z","ordinal":7143,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0c3176283919dd68016aa2181159f087d0b064e14ab4882e82","status":"completed","call_id":"call_RZJINTSYn781BSkPULxBvoZX","name":"exec","input":"const remove = \"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Delete File: /Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/shared/references/rust-tooling.md\\n*** Delete File: /Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/shared/references/typescript-tooling.md\\n*** End Patch\";\nconst add = \"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Add File: /Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/shared/references/rust-tooling.md\\n+# Rust Verification Tooling\\n+\\n+## Outcome\\n+\\n+Choose the smallest project-native checks that can falsify the changed behavior. Tool names alone are not evidence.\\n+\\n+## Discover capabilities\\n+\\n+Inspect `Cargo.toml`, workspace configuration, task runners, CI, and existing tests before choosing commands. Prefer configured tasks; otherwise use targeted Cargo commands and then consider the applicable workspace suite.\\n+\\n+| Changed risk | Useful capability when available | Evidence to record |\\n+| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n+| Local behavior | `cargo test` or configured `cargo nextest` target | Test target and observed result |\\n+| Type, feature, or crate boundary | `cargo check` under relevant features or targets | Configuration exercised |\\n+| Weak assertions | `cargo-mutants` or a safe deliberate fault | Wrong behavior rejected by the test |\\n+| Combinatorial or stateful input | `proptest` or an existing fuzz target | Generator, oracle, and minimized failure |\\n+| FFI or NAPI boundary | Project integration test or boundary harness | Boundary crossed and mocked segment |\\n+| Packaging or generated files | Project build/package task | Produced artifact inspected |\\n+\\n+## Candidate quality checks\\n+\\n+Apply these only when the repository contract or changed risk makes them relevant:\\n+\\n+- Panic paths: determine whether `unwrap`, `expect`, or `panic!` is reachable from recoverable input before treating it as a defect.\\n+- Unsafe code: verify the safety contract and boundary ownership; location alone is not proof of unsoundness.\\n+- Output ordering: require deterministic maps or sets only when output is user-visible, serialized, snapshot-tested, or contractually ordered.\\n+- String construction: investigate allocation only when measurement or a hot path makes it material.\\n+- UTF-8 slicing: reject byte indexing when the index can split a character boundary.\\n+- Public documentation: follow the repository's lint and public API policy rather than inventing one.\\n+- Plan or config fields: trace values to the execution boundary when changed behavior depends on them.\\n+\\n+Record unsupported checks as coverage gaps. Do not install optional tooling without authorization.\\n*** Add File: /Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/shared/references/typescript-tooling.md\\n+# TypeScript and JavaScript Verification Tooling\\n+\\n+## Outcome\\n+\\n+Choose project-native checks that can falsify the changed behavior across the runtime, type, bundle, or process boundary involved.\\n+\\n+## Discover capabilities\\n+\\n+Inspect `package.json` scripts, workspace configuration, lockfiles, task runners, CI, and existing tests. Prefer configured commands over guessed runner invocations.\\n+\\n+| Changed risk | Useful capability when available | Evidence to record |\\n+| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n+| Local behavior | Configured Vitest, Jest, Bun, or Node test target | Test target and observed result |\\n+| Type boundary | Configured type-check command | Project and configuration exercised |\\n+| Weak assertions | Stryker or a safe deliberate fault | Wrong behavior rejected by the test |\\n+| Combinatorial or stateful input | `fast-check` or configured generator | Arbitrary, oracle, and minimized failure |\\n+| Bundle or package boundary | Configured build/package task | Produced artifact or import path inspected |\\n+| Process or network boundary | Integration harness | Boundary crossed and mocked segment |\\n+\\n+## Candidate quality checks\\n+\\n+Apply these only when the repository contract or changed risk makes them relevant:\\n+\\n+- Dynamic values: prevent `any` from escaping an unvalidated boundary; a local, justified escape hatch is not automatically a defect.\\n+- Promises: ensure started async work has an owner for completion and failure; framework-managed handlers may provide that owner.\\n+- Logging: follow the application's logging contract. `console` use is not inherently wrong in CLIs, scripts, or configured runtimes.\\n+- Mutability: use `readonly` where mutation would violate ownership, not as a universal shape requirement.\\n+- Cloning: choose behavior that preserves the required value types and target runtime; neither JSON cloning nor `structuredClone` is universally interchangeable.\\n+- Variant handling: require exhaustive handling when the set is closed and silent fallback would be wrong.\\n+- Exported documentation: follow repository policy and document non-obvious contracts rather than restating signatures.\\n+\\n+Record unsupported checks as coverage gaps. Do not add dependencies or install optional tooling without authorization.\\n*** End Patch\";\nawait tools.apply_patch(remove);\ntext(await tools.apply_patch(add));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a088c6-48bb-7040-a264-83f7cdb1775a","create_time":1789007881.900346}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T02:38:43.587Z","ordinal":7144,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a086d4-219f-74e1-8171-83f651770f25","turn_id":"01a088c6-48bb-7040-a264-83f7cdb1775a","session_id":"01a086d4-219f-74e1-8171-83f651770f25","root_turn_id":"01a088c6-48bb-7040-a264-83f7cdb1775a","response_id":"resp_0c3176283919dd68016aa218095e2487d0a1f2f021cf502b12","usage":{"input_tokens":187714,"cached_input_tokens":187008,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1252,"reasoning_output_tokens":136,"total_tokens":188966},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":2682514,"cached_input_tokens":2474752,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":11736,"reasoning_output_tokens":4817,"total_tokens":2694250},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":106692383,"cached_input_tokens":104499840,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":479112,"reasoning_output_tokens":157472,"total_tokens":107171495}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T02:38:43.759Z","ordinal":7145,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a086d4-219f-74e1-8171-83f651770f25","turn_id":"01a088c6-48bb-7040-a264-83f7cdb1775a","item":{"type":"FileChange","id":"exec-09536da3-1154-43fc-b871-40c5825ccff8","changes":{"/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/shared/references/rust-tooling.md":{"type":"delete","content":"# Rust Tooling Reference\n\n_Loaded by: mutator, remediator. Contains test commands, build checks, and the non-negotiables that every fix must satisfy. Do not load for scanning or smell analysis._\n\n---\n\n## Workspace Test Discovery & Execution\n\nBefore running tests, check for project task runners to respect repo-specific workflows:\n\n```bash\n# Check for task runners\nls justfile Makefile moonrepo.yml .moon/ 2>/dev/null\n```\n\n- Prefer the configured task runner.\n- Otherwise use `cargo nextest` when configured or `cargo test` with the narrowest useful package or target.\n- Use `cargo-mutants` when available and relevant to changed behavior.\n- Use `proptest` or an existing fuzz target for combinatorial inputs when a structured generator and meaningful invariant are available.\n- Run the applicable workspace suite before completion.\n\n---\n\n## NAPI Boundary Rules\n\n_Applies only to crates with `napi` or `napi-derive` in `Cargo.toml`. Skip for CLI tools and pure Rust libraries._\n\n- Every `#[napi]` export must have `#[napi(catch_unwind)]`.\n- No `unsafe` outside the NAPI boundary.\n- Return `napi::Result<T>`, not bare `T`.\n\n---\n\n## Non-Negotiables\n\nEvery fix must satisfy these before the green pass is claimed:\n\n- No `unwrap()`/`expect()` in lib code (tests may use `expect` with a message).\n- No `unsafe` outside an explicit FFI/NAPI boundary.\n- `BTreeMap`/`BTreeSet` for output maps and error payloads — never `HashMap`/`HashSet` in user-visible or test-comparable output.\n- `String::with_capacity` for strings built in loops.\n- String truncation uses `floor_char_boundary`, never byte-index slicing.\n- Every `pub` item has a doc comment.\n- Plan/config struct fields must survive to the execution call site — confirm each field reaches the subprocess or downstream function.\n"},"/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/shared/references/typescript-tooling.md":{"type":"delete","content":"# TypeScript Tooling Reference\n\n_Loaded by: mutator, remediator. Contains test runner detection, test commands, and the non-negotiables every fix must satisfy. Do not load for scanning or smell analysis._\n\n---\n\n## Workspace Test Discovery & Execution\n\nBefore running tests, check `package.json` scripts and workspace task runners:\n\n```bash\n# Check package scripts and task runner configs\njq '.scripts' package.json 2>/dev/null\nls vitest.config.* jest.config.* bun.test.* moonrepo.yml turbo.json 2>/dev/null\n```\n\n- Prefer configured package scripts or task runners.\n- Otherwise target the relevant Vitest, Jest, Bun, or Node test file.\n- Use the configured type-check command for changed type boundaries.\n- Use Stryker when available and relevant to changed behavior.\n- Use `fast-check` or another configured generator for combinatorial inputs when a structured arbitrary and meaningful invariant are available.\n- Exercise compiled, bundled, or process boundaries when the change depends on them.\n- Run the applicable workspace suite before completion.\n\n---\n\n## Non-Negotiables\n\nEvery fix must satisfy these before the green pass is claimed:\n\n- No `any` in new code without an inline comment explaining why.\n- No unhandled promise rejections — every `async` call is either `await`ed, `.catch()`ed, or explicitly `void`-ed with a comment.\n- All exported functions have JSDoc or TSDoc.\n- No `console.log` in library code — use a structured logger or remove.\n- Config and options interfaces must be `readonly`.\n- No `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(x))` — use `structuredClone`.\n- Discriminated union switches must have an `assertNever` default.\n"}},"status":"completed","stdout":"Success. Updated the following files:\nD /Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/shared/references/rust-tooling.md\nD /Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/shared/references/typescript-tooling.md\n","stderr":""},"started_at_ms":1789007923611,"completed_at_ms":1789007923759}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T02:38:43.914Z","ordinal":7146,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a086d4-219f-74e1-8171-83f651770f25","turn_id":"01a088c6-48bb-7040-a264-83f7cdb1775a","item":{"type":"FileChange","id":"exec-11016bd7-028c-4ff6-8079-41ce690e3232","changes":{"/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/shared/references/typescript-tooling.md":{"type":"add","content":"# TypeScript and JavaScript Verification Tooling\n\n## Outcome\n\nChoose project-native checks that can falsify the changed behavior across the runtime, type, bundle, or process boundary involved.\n\n## Discover capabilities\n\nInspect `package.json` scripts, workspace configuration, lockfiles, task runners, CI, and existing tests. Prefer configured commands over guessed runner invocations.\n\n| Changed risk | Useful capability when available | Evidence to record |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Local behavior | Configured Vitest, Jest, Bun, or Node test target | Test target and observed result |\n| Type boundary | Configured type-check command | Project and configuration exercised |\n| Weak assertions | Stryker or a safe deliberate fault | Wrong behavior rejected by the test |\n| Combinatorial or stateful input | `fast-check` or configured generator | Arbitrary, oracle, and minimized failure |\n| Bundle or package boundary | Configured build/package task | Produced artifact or import path inspected |\n| Process or network boundary | Integration harness | Boundary crossed and mocked segment |\n\n## Candidate quality checks\n\nApply these only when the repository contract or changed risk makes them relevant:\n\n- Dynamic values: prevent `any` from escaping an unvalidated boundary; a local, justified escape hatch is not automatically a defect.\n- Promises: ensure started async work has an owner for completion and failure; framework-managed handlers may provide that owner.\n- Logging: follow the application's logging contract. `console` use is not inherently wrong in CLIs, scripts, or configured runtimes.\n- Mutability: use `readonly` where mutation would violate ownership, not as a universal shape requirement.\n- Cloning: choose behavior that preserves the required value types and target runtime; neither JSON cloning nor `structuredClone` is universally interchangeable.\n- Variant handling: require exhaustive handling when the set is closed and silent fallback would be wrong.\n- Exported documentation: follow repository policy and document non-obvious contracts rather than restating signatures.\n\nRecord unsupported checks as coverage gaps. Do not add dependencies or install optional tooling without authorization.\n"},"/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/shared/references/rust-tooling.md":{"type":"add","content":"# Rust Verification Tooling\n\n## Outcome\n\nChoose the smallest project-native checks that can falsify the changed behavior. Tool names alone are not evidence.\n\n## Discover capabilities\n\nInspect `Cargo.toml`, workspace configuration, task runners, CI, and existing tests before choosing commands. Prefer configured tasks; otherwise use targeted Cargo commands and then consider the applicable workspace suite.\n\n| Changed risk | Useful capability when available | Evidence to record |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Local behavior | `cargo test` or configured `cargo nextest` target | Test target and observed result |\n| Type, feature, or crate boundary | `cargo check` under relevant features or targets | Configuration exercised |\n| Weak assertions | `cargo-mutants` or a safe deliberate fault | Wrong behavior rejected by the test |\n| Combinatorial or stateful input | `proptest` or an existing fuzz target | Generator, oracle, and minimized failure |\n| FFI or NAPI boundary | Project integration test or boundary harness | Boundary crossed and mocked segment |\n| Packaging or generated files | Project build/package task | Produced artifact inspected |\n\n## Candidate quality checks\n\nApply these only when the repository contract or changed risk makes them relevant:\n\n- Panic paths: determine whether `unwrap`, `expect`, or `panic!` is reachable from recoverable input before treating it as a defect.\n- Unsafe code: verify the safety contract and boundary ownership; location alone is not proof of unsoundness.\n- Output ordering: require deterministic maps or sets only when output is user-visible, serialized, snapshot-tested, or contractually ordered.\n- String construction: investigate allocation only when measurement or a hot path makes it material.\n- UTF-8 slicing: reject byte indexing when the index can split a character boundary.\n- Public documentation: follow the repository's lint and public API policy rather than inventing one.\n- Plan or config fields: trace values to the execution boundary when changed behavior depends on them.\n\nRecord unsupported checks as coverage gaps. Do not install optional tooling without authorization.\n"}},"status":"completed","stdout":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA /Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/shared/references/rust-tooling.md\nA /Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/shared/references/typescript-tooling.md\n","stderr":""},"started_at_ms":1789007923805,"completed_at_ms":1789007923914}}
