`plugin.json`\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"my-plugin\",\n  \"name\": \"my-plugin\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"description\": \"One sentence.\",\n  \"author\": {\n    \"name\": \"Gabriel Castro\",\n    \"email\": \"gabe@techworx.dev\"\n  },\n  \"skills\": \"./skills/\",\n  \"agents\": [\"./agents/recon.md\", \"./agents/worker.md\", \"./agents/exit-gate.md\"]\n}\n```\n\n`skills` is always the path string `\"./skills/\"` — the loader discovers every `SKILL.md` under it, whether that's one directory or several. `agents` is the explicit list of agent file paths; every file listed must exist, and every file present must be listed (`mason`'s `auditor` checks this).\n\n### `SKILL.md`\n\nThe skill prompt is what the user invokes. It should:\n\n- Have a concise frontmatter `description` covering when to activate the skill, its input, its output, and key constraints\n- Describe the agent pipeline: which subagents run in what order, and what each returns\n- Reference shared schemas by exact relative path\n- Use the shortest direct wording that preserves each action, condition, evidence requirement, and consequence\n\n---\n\n## Agent Authoring Checklist\n\nRead `shared/agent-best-practices.md` before authoring. The hard requirements:\n\n### 5-part structure (frontmatter + body)\n- [ ] **Constitution** — copied byte-for-byte from an existing agent, never authored fresh. See `shared/constitution.md`'s Static Prompt Prefix Invariant.\n- [ ] **Backstory** — 2–4 sentences. What has this agent been burned by? What does it value? Guides judgment without constraining method.\n- [ ] **Goal** — what the agent must produce and why. Intent, not steps.\n- [ ] **Judgment** — how to tell if the goal was genuinely achieved vs. output that merely looks like it was. Name the key failure mode.\n- [ ] **Output** — structured shape; reference a schema from `shared/schemas/` when output flows to another agent.\n\nNo `<role>` section in the body — that's what `<backstory>` replaced. The frontmatter `role:` field is platform metadata (short display label for routing) and should be kept. No `success_criteria:` checklist. No filler: \"your job is to\", \"make sure to\", \"please ensure\".\n\n### Instruction economy\n\n- [ ] Each paragraph or list item contains one independently actionable rule\n- [ ] Sub-bullets express real branches, evidence, or attributes rather than split a long sentence cosmetically\n- [ ] Numbered steps are reserved for operations whose order affects correctness\n- [ ] Repeated rationale, process narration, filler, and synonymous restatement are removed\n- [ ] Non-obvious context appears once, at the decision it changes\n- [ ] Bodies over 300 words and list items over 40 words are reviewed, not automatically rejected\n\n### Progressive context loading\n- [ ] Declares a `<load_first>` block naming the specific `shared/references/` file for this agent's phase\n- [ ] Does not load reference files outside its cognitive mode (scanner loads hazards, not smells)\n- [ ] For Codex, every exact `shared/` path loaded directly or transitively appears in `harnesses/codex/catalog.json`; historical mentions omit the path when runtime access is unnecessary\n\n### EARS placement\n- [ ] EARS notation (`WHEN`, `IF`, `WHILE`, `WHERE`) used only in output contracts and never-do rules\n- [ ] No EARS in implementation steps, search strategies, or reasoning guidance\n\n### Model and effort\n- [ ] `haiku` / `low` — deterministic enumeration only (manifest building, file inventory)\n- [ ] `sonnet` / `medium` — analysis (scanning, drafting, planning, tracing)\n- [ ] `opus` / `high` — judgment (adversarial reasoning, exit gates, final verdicts)\n\n### Tool language\n- [ ] Uses abstract tool language: \"use your file reading tool\", \"use your search tool\"\n- [ ] No tool-specific calls (`view_file`, `read_file`, `grep_search`, etc.)\n- [ ] No absolute paths\n\n### Schema conformance\n\n- [ ] If the output is a named schema (e.g. `finding-report@2`), every field in the output shape is declared in `shared/schemas/` — no undeclared fields, no missing required fields\n\n### Verification evidence\n\n- [ ] Behavioral claims name evidence that can falsify them; tool invocation alone is not evidence\n- [ ] Generative checks name the generator and oracle\n- [ ] Semantic sibling searches derive candidates from domain responsibility, state transitions, and architecture, then record each outcome\n- [ ] External or asynchronous checks use fresh state and terminal results\n- [ ] Unsupported or disproportionate checks are recorded as coverage gaps\n\n---\n\n## Adding a Shared Schema\n\nShared schemas in `shared/schemas/` are the inter-plugin API. To add one:\n\n1. Create `shared/schemas/<name>@<version>.json` using JSON Schema draft-2020-12\n2. Set `additionalProperties: false`\n3. Include a `reasoning: string` field\n4. Document which plugin produces it and which consume it\n5. **Never modify an existing versioned schema** — create `<name>@<version+1>.json` instead\n\n---\n\n## Registering in `marketplace.json`\n\nAdd an entry to the `\"plugins\"` array:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"my-plugin\",\n"},"started_at_ms":1789008421266,"completed_at_ms":1789008421266}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T02:47:01.319Z","ordinal":7309,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a08936-21c7-7c60-b97a-da3eaaf066e5","call_id":"call_GQtbiNIjSjqTosh0SZCpVrnr","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.3 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"MATCHES\nARCHITECTURE.md-27-    class T3 store\nARCHITECTURE.md-28-```\nARCHITECTURE.md-29-\nARCHITECTURE.md:30:Agents pull reference files themselves using their file reading tool. The host never pre-loads them.\nARCHITECTURE.md-31-\nARCHITECTURE.md-32----\nARCHITECTURE.md-33-\n--\nARCHITECTURE.md-140-| A fix is structurally complete | A shared root cause and an explicit decision: scoped repair, consolidation, or Scribe architecture remediation |\nARCHITECTURE.md-141-| Mutable or asynchronous behavior passes | Fresh state and terminal results for every relevant started check |\nARCHITECTURE.md-142-\nARCHITECTURE.md:143:Unavailable or disproportionate checks become explicit coverage gaps; invoking a tool without a relevant oracle proves nothing. Rust, TypeScript/JavaScript, Python, and Go references suggest compatible tools without making one command mandatory.\nARCHITECTURE.md-144-\nARCHITECTURE.md-145-`mason:evaluate` tests workflow behavior with hidden-oracle fixtures. It records the fixture, route, harness, host, model, mode, roles, plugin versions, source revisions, workspace state, detections, false passes, corrections, duration, and available usage counts. Structural parity remains a build check; behavioral improvement requires comparable evidence.\nARCHITECTURE.md-146-\n--\nARCHITECTURE.md-241-- Modes: enumeration (haiku/low), tracing/analysis/repair (sonnet/medium), adversarial judgment (opus/high)\nARCHITECTURE.md-242-- A plugin with distinct scanning, tracing, adversarial, and repair phases has a dedicated agent per mode\nARCHITECTURE.md-243-\nARCHITECTURE.md:244:**Progressive context loading** — each agent loads only the reference file for its cognitive phase.\nARCHITECTURE.md:245:- A `<load_first>` block names the specific `shared/references/` file — scanner loads hazards, architect loads smells, mutator loads tooling\nARCHITECTURE.md:246:- Never load all reference files into all agents — attention degrades when the context window contains material the agent won't use\nARCHITECTURE.md-247-\nARCHITECTURE.md-248-**EARS for edges only** — EARS notation (`WHEN`, `IF`, `WHILE`, `WHERE`) belongs in output contracts and never-do rules.\nARCHITECTURE.md-249-- Not in implementation steps or search strategies — those are the interior where agent judgment is the point\nARCHITECTURE.md-250-- Over-constraining the interior caps the agent at the level of the author's imagination\nARCHITECTURE.md-251-\nARCHITECTURE.md:252:**Schema-driven handoffs** — where one agent's output is another's input, both reference the same schema file from `shared/schemas/`. The schema is the contract; the prompt describes the intent.\nARCHITECTURE.md-253-\nARCHITECTURE.md-254----\nARCHITECTURE.md-255-\n--\nARCHITECTURE.md-342-### Build guarantees\nARCHITECTURE.md-343-\nARCHITECTURE.md-344-- The generator resolves every input path beneath the repository root and rejects links that escape it.\nARCHITECTURE.md:345:- Runtime source links are dereferenced into regular files; generated bundles contain no symlinks.\nARCHITECTURE.md-346-- Authored native manifests and skill files are copied byte-for-byte; generated marketplace order follows the catalog.\nARCHITECTURE.md-347-- A normal build writes into a sibling temporary directory, then replaces `dist/codex` only after successful generation.\nARCHITECTURE.md-348-- If a build fails after moving the previous output aside, the generator restores the previous output.\n--\nARCHITECTURE.md-361-    ├── skills/<skill>/SKILL.md             # Authored native Codex workflow\nARCHITECTURE.md-362-    ├── agent-roles/*.md                    # Packaged cognitive-mode delegation cards\nARCHITECTURE.md-363-    ├── shared/schemas/*.json               # Materialized contracts needed by this plugin\nARCHITECTURE.md:364:    └── shared/references/*.md              # Materialized only when a native skill declares it\nARCHITECTURE.md-365-```\nARCHITECTURE.md-366-\nARCHITECTURE.md-367-The generated bundle intentionally omits Claude agent files. A Codex skill owns its workflow, uses shared JSON contracts, and may request a team only when that runtime capability exists. It selects a packaged role card before delegation; the role card guides cognitive mode and ownership but is not a TOML runtime configuration. This prevents a host-specific Claude agent topology from becoming a false Codex runtime contract.\n--\nARCHITECTURE.md-382-| Release drift | `tools/build-codex-marketplace.py --check` | Generated root discovery or `dist/codex` artifacts that differ from source inputs |\nARCHITECTURE.md-383-| Diagram syntax | `scripts/check-mermaid.sh` | Markdown diagrams that cannot render |\nARCHITECTURE.md-384-\nARCHITECTURE.md:385:CI runs the catalog JSON, native build, compatibility, drift, version, source-documentation, reference-size, and Mermaid checks. The release payload and its native-source, catalog, and schema change belong in the same review; a generated-only change is not a valid release.\nARCHITECTURE.md-386-\nARCHITECTURE.md-387-## 12. Failure Containment and Compatibility Boundaries\nARCHITECTURE.md-388-\n--\nAGENTS.md-12-- [**`shared/agent-best-practices.md`**](./shared/agent-best-practices.md): Principles behind the constitution, explained with examples. Authoring-time only — never loaded by agents at runtime.\nAGENTS.md-13-- [**`shared/debugging-laws.md`**](./shared/debugging-laws.md): Core proof laws, read-only investigation rules, Red-to-Green verification standards.\nAGENTS.md-14-- [**`shared/harness-authoring.md`**](./shared/harness-authoring.md): Cross-harness change classification, native workflow boundaries, and required counterpart review.\nAGENTS.md:15:- [**`shared/references/modern-cli-tools.md`**](./shared/references/modern-cli-tools.md): Global preference directive for modern CLI tools (`bat`, `zoxide`, `ripgrep`, `fd`, `eza`, `delta`, `jq`, `fzf`, `gh`).\nAGENTS.md-16-\nAGENTS.md-17----\nAGENTS.md-18-\n--\nAGENTS.md-44-1. **Use `mason`** — run `scaffolder` to generate the full directory structure and stubs.\nAGENTS.md-45-2. **5-part agent structure** — every agent body: `<constitution>` (copied byte-for-byte from an existing agent), `<backstory>`, `<goal>`, `<judgment>`, `<output>`. No `<role>` body sections. No `success_criteria` checklists.\nAGENTS.md-46-3. **EARS in output only** — WHEN/IF/WHILE/WHERE notation belongs only in `<output>` contracts and never-do rules.\nAGENTS.md:47:4. **Progressive context loading** — one `<load_first>` block per agent, naming only the reference file for its cognitive phase.\nAGENTS.md-48-5. **Schema-first handoffs** — define `shared/schemas/<name>@<version>.json` before writing the agent that produces it.\nAGENTS.md-49-6. **Abstract tool language** — \"use your file reading tool\", never `view_file`, `read_file`, or `Bash tool`.\nAGENTS.md:50:7. **Instruction economy** — keep one actionable rule per item, remove repeated rationale, and number only order-dependent work.\nAGENTS.md-51-8. **Verification evidence** — match checks to risk; require generators and oracles, independently derived semantic candidates, fresh state, terminal results, and explicit gaps when relevant.\nAGENTS.md-52-9. **Cross-harness review** — preserve identity, skill routes, artifact contracts, evidence, and acceptance intent in Claude/AGY and Codex sources; native wording and delegation may differ.\nAGENTS.md-53-10. **Manifest registration** — add an entry to root `marketplace.json` and validate: `jq . marketplace.json`.\n--\nCLAUDE.md-22-python3 -m unittest discover -s tests\nCLAUDE.md-23-./scripts/check-versions.sh\nCLAUDE.md-24-./scripts/check-skills-doc.sh\nCLAUDE.md:25:./scripts/check-reference-size.sh\nCLAUDE.md-26-python3 tools/build-codex-marketplace.py --check\nCLAUDE.md-27-./scripts/check-mermaid.sh\nCLAUDE.md-28-```\n--\nCONTRIBUTING.md-83-- [ ] **Backstory** — 2–4 sentences. What has this agent been burned by? What does it value? Guides judgment without constraining method.\nCONTRIBUTING.md-84-- [ ] **Goal** — what the agent must produce and why. Intent, not steps.\nCONTRIBUTING.md-85-- [ ] **Judgment** — how to tell if the goal was genuinely achieved vs. output that merely looks like it was. Name the key failure mode.\nCONTRIBUTING.md:86:- [ ] **Output** — structured shape; reference a schema from `shared/schemas/` when output flows to another agent.\nCONTRIBUTING.md-87-\nCONTRIBUTING.md-88-No `<role>` section in the body — that's what `<backstory>` replaced. The frontmatter `role:` field is platform metadata (short display label for routing) and should be kept. No `success_criteria:` checklist. No filler: \"your job is to\", \"make sure to\", \"please ensure\".\nCONTRIBUTING.md-89-\nCONTRIBUTING.md:90:### Instruction economy\nCONTRIBUTING.md-91-\nCONTRIBUTING.md-92-- [ ] Each paragraph or list item contains one independently actionable rule\nCONTRIBUTING.md-93-- [ ] Sub-bullets express real branches, evidence, or attributes rather than split a long sentence cosmetically\n--\nCONTRIBUTING.md-97-- [ ] Bodies over 300 words and list items over 40 words are reviewed, not automatically rejected\nCONTRIBUTING.md-98-\nCONTRIBUTING.md-99-### Progressive context loading\nCONTRIBUTING.md:100:- [ ] Declares a `<load_first>` block naming the specific `shared/references/` file for this agent's phase\nCONTRIBUTING.md:101:- [ ] Does not load reference files outside its cognitive mode (scanner loads hazards, not smells)\nCONTRIBUTING.md-102-- [ ] For Codex, every exact `shared/` path loaded directly or transitively appears in `harnesses/codex/catalog.json`; historical mentions omit the path when runtime access is unnecessary\nCONTRIBUTING.md-103-\nCONTRIBUTING.md-104-### EARS placement\n--\nCONTRIBUTING.md-165-\nCONTRIBUTING.md-166-- [ ] Frontmatter `description` reflects the new capability\nCONTRIBUTING.md-167-- [ ] If the agent's output gained a new status/enum value, the plugin's `SKILL.md` names what the caller does with it — or states it's terminal\nCONTRIBUTING.md:168:- [ ] If a new `shared/references/*.md` file was added, it's listed in root `README.md`'s Shared References table\nCONTRIBUTING.md-169-- [ ] Every prose description still matches the agent's current behavior:\nCONTRIBUTING.md-170-  - Plugin README subagent and output-schema sections\nCONTRIBUTING.md-171-  - SKILL.md overview, dispatch, and I/O sections\nCONTRIBUTING.md-172-  - Narrowed or corrected capabilities, not only additions\nCONTRIBUTING.md-173-- [ ] Plugin `CHANGELOG.md` gets a new dated entry\nCONTRIBUTING.md:174:- [ ] `plugin.json` version bumped (see the Semver Decision Guide in `shared/references/changesets.md`)\nCONTRIBUTING.md-175-- [ ] Root `marketplace.json`'s matching plugin entry version bumped to the same value\nCONTRIBUTING.md-176-- [ ] `mason:scaffolder` and `mason:auditor` reflect any new structural convention; capability-only changes need no Mason update\nCONTRIBUTING.md-177-\n--\nCONTRIBUTING.md-183-\nCONTRIBUTING.md-184-- **No absolute paths:** prompt and skill paths are relative.\nCONTRIBUTING.md-185-- **Self-contained subagents:** each prompt is readable in isolation and has no dependency on another agent's private prompt.\nCONTRIBUTING.md:186:- **Pull, don't inject:** subagents load `shared/references/` on demand; the host does not preload them.\nCONTRIBUTING.md-187-- **Authoring-only guidance:** never load `shared/agent-best-practices.md` at runtime.\nCONTRIBUTING.md-188-- **Abstract tool language:** keep prompts portable across harnesses.\nCONTRIBUTING.md-189-- **Conventional commits:** use the applicable type and plugin scope.\n--\nCONTRIBUTING.md-213-# Check every skill a README documents actually has a skills/ directory\nCONTRIBUTING.md-214-./scripts/check-skills-doc.sh\nCONTRIBUTING.md-215-\nCONTRIBUTING.md:216:# Check every shared/references/ file stays at or under the 120-line cap —\nCONTRIBUTING.md:217:# a reference loaded via <load_first> is a fixed tax on every agent\nCONTRIBUTING.md-218-# invocation that loads it.\nCONTRIBUTING.md:219:./scripts/check-reference-size.sh\nCONTRIBUTING.md-220-\nCONTRIBUTING.md-221-# Check schema files parse correctly\nCONTRIBUTING.md-222-jq . shared/schemas/*.json > /dev/null\n\nCONTRIBUTING\n# Contributing\n\nBy participating in this project, you agree to abide by the [Code of Conduct](./CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md). Found a security issue? See [SECURITY.md](./SECURITY.md) instead of opening a public issue.\n\n---\n\n## Authoring a New Plugin\n\nThe fastest way to scaffold a new plugin is with the `mason` plugin:\n\n```\n\"Create a plugin called <id> that does <goal>\"\n```\n\n`scaffolder` generates the full directory structure, `plugin.json`, `SKILL.md`, and stub subagent files. Run `auditor` afterward to verify conformance.\n\nTo scaffold manually:\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p plugins/<id>/skills/<id> plugins/<id>/agents\nln -s ../../shared plugins/<id>/shared\n```\n\nThen create `plugin.json`, `skills/<id>/SKILL.md`, and `agents/*.md` following the conventions below.\n\n---\n\n## Plugin Structure\n\n```\nplugins/<id>/\n├── plugin.json\n├── README.md\n├── CHANGELOG.md\n├── skills/<id>/SKILL.md\n└── agents/\n    ├── scanner.md       (sonnet/medium — pattern matching)\n    ├── worker.md        (sonnet/medium — analysis)\n    ├── adversary.md     (opus/high — adversarial reasoning)\n    └── exit-gate.md     (opus/high — final judgment)\n```\n\nOrganize agents by **cognitive mode**, not pipeline position. A scan agent and an analysis agent require different mental modes — split them even if they run sequentially. Agent files are never prefixed with the plugin id (`worker.md`, not `my-plugin-worker.md`) — Claude Code already namespaces agents as `<plugin_id>:<agent_name>`, so a prefix produces redundant `my-plugin:my-plugin-worker` stuttering. See `shared/agent-best-practices.md`'s Namespacing Rule.\n\nA plugin defaults to this single-skill layout. IF its scope covers several genuinely independent, heterogeneous intents on the same artifact — not a linear pipeline invoked as one flow — `skills/<id>/` may split into `skills/<skill-name>/SKILL.md` per skill instead (see `courier`, `scribe`, `weaver`, `mason`). A new skill name defaults to a single lifecycle-stage word; go to a specific compound name (`audit-spec`, not `scribe-audit`) only when the bare word is ambiguous or collides with another plugin's existing skill name — never resolve a collision by prefixing the plugin id, since `plugin:skill` invocation already disambiguates that. See `shared/constitution.md`'s Plugin Structure and Skill Names sections.\n\n### `plugin.json`\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"my-plugin\",\n  \"name\": \"my-plugin\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"description\": \"One sentence.\",\n  \"author\": {\n    \"name\": \"Gabriel Castro\",\n    \"email\": \"gabe@techworx.dev\"\n  },\n  \"skills\": \"./skills/\",\n  \"agents\": [\"./agents/recon.md\", \"./agents/worker.md\", \"./agents/exit-gate.md\"]\n}\n```\n\n`skills` is always the path string `\"./skills/\"` — the loader discovers every `SKILL.md` under it, whether that's one directory or several. `agents` is the explicit list of agent file paths; every file listed must exist, and every file present must be listed (`mason`'s `auditor` checks this).\n\n### `SKILL.md`\n\nThe skill prompt is what the user invokes. It should:\n\n- Have a concise frontmatter `description` covering when to activate the skill, its input, its output, and key constraints\n- Describe the agent pipeline: which subagents run in what order, and what each returns\n- Reference shared schemas by exact relative path\n- Use the shortest direct wording that preserves each action, condition, evidence requirement, and consequence\n\n---\n\n## Agent Authoring Checklist\n\nRead `shared/agent-best-practices.md` before authoring. The hard requirements:\n\n### 5-part structure (frontmatter + body)\n- [ ] **Constitution** — copied byte-for-byte from an existing agent, never authored fresh. See `shared/constitution.md`'s Static Prompt Prefix Invariant.\n- [ ] **Backstory** — 2–4 sentences. What has this agent been burned by? What does it value? Guides judgment without constraining method.\n- [ ] **Goal** — what the agent must produce and why. Intent, not steps.\n- [ ] **Judgment** — how to tell if the goal was genuinely achieved vs. output that merely looks like it was. Name the key failure mode.\n- [ ] **Output** — structured shape; reference a schema from `shared/schemas/` when output flows to another agent.\n\nNo `<role>` section in the body — that's what `<backstory>` replaced. The frontmatter `role:` field is platform metadata (short display label for routing) and should be kept. No `success_criteria:` checklist. No filler: \"your job is to\", \"make sure to\", \"please ensure\".\n\n### Instruction economy\n\n- [ ] Each paragraph or list item contains one independently actionable rule\n- [ ] Sub-bullets express real branches, evidence, or attributes rather than split a long sentence cosmetically\n- [ ] Numbered steps are reserved for operations whose order affects correctness\n- [ ] Repeated rationale, process narration, filler, and synonymous restatement are removed\n- [ ] Non-obvious context appears once, at the decision it changes\n- [ ] Bodies over 300 words and list items over 40 words are reviewed, not automatically rejected\n\n### Progressive context loading\n- [ ] Declares a `<load_first>` block naming the specific `shared/references/` file for this agent's phase\n- [ ] Does not load reference files outside its cognitive mode (scanner loads hazards, not smells)\n- [ ] For Codex, every exact `shared/` path loaded directly or transitively appears in `harnesses/codex/catalog.json`; historical mentions omit the path when runtime access is unnecessary\n\n### EARS placement\n- [ ] EARS notation (`WHEN`, `IF`, `WHILE`, `WHERE`) used only in output contracts and never-do rules\n- [ ] No EARS in implementation steps, search strategies, or reasoning guidance\n\n### Model and effort\n- [ ] `haiku` / `low` — deterministic enumeration only (manifest building, file inventory)\n- [ ] `sonnet` / `medium` — analysis (scanning, drafting, planning, tracing)\n- [ ] `opus` / `high` — judgment (adversarial reasoning, exit gates, final verdicts)\n\n### Tool language\n- [ ] Uses abstract tool language: \"use your file reading tool\", \"use your search tool\"\n- [ ] No tool-specific calls (`view_file`, `read_file`, `grep_search`, etc.)\n- [ ] No absolute paths\n\n### Schema conformance\n\n- [ ] If the output is a named schema (e.g. `finding-report@2`), every field in the output shape is declared in `shared/schemas/` — no undeclared fields, no missing required fields\n\n### Verification evidence\n\n- [ ] Behavioral claims name evidence that can falsify them; tool invocation alone is not evidence\n- [ ] Generative checks name the generator and oracle\n- [ ] Semantic sibling searches derive candidates from domain responsibility, state transitions, and architecture, then record each outcome\n- [ ] External or asynchronous checks use fresh state and terminal results\n- [ ] Unsupported or disproportionate checks are recorded as coverage gaps\n\n---\n\n## Adding a Shared Schema\n\nShared schemas in `shared/schemas/` are the inter-plugin API. To add one:\n\n1. Create `shared/schemas/<name>@<version>.json` using JSON Schema draft-2020-12\n2. Set `additionalProperties: false`\n3. Include a `reasoning: string` field\n4. Document which plugin produces it and which consume it\n5. **Never modify an existing versioned schema** — create `<name>@<version+1>.json` instead\n\n---\n\n## Registering in `marketplace.json`\n\nAdd an entry to the `\"plugins\"` array:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"my-plugin\",\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a088c6-48bb-7040-a264-83f7cdb1775a","create_time":1789008421.319093}}}
