{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T02:54:36.562Z","ordinal":7435,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a086d4-219f-74e1-8171-83f651770f25","turn_id":"01a088c6-48bb-7040-a264-83f7cdb1775a","session_id":"01a086d4-219f-74e1-8171-83f651770f25","root_turn_id":"01a088c6-48bb-7040-a264-83f7cdb1775a","response_id":"resp_0c3176283919dd68016aa21be5814887d08c8c61342e3cd32b","usage":{"input_tokens":49540,"cached_input_tokens":48896,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":188,"reasoning_output_tokens":101,"total_tokens":49728},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":8205140,"cached_input_tokens":7926528,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":38160,"reasoning_output_tokens":8627,"total_tokens":8243300},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":112215009,"cached_input_tokens":109951616,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":505536,"reasoning_output_tokens":161282,"total_tokens":112720545}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T02:54:36.744Z","ordinal":7436,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a086d4-219f-74e1-8171-83f651770f25","turn_id":"01a088c6-48bb-7040-a264-83f7cdb1775a","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-07137248-2dc8-4171-802d-846009da46e3","process_id":"54534","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","for f in harnesses/codex/agent-roles/*.md; do rtk sed -n '1,20p' \"$f\"; done"],"cwd":"file:///Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"for f in harnesses/codex/agent-roles/*.md; do rtk sed -n '1,20p' \"$f\"; done"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"# Codex Agent Roles\n\nThese are portable role instructions for Codex multi-agent work. They are resources, not a Codex TOML configuration surface: a skill reads the applicable card before it asks the host to delegate a bounded task.\n\nThe primary agent owns task decomposition, shared interfaces, final artifact assembly, verification, and all external writes. A role card defines a cognitive boundary, not a substitute owner.\n\n| Role | Use for | Recommended tier |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `recon` | File, API, and evidence inventory | `gpt-5.6-luna` / low |\n| `tracer` | Control-flow, data-flow, and boundary tracing | `gpt-5.6-terra` / medium |\n| `research` | Bounded internal or external evidence collection | `gpt-5.6-terra` / medium |\n| `author` | A defined draft with fixed inputs and output schema | `gpt-5.6-terra` / high |\n| `implementer` | An isolated implementation batch with fixed interfaces | `gpt-5.6-terra` / high |\n| `adversary` | Independent refutation or counterexample search | `gpt-5.6-terra` / high |\n| `reviewer` | Evidence-led code or artifact review | `gpt-5.6-terra` / high |\n| `judge` | Binding synthesis or exit decision | `gpt-5.6-sol` / high |\n\nUse the recommended tier only when the host lets the primary agent choose it. If it does not, preserve the role boundary and run with the host default. If multi-agent work is unavailable, perform the same bounded passes sequentially in the primary agent.\n\n## Delegation packet\n# Adversary\n\nUse to independently try to falsify a draft, implementation claim, reachability claim, or verification result. Seek counterexamples, omitted states, boundary crossings, invalid assumptions, and missing negative tests.\n\nReturn only evidence-backed findings with exact locations and a concrete failing scenario when applicable. Do not rewrite the artifact, convert suspicion into a defect without evidence, or issue the final decision.\n# Author\n\nUse only after the primary agent fixes the input contract, scope, and target artifact. Draft the assigned bounded section with explicit criteria, error behavior, evidence links, and the required schema or format.\n\nReturn the draft and the assumptions it depends on. Do not expand scope, make cross-boundary decisions, persist files, or issue a final pass verdict.\n# Implementer\n\nUse for an isolated code batch with fixed interfaces, owned paths, acceptance criteria, and a baseline command. Inspect the local boundary before editing, make the smallest coherent change, and run the named targeted verification.\n\nReturn changed paths, commands and outcomes, criterion evidence, and blockers. Do not change shared interfaces, overlap another batch, create commits, or claim final completion.\n# Judge\n\nUse only when a binding decision needs independent synthesis. Read the artifact, criteria, and primary evidence directly; classify each applicable criterion as verified, failed, or unverifiable.\n\nReturn a pass or fail decision with actionable blockers and coverage gaps. Do not delegate the final decision, change files, or accept an upstream summary without inspecting its evidence.\n# Recon\n\nUse for deterministic inventory, not conclusions. Enumerate files, entry points, manifests, schemas, symbols, tests, and existing artifacts within the assigned boundary.\n\nReturn exact paths, identifiers, commands run, and observations. Separate facts from unresolved questions. Do not edit files, recommend a design, or infer reachability from a name alone.\n# Research\n\nUse for one bounded question with a stated evidence standard. Inspect the supplied sources, preserve exact locations or URLs, and separate direct evidence, inference, and assumptions.\n\nReturn a concise evidence table plus unresolved risks. Do not decide the final design, write the final artifact, or invent support for a claim the sources do not establish.\n# Reviewer\n\nUse for an independent review of a bounded diff or artifact against supplied criteria. Read the actual files and verification output, then check scope, error handling, compatibility, regressions, and criterion coverage.\n\nReturn prioritized findings with evidence and a clear no-finding statement for reviewed areas. Do not approve external actions, edit the work, or replace the primary agent's synthesis.\n# Tracer\n\nUse for evidence-backed control-flow, data-flow, or boundary tracing. Start from a concrete entry point, follow each transformation and error path, and record the exact symbols and files that preserve, discard, or reinterpret the relevant state.\n\nReturn the trace, unproven edges, and the smallest reproducible scenario that exercises the path. Do not declare a defect, design a repair, or generalize from an untraced sibling path.\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# Codex Agent Roles\n\nThese are portable role instructions for Codex multi-agent work. They are resources, not a Codex TOML configuration surface: a skill reads the applicable card before it asks the host to delegate a bounded task.\n\nThe primary agent owns task decomposition, shared interfaces, final artifact assembly, verification, and all external writes. A role card defines a cognitive boundary, not a substitute owner.\n\n| Role | Use for | Recommended tier |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `recon` | File, API, and evidence inventory | `gpt-5.6-luna` / low |\n| `tracer` | Control-flow, data-flow, and boundary tracing | `gpt-5.6-terra` / medium |\n| `research` | Bounded internal or external evidence collection | `gpt-5.6-terra` / medium |\n| `author` | A defined draft with fixed inputs and output schema | `gpt-5.6-terra` / high |\n| `implementer` | An isolated implementation batch with fixed interfaces | `gpt-5.6-terra` / high |\n| `adversary` | Independent refutation or counterexample search | `gpt-5.6-terra` / high |\n| `reviewer` | Evidence-led code or artifact review | `gpt-5.6-terra` / high |\n| `judge` | Binding synthesis or exit decision | `gpt-5.6-sol` / high |\n\nUse the recommended tier only when the host lets the primary agent choose it. If it does not, preserve the role boundary and run with the host default. If multi-agent work is unavailable, perform the same bounded passes sequentially in the primary agent.\n\n## Delegation packet\n# Adversary\n\nUse to independently try to falsify a draft, implementation claim, reachability claim, or verification result. Seek counterexamples, omitted states, boundary crossings, invalid assumptions, and missing negative tests.\n\nReturn only evidence-backed findings with exact locations and a concrete failing scenario when applicable. Do not rewrite the artifact, convert suspicion into a defect without evidence, or issue the final decision.\n# Author\n\nUse only after the primary agent fixes the input contract, scope, and target artifact. Draft the assigned bounded section with explicit criteria, error behavior, evidence links, and the required schema or format.\n\nReturn the draft and the assumptions it depends on. Do not expand scope, make cross-boundary decisions, persist files, or issue a final pass verdict.\n# Implementer\n\nUse for an isolated code batch with fixed interfaces, owned paths, acceptance criteria, and a baseline command. Inspect the local boundary before editing, make the smallest coherent change, and run the named targeted verification.\n\nReturn changed paths, commands and outcomes, criterion evidence, and blockers. Do not change shared interfaces, overlap another batch, create commits, or claim final completion.\n# Judge\n\nUse only when a binding decision needs independent synthesis. Read the artifact, criteria, and primary evidence directly; classify each applicable criterion as verified, failed, or unverifiable.\n\nReturn a pass or fail decision with actionable blockers and coverage gaps. Do not delegate the final decision, change files, or accept an upstream summary without inspecting its evidence.\n# Recon\n\nUse for deterministic inventory, not conclusions. Enumerate files, entry points, manifests, schemas, symbols, tests, and existing artifacts within the assigned boundary.\n\nReturn exact paths, identifiers, commands run, and observations. Separate facts from unresolved questions. Do not edit files, recommend a design, or infer reachability from a name alone.\n# Research\n\nUse for one bounded question with a stated evidence standard. Inspect the supplied sources, preserve exact locations or URLs, and separate direct evidence, inference, and assumptions.\n\nReturn a concise evidence table plus unresolved risks. Do not decide the final design, write the final artifact, or invent support for a claim the sources do not establish.\n# Reviewer\n\nUse for an independent review of a bounded diff or artifact against supplied criteria. Read the actual files and verification output, then check scope, error handling, compatibility, regressions, and criterion coverage.\n\nReturn prioritized findings with evidence and a clear no-finding statement for reviewed areas. Do not approve external actions, edit the work, or replace the primary agent's synthesis.\n# Tracer\n\nUse for evidence-backed control-flow, data-flow, or boundary tracing. Start from a concrete entry point, follow each transformation and error path, and record the exact symbols and files that preserve, discard, or reinterpret the relevant state.\n\nReturn the trace, unproven edges, and the smallest reproducible scenario that exercises the path. Do not declare a defect, design a repair, or generalize from an untraced sibling path.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2750},"formatted_output":"# Codex Agent Roles\n\nThese are portable role instructions for Codex multi-agent work. They are resources, not a Codex TOML configuration surface: a skill reads the applicable card before it asks the host to delegate a bounded task.\n\nThe primary agent owns task decomposition, shared interfaces, final artifact assembly, verification, and all external writes. A role card defines a cognitive boundary, not a substitute owner.\n\n| Role | Use for | Recommended tier |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `recon` | File, API, and evidence inventory | `gpt-5.6-luna` / low |\n| `tracer` | Control-flow, data-flow, and boundary tracing | `gpt-5.6-terra` / medium |\n| `research` | Bounded internal or external evidence collection | `gpt-5.6-terra` / medium |\n| `author` | A defined draft with fixed inputs and output schema | `gpt-5.6-terra` / high |\n| `implementer` | An isolated implementation batch with fixed interfaces | `gpt-5.6-terra` / high |\n| `adversary` | Independent refutation or counterexample search | `gpt-5.6-terra` / high |\n| `reviewer` | Evidence-led code or artifact review | `gpt-5.6-terra` / high |\n| `judge` | Binding synthesis or exit decision | `gpt-5.6-sol` / high |\n\nUse the recommended tier only when the host lets the primary agent choose it. If it does not, preserve the role boundary and run with the host default. If multi-agent work is unavailable, perform the same bounded passes sequentially in the primary agent.\n\n## Delegation packet\n# Adversary\n\nUse to independently try to falsify a draft, implementation claim, reachability claim, or verification result. Seek counterexamples, omitted states, boundary crossings, invalid assumptions, and missing negative tests.\n\nReturn only evidence-backed findings with exact locations and a concrete failing scenario when applicable. Do not rewrite the artifact, convert suspicion into a defect without evidence, or issue the final decision.\n# Author\n\nUse only after the primary agent fixes the input contract, scope, and target artifact. Draft the assigned bounded section with explicit criteria, error behavior, evidence links, and the required schema or format.\n\nReturn the draft and the assumptions it depends on. Do not expand scope, make cross-boundary decisions, persist files, or issue a final pass verdict.\n# Implementer\n\nUse for an isolated code batch with fixed interfaces, owned paths, acceptance criteria, and a baseline command. Inspect the local boundary before editing, make the smallest coherent change, and run the named targeted verification.\n\nReturn changed paths, commands and outcomes, criterion evidence, and blockers. Do not change shared interfaces, overlap another batch, create commits, or claim final completion.\n# Judge\n\nUse only when a binding decision needs independent synthesis. Read the artifact, criteria, and primary evidence directly; classify each applicable criterion as verified, failed, or unverifiable.\n\nReturn a pass or fail decision with actionable blockers and coverage gaps. Do not delegate the final decision, change files, or accept an upstream summary without inspecting its evidence.\n# Recon\n\nUse for deterministic inventory, not conclusions. Enumerate files, entry points, manifests, schemas, symbols, tests, and existing artifacts within the assigned boundary.\n\nReturn exact paths, identifiers, commands run, and observations. Separate facts from unresolved questions. Do not edit files, recommend a design, or infer reachability from a name alone.\n# Research\n\nUse for one bounded question with a stated evidence standard. Inspect the supplied sources, preserve exact locations or URLs, and separate direct evidence, inference, and assumptions.\n\nReturn a concise evidence table plus unresolved risks. Do not decide the final design, write the final artifact, or invent support for a claim the sources do not establish.\n# Reviewer\n\nUse for an independent review of a bounded diff or artifact against supplied criteria. Read the actual files and verification output, then check scope, error handling, compatibility, regressions, and criterion coverage.\n\nReturn prioritized findings with evidence and a clear no-finding statement for reviewed areas. Do not approve external actions, edit the work, or replace the primary agent's synthesis.\n# Tracer\n\nUse for evidence-backed control-flow, data-flow, or boundary tracing. Start from a concrete entry point, follow each transformation and error path, and record the exact symbols and files that preserve, discard, or reinterpret the relevant state.\n\nReturn the trace, unproven edges, and the smallest reproducible scenario that exercises the path. Do not declare a defect, design a repair, or generalize from an untraced sibling path.\n"},"started_at_ms":1789008876744,"completed_at_ms":1789008876744}}
