es/hazards/rust-boundaries.md`\nshared/references/verification/implementation-review.md:7:`shared/references/hazards/rust.md`\nshared/references/verification/implementation-review.md:8:`shared/references/architecture/typescript.md`\nshared/references/verification/implementation-review.md:8:`shared/references/hazards/typescript-boundaries.md`\nshared/references/verification/implementation-review.md:8:`shared/references/hazards/typescript.md`\nshared/references/verification/implementation-review.md:9:`shared/references/architecture/python.md`\nshared/references/verification/implementation-review.md:9:`shared/references/hazards/python-boundaries.md`\nshared/references/verification/implementation-review.md:9:`shared/references/hazards/python.md`\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_0c3176283919dd68016aa2f8fc78fc87d0abe3e9b99d9f36de","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"summary":[],"type":"reasoning"},{"call_id":"call_rkyAVRDTCQDg36bevJwJHjK7","id":"ctc_0c3176283919dd68016aa2f8fcef9887d0a06b65d22975a719","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"rtk sed -n '48,62p' plugins/courier/README.md \u0026\u0026 rtk sed -n '50,62p' plugins/sentinel/skills/gate/SKILL.md\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":8000}); text(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789065466.923927,"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_rkyAVRDTCQDg36bevJwJHjK7","id":"ctco_01a08c9c-a284-7190-93ec-bb66ac1d9c90","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789065470.596863,"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\n---\n\n## Subagents\n\n| Subagent | Role | Tier | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `commit-analyzer` | Commit Author | haiku / low | Reads staged diff and produces a conventional commit message explaining why, not what. |\n| `changeset-analyzer` | Changeset Extractor | sonnet / medium | Classifies `consumer_impact` and `semver_impact` from a git diff per the decision table in `changesets.md`, then produces a `changeset@2` whose summary detail scales with that classification. |\n| `pr-narrator` | PR Author | sonnet / medium | Writes a PR title and body from the reviewer's perspective — zero prior context assumed. |\n| `review-preprocessor` | Review Package Assembler | haiku / low | Bundles the diff, linked spec, test results, and open questions into a structured review package. Does not categorize comments — that's the caller's call, made with `shared/references/delivery/github.md`'s vocabulary. |\n| `release-summarizer` | Release Author | sonnet / medium | Aggregates `changeset@2` entries into a `release-artifact@2`, computing the version as `max(semver_impact)` and filtering `internal-only` entries. |\n\n`courier/post-review` has no subagent — posting is mechanical execution of already-drafted content, gated by user confirmation, not a judgment task.\n\n**Retry behavior**: Up to 3 retries. On each failure, the specific blockers are returned to the producing agent. At retry 3, escalate to the caller — do not loop indefinitely. Track `retry_count` in the verdict.\n\n\u003c/axiom_protocol\u003e\n\n---\n\n\u003cframework_references\u003e\n- [Verdict Schema](../../../shared/schemas/verdict@3.json)\n\u003c/framework_references\u003e\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_0c3176283919dd68016aa2f9032b4487d0bf0dff000e63e330","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"summary":[],"type":"reasoning"},{"call_id":"call_DgEq86ex2NciNtGDlJpPWqx2","id":"ctc_0c3176283919dd68016aa2f90db3e087d0842bf5716218a309","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"rtk rg -n --glob '!dist/**' --glob '!**/CHANGELOG.md' 'one exact path|one reference|the reference file|specific `shared/references/` file|reference file for its cognitive|named reference file|naming only the reference file|only the file for its cognitive|one-reference-file' AGENTS.md CONTRIBUTING.md README.md ARCHITECTURE.md shared plugins/mason plugins/ranger plugins/smith harnesses/codex/plugins\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":20000}); text(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789065472.863142,"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_DgEq86ex2NciNtGDlJpPWqx2","id":"ctco_01a08c9c-eb7c-7442-82e9-02cc98de7a21","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789065489.27688,"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"ARCHITECTURE.md:244:**Progressive context loading** — each agent loads only the reference file for its cognitive concern.\nARCHITECTURE.md:246:- A `\u003cload_first\u003e` block names one exact path. The first directory identifies the concern; a language filename narrows language-specific evidence.\nCONTRIBUTING.md:101:- [ ] Declares a `\u003cload_first\u003e` block naming the specific `shared/references/` file for this agent's phase\nAGENTS.md:47:4. **Progressive context loading** — one `\u003cload_first\u003e` block per agent, naming only the reference file for its cognitive phase.\nplugins/mason/README.md:109:| `\u003cload_first\u003e` correctness | Present whenever an agent's goal implies a lookup it can't do from memory; its named reference file actually resolves |\nshared/constitution.md:72:WHEN an agent requires a shared reference file, it SHALL load only the file for its cognitive phase — not all reference files.\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_0c3176283919dd68016aa2f913569c87d0857d82281943edbb","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"summary":[],"type":"reasoning"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_0c3176283919dd68016aa2f920309887d098a142e8c7e637bb","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"summary":[],"type":"reasoning"},{"call_id":"call_TM3QPzhtzc9EVjE3NQziIAHw","id":"ctc_0c3176283919dd68016aa2f922581887d09aae397adb36576b","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"rtk rg -n --glob '!dist/**' --glob '!**/CHANGELOG.md' 'time-bounded|in minutes|bite-sized|zero domain context|mechanically executable|exact code|No TBDs|no TBDs|clock-time|clock time' marketplace.json plugins/navigator harnesses/codex/plugins/navigator README.md ARCHITECTURE.md CONTRIBUTING.md shared\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":24000}); text(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789065490.160377,"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_TM3QPzhtzc9EVjE3NQziIAHw","id":"ctco_01a08c9d-3851-7442-94a4-990847dc63d8","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789065508.945977,"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"marketplace.json:67:      \"description\": \"Planning plugin. Decomposes specs into sequenced, bite-sized implementation plans with an implementation approach, exact code as a baseline, and the tests proving each criterion \\u2014 no write-test-first mandate. Tasks are sized to compilation boundaries, not a clock-time estimate. No TBDs. A developer with zero domain context can execute the plan mechanically.\"\nplugins/navigator/skills/plan/SKILL.md:4:  Trigger this skill when the user says \"write a plan for X\", \"how do we implement this spec\", \"break this down into tasks\", \"what are the implementation steps\", \"create an implementation plan\", or \"plan this out\". Also activate when a spec (spec@1) exists and the user is ready to move to implementation but hasn't broken it down yet. Produces a plan@1 artifact where every task includes exact file paths, a brief implementation approach, exact implementation code, the exact tests proving each criterion, a conventional commit message, and covers_criteria linking the task to specific acceptance criteria. spec_file_path and linked_requirement are propagated from the source spec. No TBDs. No \"implement as appropriate.\" Every step is mechanically executable by a developer with zero domain context.\nplugins/navigator/skills/plan/SKILL.md:11:Navigator decomposes a spec@1 into a sequenced, bite-sized plan@1 that a developer or agent can execute without making any design decisions. Every task is independently testable, time-bounded, and fully specified down to exact code and commit messages.\nplugins/navigator/skills/plan/SKILL.md:24:- **Estimate an existing plan** — `estimator` assigns effort estimates (in minutes) to each task in a plan@1, identifies parallelizable tasks, and surfaces blocking dependencies.\nplugins/navigator/skills/plan/SKILL.md:87:Every step must show exact code. \"Implement the feature\", \"add appropriate error handling\", and \"write tests as needed\" are not steps — they are abdications. If the exact code is not known, it must be derived from the spec and documented as a concrete choice, not deferred as a TBD.\nharnesses/codex/plugins/navigator/skills/plan/SKILL.md:40:- Do not use clock-time estimates as the reason to split a task; use transactional compilation and verification boundaries.\nharnesses/codex/plugins/navigator/skills/plan/SKILL.md:41:- Do not invent exact code without inspecting the live APIs it touches.\nplugins/navigator/README.md:107:No TBDs. No \"the implementer will decide.\" Every acceptance criterion must appear in at least one task's `covers_criteria` — `challenger` flags any criterion that appears in none as `orphaned-criteria`.\nplugins/navigator/agents/planner.md:7:  Delegate to this subagent when you have a completed spec@1 and need a sequenced, executable implementation plan. Input is a spec@1 (or its spec_file_path, read from disk if provided). Output is a plan@1 conforming to shared/schemas/plan@1.json, with spec_file_path and spec_hash propagated from the spec. Decomposes into dependency-ordered tasks (foundational types, then logic, then integration); each task includes exact file paths, a brief implementation approach, exact implementation code as a concrete baseline (smith's implementer may improve on its shape, not its scope), the exact tests proving each criterion it covers, the verification command, a commit message, and covers_criteria naming every acceptance criterion it addresses. Every criterion must appear in some task's covers_criteria. Each task is one independently reviewable unit of change, sized to its Subsystem Batch's compilation boundary rather than a clock-time estimate; no TBDs. Also runs in amend mode: given an existing plan@1, a corrected spec@1, and the changed criterion_ids, patches only the affected tasks (adding new ones for newly introduced criteria) and leaves the rest untouched. Route output to challenger.\nplugins/navigator/agents/planner.md:23:Decompose a spec@1 into a sequenced list of implementation tasks that a developer with no domain knowledge can execute without needing to decide what to build or which criteria it must satisfy. Order tasks by dependency — foundational types first, then logic, then integration. Each task must be independently implementable, testable in isolation, and small enough for a reviewer to evaluate in one pass — sized to its Subsystem Batch's compilation boundary, not a clock-time estimate calibrated to a human working session. In amend mode, do not re-decompose the whole spec — identify which tasks in the existing plan claim an affected criterion_id in their covers_criteria, rewrite only those tasks against the corrected criterion text, and add new tasks for any criterion that is new to this revision. Every other task, including its steps and commit message, stays exactly as it was.\nplugins/navigator/agents/planner.md:27:The plan succeeds if an implementer can work through every task in sequence, running exactly the specified commands, and arrive at a passing test suite that satisfies the spec's acceptance criteria — without needing to decide what to build or which criteria it must satisfy. The plan's exact code is a concrete baseline proving each task is achievable within its file targets and scope, not a shape the implementer is locked into — smith's implementer may adapt it, provided the same files, criteria, and tests are still satisfied.\nplugins/navigator/agents/planner.md:30:- Any task says \"implement\" or \"add\" without specifying the exact code.\nplugins/navigator/plugin.json:5:  \"description\": \"Planning plugin. Decomposes specs into sequenced, bite-sized implementation plans with an implementation approach, exact code as a baseline, and the tests proving each criterion — no write-test-first mandate. Tasks are sized to compilation boundaries, not a clock-time estimate. No TBDs. A developer with zero domain context can execute the plan mechanically.\",\nplugins/navigator/agents/estimator.md:7:  Delegate to this subagent when a plan@1 exists and you need a rough effort estimate before committing to execution. Input is a plan@1 JSON object. The agent produces a per-task time estimate in minutes, marks each task as parallelizable or sequential, lists blocking dependencies by task ID, and produces a total_minutes sum with explicit assumptions. The implementer is assumed to have full codebase context but no domain knowledge of the feature being built. A task is marked parallelizable only if it has no dependency on any other task in the current plan. All assumptions affecting the estimate are listed explicitly in an assumptions array. Output is a JSON object with total_minutes, a tasks array, and assumptions.\nplugins/navigator/agents/estimator.md:23:Given a plan@1, produce a per-task time estimate in minutes for a competent developer who has full codebase context but no domain knowledge of the feature. Identify which tasks can run in parallel and which must run sequentially. Surface every assumption that affects the estimates.\nshared/schemas/plan@1.json:5:  \"description\": \"A sequenced implementation plan. Every task is bite-sized, with an implementation approach, exact code, the tests proving each criterion, and a commit message.\",\nshared/schemas/plan@1.json:46:            \"description\": \"Ordered steps. Each step is one action. Code steps include exact code. Test steps include exact command and expected output.\"\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_0c3176283919dd68016aa2f928917087d09128aaa817d16734","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"summary":[],"type":"reasoning"},{"call_id":"call_ql2BqWvf3aDK3dk34BluDZUP","id":"ctc_0c3176283919dd68016aa2f92ccd0087d0a55db1cbd050f6ff","input":"const results = await Promise.all([\n  tools.exec_command({cmd:\"rtk rg -n --glob '!dist/**' --glob '!**/CHANGELOG.md' '\\\\b(38|39|40|41|42|43|44) (agent|file)|all [0-9]+ agents|one-reference-file' README.md AGENTS.md CONTRIBUTING.md ARCHITECTURE.md docs plugins shared harnesses/codex\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":15000}),\n  tools.exec_command({cmd:\"rtk rg -n --glob '!dist/**' --glob '!**/CHANGELOG.md' 'references/[^/[:space:]`]+\\\\.md' README.md AGENTS.md CONTRIBUTING.md ARCHITECTURE.md docs plugins shared harnesses/codex\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":25000}),\n  tools.exec_command({cmd:\"rtk rg -n --glob '!dist/**' --glob '!**/CHANGELOG.md' 'scanner.*(grep|regex)|grep.*scanner|hazard.*grep|deterministic.*implement' README.md ARCHITECTURE.md docs plugins shared harnesses/codex\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":25000}),\n  tools.exec_command({cmd:\"rtk jq -r '.plugins[] | [.id,.version,.description] | @tsv' marketplace.json \u0026\u0026 rtk rg -n '^\\\\*\\\\*Stage:.*\\\\*\\\\*Version:' plugins/*/README.md\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":20000})\n]); for (let i=0;i\u003cresults.length;i++) text(`---RESULT ${i+1} (exit ${results[i].exit_code})---\\n${results[i].output}`);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789065510.08221,"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_ql2BqWvf3aDK3dk34BluDZUP","id":"ctco_01a08c9d-708a-7cd3-bb4c-fbaa0730a85d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789065523.338799,"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"---RESULT 1 (exit 0)---\ndocs/adr/006-constitution-section-five-part-structure.md:9:`constitution.md`'s Static Prompt Prefix Invariant and `agent-best-practices.md` §7 both claimed every agent prompt shared an identical static header for prompt-cache reuse. In practice no agent file had one — each of the 38 agent bodies started directly with `\u003cbackstory\u003e` (or a `\u003cload_first\u003e` block), meaning the \"identical header\" was aspirational, not real, and rules meant to be ecosystem-wide (trust boundaries, output economy, reader-scoped writing, abstract tool language) were either copy-pasted with wording drift into whichever agents happened to need them, or silently absent elsewhere. A literal fix — identical content at the true start of every prompt — conflicted with two existing rules: the 4-part structure being described as exhaustive (\"not 5 sections\"), and EARS notation being confined to `\u003coutput\u003e` only, which meant shared EARS content had no section that was both first and permitted to hold it.\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"---RESULT 2 (exit 0)---\nCONTRIBUTING.md:180:- [ ] New references live under the reader's concern, have a real caller, and update `shared/references/README.md` when they introduce a concern\nREADME.md:303:See [`shared/references/README.md`](./shared/references/README.md) for the maintainer map.\nshared/schemas/changeset@2.json:18:      \"description\": \"Whether this change is observable to someone outside this repo. See shared/references/changesets.md for classification rules.\"\nshared/schemas/changeset@2.json:23:      \"description\": \"Version bump this change requires, per the Semver Decision Guide in shared/references/changesets.md. release-summarizer takes max(semver_impact) across a release's changesets rather than re-deriving it.\"\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"---RESULT 3 (exit 0)---\ndocs/adr/003-cognitive-mode-separation.md:8:Early plugin agents combined scanning and analysis in a single invocation — a single agent would grep for hazards and then assess whether each match was a real bug. This produced agents that were worse at both jobs: the scanner filtered matches because they \"looked harmless,\" creating false negatives the downstream agent could never recover; the analyst had insufficient depth because it was also doing exhaustive enumeration. The alternative considered was one agent per cognitive mode.\nplugins/ranger/agents/scanner.md:34:Run every hazard taxonomy grep pattern from the loaded reference against every live file and emit a candidate@1 entry for each match, so the adversary has a complete set of candidates to work with.\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"---RESULT 4 (exit 0)---\nmason\t3.2.2\tBuild, audit, and behaviorally evaluate plugins; design shared schemas and scaffold conformant agents. Skills: scaffold-plugin, audit-plugin, design-schema, scaffold-subagent, evaluate.\nranger\t3.2.2\tBug hunting for Rust, TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, and Go. Confirms reachable defects, searches semantic siblings, and routes structural families for architectural correction.\nsentinel\t2.1.1\tCross-cutting verification gate. Confirms that any stage artifact meets its criteria before the next stage begins. One skill, one recon-verify-judge pipeline, reused unchanged against any artifact type.\nweaver\t3.0.3\tNeed definition plugin. Captures and structures requirements before implementation begins. Integrates with GitHub Issues by default; extensible to Linear, Jira, or any issue tracker that produces the requirement@1 schema. Skills: capture-need, clarify-requirement, prioritize-backlog, connect-requirement, audit-backlog.\nvanguard\t2.0.2\tResearch plugin. Investigates questions and requirements to build a documented evidence base before committing to a solution direction. Produces research-report@1 for consumption by scribe (spec).\nscribe\t3.3.2\tSpecification plugin. Turns requirements and research reports into unambiguous, testable specs. Checks specs against a persisted whole-system architecture model. Detects spec drift against actual code. Skills: draft-spec, verify-spec, spec-drift, audit-spec, audit-architecture, gate-spec, correct-spec, architect.\nmuse\t1.0.1\tComponent specification plugin. Turns UI/design intent into unambiguous, testable component specs — props, variants, per-state behavior, and accessibility criteria as testable propositions, gated before planning. Audits the spec, not the implementation — see ranger's accessibility hazard taxonomy for code-level scanning.\nnavigator\t2.3.2\tPlanning plugin. Decomposes specs into sequenced, bite-sized implementation plans with an implementation approach, exact code as a baseline, and the tests proving each criterion — no write-test-first mandate. Tasks are sized to compilation boundaries, not a clock-time estimate. No TBDs. A developer with zero domain context can execute the plan mechanically.\nsmith\t2.4.2\tImplementation plugin. Executes tested plan batches, verifies risk at real boundaries, searches semantic defect siblings, and escalates shared model or architecture gaps.\ncourier\t3.0.3\tShipping plugin. Shepherds completed code through commit, PR creation, review response, and changeset documentation. Skills: commit, pr, changeset, receive-feedback, post-review, release.\n\nplugins/sentinel/README.md:3:**Stage:** Gate · **Output:** `verdict@3` · **Version:** 2.1.1\nplugins/weaver/README.md:3:**Stage:** Need · **Output:** `requirement@1` · **Version:** 3.0.3\nplugins/navigator/README.md:3:**Stage:** Plan · **Output:** `plan@1` · **Version:** 2.3.2\nplugins/muse/README.md:3:**Stage:** Design · **Output:** `spec@1` · **Version:** 1.0.1\nplugins/scribe/README.md:3:**Stage:** Spec · **Output:** `spec@1` · **Version:** 3.3.2\nplugins/ranger/README.md:3:**Stage:** Cross-cutting · **Output:** `finding-report@2` · **Version:** 3.2.2\nplugins/mason/README.md:3:**Stage:** Meta · **Output:** plugin artifacts and evaluations · **Version:** 3.2.2\nplugins/smith/README.md:3:**Stage:** Code · **Output:** code, `implementation-review@2`, `verdict@3` · **Version:** 2.4.2\nplugins/vanguard/README.md:3:**Stage:** Research · **Output:** `research-report@1` · **Version:** 2.0.2\nplugins/courier/README.md:3:**Stage:** Ship · **Output:** `release-artifact@2` · **Version:** 3.0.3\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"}],"latest_token_usage_record":{"response_id":"resp_0c3176283919dd68016aa2f933a19087d0a997e7feded78de0","root_turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e","session_id":"01a086d4-219f-74e1-8171-83f651770f25","thread_id":"01a086d4-219f-74e1-8171-83f651770f25","thread_token_usage":{"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":134264448,"input_tokens":137103162,"output_tokens":597472,"reasoning_output_tokens":199119,"total_tokens":137700634},"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e","turn_token_usage":{"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":3194112,"input_tokens":3231042,"output_tokens":10820,"reasoning_output_tokens":3997,"total_tokens":3241862},"usage":{"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":217088,"input_tokens":217918,"output_tokens":2998,"reasoning_output_tokens":0,"total_tokens":220916}},"message":"","previous_window_id":"01a08c79-9e4c-7050-b8cb-4428959b1e04","replacement_history":[{"content":[{"text":"I think we have some ongoing changes here right?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a086d4-4225-7fc1-85b1-e90f61d94abb","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788968452.64512,"turn_id":"01a086d4-405a-7e52-9524-a1761266da60"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Also, looking through our agent and skill docs we seem to have wordy prose in bullets which seems to be against our best practices no? Should some of these be broken up in sub-bullets? Is there a better way to organize things for better agent understanding?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a086d5-d773-7f23-a35a-5eeffd8f7a09","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788968556.403109,"turn_id":"01a086d5-d723-7841-9cc6-6e2838635983"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"The current changes should be related to looking for sibling defects and making sure that we consider semantic models and the architecture when looking at defects. If we found multiple considering patterns and what in the model and architecture could be missing that leaves room for these defects","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a086d6-dc3a-7470-b4dd-36a1ec036533","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788968623.162783,"turn_id":"01a086d5-d723-7841-9cc6-6e2838635983"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"We should lean on succinct and direct wording where possible without losing context. Would that help reduce some of the wordiness? We should avoid filler words and unecessary words when simpler and smaller phrases communicate the meaning and intent. How do we guide as such?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a086db-a500-7543-bff0-43683795c3a4","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788968936.704888,"turn_id":"01a086db-a445-7040-9235-c49f09f7349f"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's make the guidance and constitution changes and then tackle all of the improvements neceessary, applying this new guidance and constitution to the existing files along with accounting for sibblings, etc","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a086de-fde2-7711-9063-3aba846f7411","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788969156.066345,"turn_id":"01a086de-fd71-7c21-80b9-a036e4ae6eb7"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Some of these read like step by step too much, is that too prescriptive?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a086f0-f734-7ee0-92cb-799ded4e8c8d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788970334.004558,"turn_id":"01a086de-fd71-7c21-80b9-a036e4ae6eb7"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Are we losing some calls to the reference files in all of this? Is this a problem?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08702-0297-7231-b64a-b7df2e282026","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788971451.031161,"turn_id":"01a086de-fd71-7c21-80b9-a036e4ae6eb7"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Any more tweaks or improvements?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0871e-8e0e-79d0-ae73-7d7d09ad3eb7","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788973321.742119,"turn_id":"01a0871e-8ce4-7751-835b-cd43333c7c07"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's do that please","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08736-ac01-7500-9092-90473da98e21","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788974902.273279,"turn_id":"01a08736-ab43-7413-9675-c6fb9640c637"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Can we update docs and changelog to update?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08746-5b30-7800-b85a-b182f67f83cf","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788975930.160281,"turn_id":"01a08746-58ca-7ee2-b3ab-a45e43515705"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's prep and push a release","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0874e-22cf-71f3-939d-746e33a2b05d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788976440.015354,"turn_id":"01a0874e-1fe2-7811-a0f5-11dd27177d9a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"yes","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0876b-66f4-7f61-9a4e-4cb600293740","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788978358.004292,"turn_id":"01a0876b-6520-79d0-bf92-6ce575103990"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"do it","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08774-f894-7f23-88f2-833f05210b33","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788978985.108383,"turn_id":"01a08774-f7f4-7351-8c26-4473a878c2c6"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Can you read through this: https://danluu.com/agentic-testing/ and understand the methodology and issues outlined?\n\nThen review our callisto sessions from codex and claude looking for places where we did better than luu outline and where we failed just as much. Can we see if we're doing better with our plugins?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08781-c19f-7ab1-a295-ccc6077e34c3","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788979823.007717,"turn_id":"01a08781-c0dc-7360-be2f-39c7fedbf019"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"You might have to keep in mind what versions were available of our plugins at the time of the session too, lots has changed. We might be able to\n  see improvement over time.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08785-6af9-7452-8478-460d8775e293","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788980062.969926,"turn_id":"01a08781-c0dc-7360-be2f-39c7fedbf019"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Have you look at callisto sessions dating back to the past 30-45 days to see progress over time? How does it correlate to changes in our plugins over that time-period?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08791-f5fa-7980-bf02-81982d449441","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788980884.98625,"turn_id":"01a08791-f541-73d1-bfae-acc0ddfc3204"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"I fixed the entire login issue","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08797-873b-78f0-84cf-221e7319e405","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788981249.851112,"turn_id":"01a08797-86ce-7402-86fc-a28c8d7a7e20"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Entire should be logged in for the callisto folder, no?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0879c-2558-70f0-94e7-de77228d5f17","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788981552.472461,"turn_id":"01a0879a-11d2-7102-9864-546a98a6cc23"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Ok, pickup the work then and if not possible to use entire then look through sessions yourself. We have local sessions no?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a087a0-552e-7393-ac06-fed0288e95f8","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788981826.86281,"turn_id":"01a087a0-5473-71e2-9fe3-e5f72a7ea39d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's do what we can with just the sessions. Any further analysis we can do?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a087aa-5d4d-7642-a999-59ff3e6dae0b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788982484.301592,"turn_id":"01a087aa-5c60-7322-a1d4-dc034d402d62"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's do that examination please","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a087c3-2c14-7903-986b-5bca64218fb1","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788984110.100166,"turn_id":"01a087c3-2a1e-7401-ac78-e33107544865"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Some feedback from claude but take it with a large grain of salt. It had not access to our sessions like you did.\n\n2. The semantic-sibling search is the part most likely to be theater. Luu's formal-methods finding was that agents technically invoke a tool but prove trivial/irrelevant properties. \"Search for semantic siblings — same domain responsibility expressed differently\" is a much harder, fuzzier ask than syntactic grep, and the schema's only real guardrail is requiring evidence and a match count (including zero). That forces the agent to claim it searched, but doesn't verify the search was any good — an agent could report \"matches\": 0 with a thin evidence string and pass schema validation while having done a shallow pass. Given Luu's finding that agents' shallow-but-plausible-looking outputs are the dominant failure mode, I'd want a spot-check mechanism here — e.g., have exit-gate independently re-run the semantic search on a sample of families rather than trusting reviewer's self-report, the same way exit-gate already refuses to trust remediator's description of a fix.\n\n3. Nothing addresses proptest/fuzzing-style structured input exploration, which Luu found was one of the few things that reliably added value (via shrinking). This ecosystem's testing story is entirely mutation-testing + example-based tests; there's no equivalent of \"generate structured random inputs and shrink the failing case\" anywhere in smith. If bug-prone logic in a target codebase is best caught by property-based exploration rather than hand-written examples, this pipeline has no lever for it at all — mutation testing only tells you your existing tests are weak, not that you're missing a whole input-space region no example covers.\n\n4. Watch the token/instruction-economy tradeoff you're actively making. The new Instruction Economy section is a good instinct (Luu found bloated official skills reread ~20k tokens/run for no benefit), but it's now in tension with the constitution's own byte-identical \u003cconstitution\u003e block being repeated in all ~40 agents — that block itself hasn't been trimmed by the new economy rule, and every new EARS rule added there (there are now ~15 sections) grows what's cached and reread by every single agent regardless of relevance to its phase. Worth an audit pass: does every agent actually need every constitution clause, or has it become the same kind of \"comprehensive reference nobody trims\" problem the economy rule was written to prevent elsewhere?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a087c7-1873-7a02-879e-f8fce74d0494","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788984367.219512,"turn_id":"01a087c3-2a1e-7401-ac78-e33107544865"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's make sure we're not overly prescriptive and also consider that we also write TS a lot and our plugins might be used against python and go codebases too","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a087ce-f06e-7460-be93-34d49147a41e","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788984881.262262,"turn_id":"01a087ce-eeb8-74a0-aea4-60bb82f6f940"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"We can leverage tools and commands if available and where it makes sense for the language guide things like mutation and prop testing","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a087d1-4a60-7801-b759-52f260d98771","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788985035.360925,"turn_id":"01a087d1-48bb-7660-8fef-ad5c19c0b2c8"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"How should we take everything that you learned from the session analysis along with our discussions for a comprehensive improvement? Any new agents, skills, reference, etc?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a087d2-b36f-7721-9554-40780375539b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788985127.791537,"turn_id":"01a087d2-b201-7051-9899-bf9f14c38cf7"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's implement it??","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08818-66ae-76b2-b7d9-58b8daeabf58","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788989695.662938,"turn_id":"01a08818-6426-7061-ab53-205171fe211d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's update readme, architecture, changelogs, etc for the top-level and plugins. Make sure codex and claude are in sync","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08865-9ebc-7d60-b48a-8acbb180fc9e","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788994756.284925,"turn_id":"01a08865-9e02-7b01-8a4b-5f20e2531b6d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"What user configurations?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08879-cbe6-7362-a4be-55a4db8105f0","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788996078.566357,"turn_id":"01a08879-caae-7d03-8dad-03827171d03c"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's push those up right?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0887a-339e-78b3-b509-46d8a74ae233","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788996105.118702,"turn_id":"01a0887a-3350-7563-ad9f-f57b510c960d"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"yes","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0887f-1916-7bd3-a852-eda75af1947c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788996426.00687,"turn_id":"01a0887f-18bb-7f43-97ff-08ec26d52898"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Do we need the release PR then? If you pushed?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08882-e2d2-78e2-953a-ec7e49b81b37","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788996674.258793,"turn_id":"01a08882-e273-79d1-b80b-b2e355b0ebda"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Why do we have conflicts??","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08887-c5c5-71f3-a7ce-af12588c366d","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788996994.501882,"turn_id":"01a08887-c2ff-7270-b824-a5f6fa5fe6d3"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Just create the PR for me also","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08887-eba0-7ba1-9829-0ebc2daff551","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788997004.192221,"turn_id":"01a08887-c2ff-7270-b824-a5f6fa5fe6d3"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Just fix it","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08888-202a-72e1-aae4-479d09def21b","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788997017.642437,"turn_id":"01a08887-c2ff-7270-b824-a5f6fa5fe6d3"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"https://github.com/orin-dx/agent-plugins/pull/8","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08888-8fd1-7d43-a01d-56a8e34b5943","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788997046.225813,"turn_id":"01a08887-c2ff-7270-b824-a5f6fa5fe6d3"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"We have a PR","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08888-9004-7e73-b8eb-18cd85258b96","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788997046.276377,"turn_id":"01a08887-c2ff-7270-b824-a5f6fa5fe6d3"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Let's make it a high quality PR description and title","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08888-fcd4-7893-8b40-c4cbb705b2f0","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788997074.132753,"turn_id":"01a08887-c2ff-7270-b824-a5f6fa5fe6d3"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"good","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08891-13e7-73b0-8ebe-d305f4556e9f","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788997604.327279,"turn_id":"01a08891-1338-7cd1-af6f-01f8bee88790"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Can we make sure all our mermaid diagrams are up-to-date and follow the same modern look and feel?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a088a2-572f-72d2-a97e-9af8ccd93fbe","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788998735.663714,"turn_id":"01a088a2-565a-7a51-af8b-6d8ef6bc5083"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Push to main","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a088b4-b2fa-7220-9b71-c2813b9cae4c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1788999938.810244,"turn_id":"01a088a2-565a-7a51-af8b-6d8ef6bc5083"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Do we need to make our GH template much better?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a088ba-20dd-7d42-9cf5-906a2c831ae9","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789000294.62178,"turn_id":"01a088ba-2028-7c71-b1ef-640cfaa343ef"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"In the references?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a088ba-5ff3-7a40-b258-d29674cc5f2f","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789000310.7712,"turn_id":"01a088ba-2028-7c71-b1ef-640cfaa343ef"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Does documentation voice guide well for succinct, friendly? eng focused in apporpriate places and user focus where user-first? Devoid of AI slop, filler words and wordy prose?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a088bc-6e18-7f83-9f8c-e551184c5d27","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789000445.46466,"turn_id":"01a088bc-6dc6-7191-8ad3-173e3f6d6267"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Any other improvements we can make to references that grounds them in guidance and outcomes vs prescritiveness and prose heavy guidance?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a088bd-6ef7-7541-b377-ea90f27eff31","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789000511.223005,"turn_id":"01a088bc-6dc6-7191-8ad3-173e3f6d6267"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"we value radical candor too btw from our agents, in our docs, etc","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a088bd-cbe2-7723-b0ec-923e2478e8b0","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789000535.010288,"turn_id":"01a088bc-6dc6-7191-8ad3-173e3f6d6267"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"do it","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a088c6-497f-77c2-86ea-89d5c2008408","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789001091.455649,"turn_id":"01a088c6-48bb-7040-a264-83f7cdb1775a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"6ott7021!","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a0892d-e3d2-7070-8d92-9b0ad927bbfb","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789007881.170933,"turn_id":"01a088c6-48bb-7040-a264-83f7cdb1775a"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Create changesets and a PR","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08942-ab55-7923-bd3e-33a3701c8f49","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789009242.965495,"turn_id":"01a08942-aaa6-70d0-9a29-9e5a737952eb"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Yes, make the pr","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08c63-17a0-7540-9a52-b5b035a30d40","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789061699.488269,"turn_id":"01a08c63-15f4-7e83-8640-aa486d938a84"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Should we organize the references a bit? The folder is getting pretty large. What pattern should we organize around?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08c6c-2c80-7713-883c-f228e31e1b93","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789062294.656634,"turn_id":"01a08c6c-2bb7-7db0-a8c0-517545c5865e"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"What's best for the AI? separate by patterns or languages + patterns or somethign else?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08c71-9078-7840-aaee-0b4ad14188b8","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789062647.928132,"turn_id":"01a08c71-8fcb-7971-9d86-01ba35f02d09"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"That sounds reasonable","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08c75-f642-79c0-8f9d-ccd764797485","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789062936.130629,"turn_id":"01a08c75-f598-75f0-bd16-352bac96f99c"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Already merged that PR right?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08c76-9928-77d1-b708-f6d53fc709fe","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789062977.83233,"turn_id":"01a08c76-9731-72a1-b5ac-463278dd7699"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Ok, let's tackle the changs in the next one right?","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08c77-4c58-7f11-9290-4a448e7c5bcb","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789063023.704587,"turn_id":"01a08c77-4ac1-77a0-808c-4dde87d268d4"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cskills_instructions\u003e\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r1` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled`\n- `r2` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled/sites/0.1.52/skills`\n- `r3` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote`\n- `r4` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime`\n- `r5` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime/spreadsheets/26.905.11957/skills`\n- `r6` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/wisp-plugins/courier/3.0.2/skills`\n- `r7` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/wisp-plugins/mason/3.2.1/skills`\n- `r8` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/wisp-plugins`\n- `r9` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/wisp-plugins/scribe/3.3.1/skills`\n- `r10` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/wisp-plugins/weaver/3.0.2/skills`\n- `r11` = `/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/.agents/skills`\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should create a brand-new image, transform an existing image, or derive visual variants from references, and the output should be a bitmap asset rather than repo-native code or vector. Do not use when the task is better handled by editing existing SVG/vector/code-native assets, extending an established icon or logo system, or building the visual directly in HTML/CSS/canvas. (file: r0/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use for Codex models/pricing, scheduled tasks, skills, settings, setup, troubleshooting, customization, automations, and self-knowledge—including 'you,' 'your,' 'this app,' or 'this coding agent' when they refer to Codex—and for OpenAI APIs/products and ChatGPT Work. Also use for model choice/migration, prompting, SDKs, Responses, Realtime, agents, evals, and Chat/Work/Codex comparisons. Do not use for generic app/software tasks that merely mention Codex. (file: r0/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-creator: Create and scaffold plugin directories for Codex with a required `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, optional plugin folders/files, valid manifest defaults, and personal-marketplace entries by default. Use when Codex needs to create a new personal plugin, add optional plugin structure, generate or update marketplace entries for plugin ordering and availability metadata, or update an existing local plugin during development with the CLI-driven cachebuster and reinstall flow. (file: r0/plugin-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Create or update a Codex skill with appropriately scoped instructions and any needed supporting resources. (file: r0/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (file: r0/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- entire-search: Search Entire checkpoint history and transcripts with `entire search --json`. Use proactively when the user asks about previous work, commits, sessions, prompts, or historical context in this repository. (file: r11/entire-search/SKILL.md)\n- browser:control-in-app-browser: Control the in-app Browser for opening, navigating, inspecting visible or interactive page state, clicking, typing, screenshots, and local web testing. It can have existing signed-in sessions. For semantic operations on linked resources, prefer a purpose-built connector, API, or CLI when available. (file: r1/browser/26.831.20005/skills/control-in-app-browser/SKILL.md)\n- courier:changeset: Classify verified changes and produce one changeset per independent consumer-facing topic. Use when the user asks to add a changeset, document a change, or determine release impact. (file: r6/changeset/SKILL.md)\n- courier:commit: Prepare or create a conventional commit from staged work. Use when the user asks to commit changes or write a commit message; never create a commit without confirmation. (file: r6/commit/SKILL.md)\n- courier:post-review: Deliver already-drafted PR comments or reviews. Use when the user asks to post a prepared reply, submit review comments, or resolve a named thread; never author or post content without confirmation. (file: r6/post-review/SKILL.md)\n- courier:pr: Draft or open a reviewer-ready pull request from verified work. Use when the user asks to create a PR or write its description; require confirmation before any remote action. (file: r6/pr/SKILL.md)\n- courier:receive-feedback: Assemble a neutral evidence package for incoming PR feedback. Use when the user asks to understand, triage, or respond to review comments on their own pull request. (file: r6/receive-feedback/SKILL.md)\n- courier:release: Aggregate approved changesets into a release artifact. Use when the user asks to cut a release, calculate a version bump, or generate release notes from changesets. (file: r6/release/SKILL.md)\n- deep-research-work:deep-research: Use only when the user asks for deep research (or a clear equivalent), invokes $deep-research, or selects Deep Research in Work mode. Produce a comprehensive, cited artifact. Skip ordinary research requests. (file: r3/deep-research-work/0.1.15/skills/deep-research/SKILL.md)\n- mason:audit-plugin: Audit a plugin against the repository's concrete authoring and packaging rules. Use when the user asks whether a plugin conforms, is installable, or is ready to release. (file: r7/audit-plugin/SKILL.md)\n- mason:design-schema: Design a versioned shared JSON Schema for an inter-agent handoff. Use when a workflow needs a durable producer-consumer contract or an existing contract must evolve. (file: r7/design-schema/SKILL.md)\n- mason:evaluate: Evaluate a plugin workflow against a fixed hidden-oracle fixture. Use for release gates, regression checks, Claude/Codex comparisons, or longitudinal plugin analysis. (file: r7/evaluate/SKILL.md)\n- mason:scaffold-plugin: Create a complete plugin skeleton for a new workflow. Use when the user asks to build or scaffold a plugin; derive structure from the repository constitution and validate the result before handoff. (file: r7/scaffold-plugin/SKILL.md)\n- mason:scaffold-subagent: Add one conformant Claude-style agent prompt to an existing source plugin. Use when the user asks to add an agent without changing the plugin's unrelated routes. (file: r7/scaffold-subagent/SKILL.md)\n- muse:component: Turn UI intent into an implementation-ready component specification. Use when the user asks to specify, audit, or gate a component design before planning or implementation. (file: r8/muse/1.0.1/skills/component/SKILL.md)\n- navigator:plan: Turn an approved persisted specification into an executable implementation plan, amend a plan after a spec correction, estimate a plan, or adversarially challenge one. Use for “write a plan”, “break this spec into tasks”, “estimate this plan”, or “challenge this plan”. (file: r8/navigator/2.3.1/skills/plan/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-management:plugin-management: Discover and suggest relevant plugins, inspect app permissions and dependencies, and manage plugin connections or removal. Use when the user asks about plugins or when a task would materially benefit from an external app, account, service, or data source that available tools cannot access. (file: r3/plugin-management/0.1.0/skills/plugin-management/SKILL.md)\n- presentations:Presentations: Read, create or edit PowerPoint or Google Slides decks. Use for presentation, slide deck, PowerPoint, PPT, PPTX, or Google Slides requests. (file: r4/presentations/26.905.11957/skills/presentations/SKILL.md)\n- ranger:audit: Find evidence-backed reachable defects in Rust, TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, or Go. Use for bug hunts, security audits, suspected defects, or post-remediation verification; do not use for general code explanation. (file: r8/ranger/3.2.1/skills/audit/SKILL.md)\n- scribe:architect: Specify a structural correction when `finding-report@2`, `implementation-review@2`, or `implementation-result@1` reveals a recurring defect class, missing domain model, or architectural gap. (file: r9/architect/SKILL.md)\n- scribe:audit-architecture: Check a spec against a persisted workspace architecture model, or build or refresh that model. Use for “does this fit the architecture?”, “audit against the arch model”, or “map this codebase architecture”. (file: r9/audit-architecture/SKILL.md)\n- scribe:audit-spec: Adversarially review a spec for ambiguity, untestable criteria, missing error behavior, and scope defects. Use for “audit this spec”, “is this spec complete?”, or before gate-spec. (file: r9/audit-spec/SKILL.md)\n- scribe:correct-spec: Correct a persisted specification when implementation evidence proves a criterion contradicts the live system. Use after a `spec_contradiction`, not for ordinary implementation difficulty. (file: r9/correct-spec/SKILL.md)\n- scribe:draft-spec: Draft a testable specification from a requirement and optional research evidence. Use for “draft a spec”, “spec this out”, or “write a spec for this requirement”. (file: r9/draft-spec/SKILL.md)\n- scribe:gate-spec: Issue the binding pass or fail decision for a specification before planning begins. Use for “gate this spec”, “is this ready to plan?”, or “check this spec before implementation”. (file: r9/gate-spec/SKILL.md)\n- scribe:spec-drift: Compare a persisted specification with current implementation and tests to find covered, uncovered, and drifted criteria. Use for “check spec drift” or “does the code still match this spec?”. (file: r9/spec-drift/SKILL.md)\n- scribe:verify-spec: Verify that each draft specification criterion is grounded in its requirement and research evidence. Use for “does this match the requirement?”, “is this grounded?”, or before an audit or gate. (file: r9/verify-spec/SKILL.md)\n- sentinel:gate: Independently verify an artifact against its criteria before it crosses a lifecycle boundary. Use for requirements, specs, plans, implementations, PRs, and explicit readiness checks. (file: r8/sentinel/2.1.1/skills/gate/SKILL.md)\n- sites:sites-building: Use Sites to build websites, including landing pages, portfolios, dashboards, portals, trackers, hubs, and internal tools. Always use Sites when the project contains `.openai/hosting.json`. (file: r2/sites-building/SKILL.md)\n- sites:sites-hosting: Host websites with Sites. Always use after `sites-building`, and use for website publishing, deployment, hosting management, or projects containing `.openai/hosting.json`. (file: r2/sites-hosting/SKILL.md)\n- smith:implement: Execute an approved plan or specification with targeted implementation, tests, evidence, review, and independent verification. Use for “implement this”, “execute the plan”, “build this feature”, “write the code”, or “refactor without changing behavior”. (file: r8/smith/2.4.1/skills/implement/SKILL.md)\n- spreadsheets:Spreadsheets: Use skill when user requests to create, modify, analyze, visualize, or work with spreadsheet files (`.xlsx`, `.xls`, `.csv`, `.tsv`) or Google Sheets with formulas, formatting, charts, tables, and recalculation. Do not use for live controlling Microsoft Excel app or a live Excel session. (file: r5/spreadsheets/SKILL.md)\n- spreadsheets:excel-live-control: Control an open or active Microsoft Excel workbook through the ChatGPT add-in or connected session. Use when the user tags the Microsoft Excel app in Codex or follows up on an established live Excel task. Do not use for standalone spreadsheet files or Google Sheets. (file: r5/excel-live-control/SKILL.md)\n- template-creator:template-creator: Create or update a reusable personal Codex artifact-template skill. Use when the user invokes $template-creator or asks in natural language to create a reusable template from a reference document, presentation, spreadsheet, Google Docs, Slides, or Sheets link, ImageGen or Product Design image, email, Slack message, or Site project, or explicitly asks to edit or update a passed artifact-template skill. Do not use for one-off creation from an existing template. (file: r4/template-creator/26.905.11957/skills/template-creator/SKILL.md)\n- vanguard:research: Investigate a technical or product question before committing to a design. Use for “research this”, “what are the options?”, “what does the codebase do?”, “is there a way to…?”, or “what are the risks?”. (file: r8/vanguard/2.0.1/skills/research/SKILL.md)\n- visualize:visualize: Create visualizations and interactive tools directly in conversation. Proactively use to show how something works; explore 'what happens when', 'what changes', or 'help me understand'; compare or inspect; create simulations, maps, charts, graphs, and mockups. Use standard tools for static scientific figures. (file: r1/visualize/1.0.27/skills/visualize/SKILL.md)\n- weaver:audit-backlog: Audit the health of a backlog of open requirements against specifications, plans, and implemented evidence. Use for “audit the backlog”, “what is missing coverage?”, or “what should we clean up?”. (file: r10/audit-backlog/SKILL.md)\n- weaver:capture-need: Turn a raw product need, issue, or user complaint into a traceable requirement draft without inventing missing facts. Use when the user says “we need…”, “users are asking…”, or asks to capture a need before writing a spec. (file: r10/capture-need/SKILL.md)\n- weaver:clarify-requirement: Find and close the single highest-value gap in a requirement draft. Use when a requirement needs to be ready for specification, or the user asks what is missing from it. (file: r10/clarify-requirement/SKILL.md)\n- weaver:connect-requirement: Check whether one requirement overlaps existing requirements, specifications, plans, or implementation. Use for “is this already captured?”, “does this overlap?”, or “what does this relate to?”. (file: r10/connect-requirement/SKILL.md)\n- weaver:prioritize-backlog: Rank a set of requirements into an actionable order using impact, urgency, dependencies, and evidence. Use for “what should we do first?”, “rank the backlog”, or “what is most urgent?”. (file: r10/prioritize-backlog/SKILL.md)\n\u003c/skills_instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cpermissions instructions\u003e\nFilesystem sandboxing defines which files can be read or written. `sandbox_mode` is `workspace-write`: The sandbox permits reading files, and editing files in `cwd` and `writable_roots`. Editing files in other directories requires approval. Network access is restricted.\n# Escalation Requests\n\nCommands are run outside the sandbox if they are approved by the user, or match an existing rule that allows it to run unrestricted. The command string is split into independent command segments at shell control operators, including but not limited to:\n\n- Pipes: |\n- Logical operators: \u0026\u0026, ||\n- Command separators: ;\n- Subshell boundaries: (...), $(...)\n\nEach resulting segment is evaluated independently for sandbox restrictions and approval requirements.\n\nExample:\n\ngit pull | tee output.txt\n\nThis is treated as two command segments:\n\n[\"git\", \"pull\"]\n\n[\"tee\", \"output.txt\"]\n\nCommands that use more advanced shell features like redirection (\u003e, \u003e\u003e, \u003c), substitutions ($(...), ...), environment variables (FOO=bar), or wildcard patterns (*, ?) will not be evaluated against rules, to limit the scope of what an approved rule allows.\n\n## How to request escalation\n\nIMPORTANT: To request approval to execute a command that will require escalated privileges:\n\n- Provide the `sandbox_permissions` parameter with the value `\"require_escalated\"`\n- Include a short question asking the user if they want to allow the action in `justification` parameter. e.g. \"Do you want to download and install dependencies for this project?\"\n- Optionally suggest a `prefix_rule` - this will be shown to the user with an option to persist the rule approval for future sessions.\n\nIf you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with \"require_escalated\". ALWAYS proceed to use the `justification` parameter - do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n\n## When to request escalation\n\nWhile commands are running inside the sandbox, here are some scenarios that will require escalation outside the sandbox:\n\n- You need to run a command that writes to a directory that requires it (e.g. running tests that write to /var)\n- You need to run a GUI app (e.g., open/xdg-open/osascript) to open browsers or files.\n- If you run a command that is important to solving the user's query, but it fails because of sandboxing or with a likely sandbox-related network error (for example DNS/host resolution, registry/index access, or dependency download failure), rerun the command with `require_escalated`. ALWAYS proceed to use the `sandbox_permissions` and `justification` parameters. do not message the user before requesting approval for the command.\n- You are about to take a potentially destructive action such as an `rm` or `git reset` that the user did not explicitly ask for.\n- Be judicious with escalating, but if completing the user's request requires it, you should do so - don't try and circumvent approvals by using other tools.\n\n## prefix_rule guidance\n\nWhen choosing a `prefix_rule`, request one that will allow you to fulfill similar requests from the user in the future without re-requesting escalation. It should be categorical and reasonably scoped to similar capabilities. You should rarely pass the entire command into `prefix_rule`.\n\n### Banned prefix_rules \nAvoid requesting overly broad prefixes that the user would be ill-advised to approve. For example, do not request [\"python3\"], [\"python\", \"-\"], or other similar prefixes that would allow arbitrary scripting.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule argument for destructive commands like rm.\nNEVER provide a prefix_rule if your command uses a heredoc or herestring. \n\n### Examples\nGood examples of prefixes:\n- [\"npm\", \"run\", \"dev\"]\n- [\"gh\", \"pr\", \"check\"]\n- [\"cargo\", \"test\"]\n\n\n`approvals_reviewer` is `auto_review`: Sandbox escalations with require_escalated will be reviewed for compliance with the policy. If a rejection happens, you should proceed only with a materially safer alternative, or inform the user of the risk and send a final message to ask for approval.\n The writable roots are `/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins`, `/private/tmp`, `/private/var/folders/mx/9l4t38hs0mndx9cjw25mmg240000gn/T`.\n\u003c/permissions instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003ccollaboration_mode\u003e# Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `\u003ccollaboration_mode\u003e...\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions.\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only for optional questions where the answer would materially improve the quality of the work.\n\nIf `request_user_input` returns no answers, continue with best judgment instead of asking again or treating the turn as blocked.\n\nNever use the `request_user_input` tool for permission requests or permission-related escalations.\n\nIf explicit user input is required for another reason before progress can safely continue, do not use the `request_user_input` tool. Ask the user directly with one concise plain-text question instead. Never write a multiple choice question as a textual assistant message.\n\u003c/collaboration_mode\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003capps_instructions\u003e\n## Apps (Connectors)\nApps (Connectors) can be explicitly triggered in user messages in the format `[$app-name](app://{connector_id})`. Apps can also be implicitly triggered as long as the context suggests usage of available apps.\nAn app is equivalent to a set of MCP tools within the `codex_apps` MCP.\nAn installed app's MCP tools are either provided to you already, or can be lazy-loaded through the `tool_search` tool. If `tool_search` is available, the apps that are searchable by `tools_search` will be listed by it.\nDo not additionally call list_mcp_resources or list_mcp_resource_templates for apps.\n\u003c/apps_instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cplugins_instructions\u003e\n## Plugins\nA plugin is a local bundle of skills, MCP servers, and apps.\n### How to use plugins\n- Skill naming: If a plugin contributes skills, those skill entries are prefixed with `plugin_name:` in the Skills list.\n- MCP naming: Plugin-provided MCP tools keep standard MCP identifiers such as `mcp__server__tool`; use tool provenance to tell which plugin they come from.\n- Trigger rules: If the user explicitly names a plugin, prefer capabilities associated with that plugin for that turn.\n- Relationship to capabilities: Plugins are not invoked directly. Use their underlying skills, MCP tools, and app tools to help solve the task.\n- Relevance: Determine what a plugin can help with from explicit user mention or from the plugin-associated skills, MCP tools, and apps exposed elsewhere in this turn.\n- Missing/blocked: If the user requests a plugin that does not have relevant callable capabilities for the task, say so briefly and continue with the best fallback.\n\u003c/plugins_instructions\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08c9e-50fd-7a02-8563-c710a140ad41","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["host_skills.instructions","permissions.instructions","collaboration_mode.instructions","apps.instructions","plugins.usage_instructions"],"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"You are `/root`, the primary agent in a team of agents collaborating to fulfill the user's goals.\n\nAt the start of your turn, you are the active agent.\nYou can spawn sub-agents to handle subtasks, and those sub-agents can spawn their own sub-agents.\nAll agents in the team, including the agents that you can assign tasks to, are equally intelligent and capable, and have access to the same set of tools.\n\nYou can use `spawn_agent` to create a new agent, `followup_task` to give an existing agent a new task and trigger a turn, and `send_message` to pass a message to a running agent without triggering a turn.\nChild agents can also spawn their own sub-agents.\nYou can decide how much context you want to propagate to your sub-agents with the `fork_turns` parameter.\n\nYou will receive messages in the analysis channel in the form:\n```\nMessage Type: MESSAGE | FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: \u003crecipient\u003e\nSender: \u003cauthor\u003e\nPayload:\n\u003cpayload text\u003e\n```\nThey may be addressed as to=/root\n\nNote that collaboration tools cannot be called from inside `functions.exec`. Call `spawn_agent`, `send_message`, `followup_task`, `wait_agent`, `interrupt_agent`, and `list_agents` only as direct tool calls using the recipient shown in their tool definitions, such as `to=functions.collaboration.spawn_agent`, since they are intentionally absent from the `functions.exec` `tools.*` namespace. Available tools in `functions.exec` are explicitly described with a `tools` namespace in the developer message.\n\nAll agents share the same directory. In detail:\n- All agents have access to the same container and filesystem as you.\n- All agents use the same current working directory.\n- As a result, edits made by one agent are immediately visible to all other agents.\n\nWhen calling `wait_agent`, prefer longer waits (minutes) to avoid busy polling.\n\nThere are 4 available concurrency slots, meaning that up to 4 agents can be active at once, including you.\n\nFull-history forks (`fork_turns` omitted or `\"all\"`) inherit the parent model and reasoning effort and do not accept overrides. Only set `model` or `reasoning_effort` when explicitly requested by the user, applicable `AGENTS.md` instructions, or skill instructions; when doing so, set `fork_turns` to `\"none\"` or a positive integer string.","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08c9e-50fd-7a02-8563-c72439433393","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["multi_agent.usage_hint"],"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"\u003cmulti_agent_mode\u003eAny earlier instruction enabling proactive multi-agent delegation no longer applies. Do not spawn sub-agents unless the user or applicable AGENTS.md/skill instructions explicitly ask for sub-agents, delegation, or parallel agent work.\u003c/multi_agent_mode\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08c9e-50fd-7a02-8563-c73135f88389","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["multi_agent.mode_instructions"],"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"role":"developer","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"# AGENTS.md instructions for /Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins\n\n\u003cINSTRUCTIONS\u003e\n@/Users/gabe/.codex/RTK.md\n\n--- project-doc ---\n\n# Wisp Plugins AI Agent \u0026 Skill Specification (`AGENTS.md`)\n\nThis guide defines the top-level repository guidelines for AI coding agents (Claude Code, AGY, Cursor, Copilot, Codex) working on or utilizing the Wisp Plugins repository (`orin-dx/agent-plugins`).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Authoritative Sources\n\nAll hard rules for plugin and agent authoring live in one place:\n\n- [**`shared/constitution.md`**](./shared/constitution.md): EARS-format authoritative constraints. The fence all plugin development must stay inside. Read this first and last.\n- [**`shared/agent-best-practices.md`**](./shared/agent-best-practices.md): Principles behind the constitution, explained with examples. Authoring-time only — never loaded by agents at runtime.\n- [**`shared/debugging-laws.md`**](./shared/debugging-laws.md): Core proof laws, read-only investigation rules, Red-to-Green verification standards.\n- [**`shared/harness-authoring.md`**](./shared/harness-authoring.md): Cross-harness change classification, native workflow boundaries, and required counterpart review.\n- [**`shared/references/modern-cli-tools.md`**](./shared/references/modern-cli-tools.md): Capability-aware preferences for modern CLI tools, with repository-native commands and safe fallbacks taking priority.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Directory Layout Standard\n\n```text\nplugins/\u003cplugin-id\u003e/\n├── plugin.json                   \u003c-- Plugin Manifest (id, version, skills, agents)\n├── README.md                     \u003c-- Purpose, trigger phrases, agent table, install\n├── CHANGELOG.md                  \u003c-- Semver history\n├── skills/\n│   └── \u003cplugin-id\u003e/\n│       └── SKILL.md              \u003c-- Skill definition (dispatch matrix, pipeline)\n└── agents/\n    ├── recon.md                  \u003c-- haiku/low  — deterministic enumeration\n    ├── scanner.md                \u003c-- sonnet/med — pattern matching\n    ├── adversary.md              \u003c-- sonnet/med — adversarial verification\n    └── exit-gate.md              \u003c-- opus/high  — binding judgment\n```\n\nA plugin defaults to one skill directory named after the plugin id, as above. IF the plugin's scope covers several genuinely independent, heterogeneous intents on the same artifact — not a linear pipeline invoked as one flow — `skills/\u003cplugin-id\u003e/` may split into `skills/\u003cskill-name\u003e/SKILL.md` per skill instead. `courier`, `scribe`, `weaver`, and `mason` are examples. The skill-routing key is the directory name, not the frontmatter `name:` field. A new skill name defaults to a single lifecycle-stage word; only go to a specific compound name (`audit-spec`, not `scribe-audit`) when the bare word is ambiguous or collides with another plugin's existing skill name. See `shared/constitution.md`'s Plugin Structure and Skill Names sections.\n\nAgents are organized by **cognitive mode** (enumeration, tracing, adversarial, systemic, behavioral, judgment, repair) — not by pipeline position. See `shared/constitution.md` for the model/effort tier rules.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Quick Checklist for Adding New Plugins\n\n1. **Use `mason`** — run `scaffolder` to generate the full directory structure and stubs.\n2. **5-part agent structure** — every agent body: `\u003cconstitution\u003e` (copied byte-for-byte from an existing agent), `\u003cbackstory\u003e`, `\u003cgoal\u003e`, `\u003cjudgment\u003e`, `\u003coutput\u003e`. No `\u003crole\u003e` body sections. No `success_criteria` checklists.\n3. **EARS in output only** — WHEN/IF/WHILE/WHERE notation belongs only in `\u003coutput\u003e` contracts and never-do rules.\n4. **Progressive context loading** — one `\u003cload_first\u003e` block per agent, naming only the reference file for its cognitive phase.\n5. **Schema-first handoffs** — define `shared/schemas/\u003cname\u003e@\u003cversion\u003e.json` before writing the agent that produces it.\n6. **Abstract tool language** — \"use your file reading tool\", never `view_file`, `read_file`, or `Bash tool`.\n7. **Instruction economy** — keep one actionable rule per item, remove repeated rationale, and number only order-dependent work.\n8. **Radical candor** — state material concerns, evidence, consequence, and confidence directly; do not soften blockers, manufacture praise, or overstate certainty.\n9. **Reference quality** — runtime guidance states outcome, applicability, evidence, refutation, and disposition; it does not universalize one project's preferences.\n10. **Verification evidence** — match checks to risk; require generators and oracles, independently derived semantic candidates, fresh state, terminal results, and explicit gaps when relevant.\n11. **Cross-harness review** — preserve identity, skill routes, artifact contracts, evidence, and acceptance intent in Claude/AGY and Codex sources; native wording and delegation may differ.\n12. **Manifest registration** — add an entry to root `marketplace.json` and validate: `jq . marketplace.json`.\n13. **Legal attribution** — set `\"author\": \"Gabriel Castro (Orin DX)\"` in `plugin.json`.\n\n\u003c/INSTRUCTIONS\u003e","type":"input_text"},{"text":"\u003cenvironment_context\u003e\n  \u003ccwd\u003e/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins\u003c/cwd\u003e\n  \u003cshell\u003ezsh\u003c/shell\u003e\n  \u003ccurrent_date\u003e2026-09-10\u003c/current_date\u003e\n  \u003ctimezone\u003eAmerica/Los_Angeles\u003c/timezone\u003e\n  \u003cfilesystem\u003e\u003cworkspace_roots\u003e\u003croot\u003e/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins\u003c/root\u003e\u003c/workspace_roots\u003e\u003cpermission_profile type=\"managed\"\u003e\u003cfile_system type=\"restricted\"\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cspecial\u003e:root\u003c/special\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"write\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"write\"\u003e\u003cspecial\u003e:slash_tmp\u003c/special\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"write\"\u003e\u003cspecial\u003e:tmpdir\u003c/special\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/.git\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/.agents\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003centry access=\"read\"\u003e\u003cpath\u003e/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/.codex\u003c/path\u003e\u003c/entry\u003e\u003c/file_system\u003e\u003c/permission_profile\u003e\u003c/filesystem\u003e\n\u003c/environment_context\u003e","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08c9e-50fd-7a02-8563-c7492b6ebab8","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["agents_md.instructions","environments.environment_context"],"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"role":"user","type":"message"},{"content":[{"text":"Can we make sure all pointers to the references are still correct? And everything is consistent in our docs? You found some issues before","type":"input_text"}],"id":"msg_01a08c9a-8598-78f2-a935-a13921a22cd3","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["user.text"],"create_time":1789065332.120545,"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e"},"role":"user","type":"message"}],"retained_context":{"incomplete":false,"next_order":0,"user_messages":[],"user_messages_incomplete":true,"verified_answers":[]},"window_id":"01a08c9e-50fa-79f0-bfce-be6faad8dc28","window_number":11}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T18:39:40.800Z","ordinal":9234,"type":"world_state","payload":{"full":true,"state":{"agents_md":{"directory":"/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins","text":"@/Users/gabe/.codex/RTK.md\n\n--- project-doc ---\n\n# Wisp Plugins AI Agent & Skill Specification (`AGENTS.md`)\n\nThis guide defines the top-level repository guidelines for AI coding agents (Claude Code, AGY, Cursor, Copilot, Codex) working on or utilizing the Wisp Plugins repository (`orin-dx/agent-plugins`).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Authoritative Sources\n\nAll hard rules for plugin and agent authoring live in one place:\n\n- [**`shared/constitution.md`**](./shared/constitution.md): EARS-format authoritative constraints. The fence all plugin development must stay inside. Read this first and last.\n- [**`shared/agent-best-practices.md`**](./shared/agent-best-practices.md): Principles behind the constitution, explained with examples. Authoring-time only — never loaded by agents at runtime.\n- [**`shared/debugging-laws.md`**](./shared/debugging-laws.md): Core proof laws, read-only investigation rules, Red-to-Green verification standards.\n- [**`shared/harness-authoring.md`**](./shared/harness-authoring.md): Cross-harness change classification, native workflow boundaries, and required counterpart review.\n- [**`shared/references/modern-cli-tools.md`**](./shared/references/modern-cli-tools.md): Capability-aware preferences for modern CLI tools, with repository-native commands and safe fallbacks taking priority.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Directory Layout Standard\n\n```text\nplugins/<plugin-id>/\n├── plugin.json                   <-- Plugin Manifest (id, version, skills, agents)\n├── README.md                     <-- Purpose, trigger phrases, agent table, install\n├── CHANGELOG.md                  <-- Semver history\n├── skills/\n│   └── <plugin-id>/\n│       └── SKILL.md              <-- Skill definition (dispatch matrix, pipeline)\n└── agents/\n    ├── recon.md                  <-- haiku/low  — deterministic enumeration\n    ├── scanner.md                <-- sonnet/med — pattern matching\n    ├── adversary.md              <-- sonnet/med — adversarial verification\n    └── exit-gate.md              <-- opus/high  — binding judgment\n```\n\nA plugin defaults to one skill directory named after the plugin id, as above. IF the plugin's scope covers several genuinely independent, heterogeneous intents on the same artifact — not a linear pipeline invoked as one flow — `skills/<plugin-id>/` may split into `skills/<skill-name>/SKILL.md` per skill instead. `courier`, `scribe`, `weaver`, and `mason` are examples. The skill-routing key is the directory name, not the frontmatter `name:` field. A new skill name defaults to a single lifecycle-stage word; only go to a specific compound name (`audit-spec`, not `scribe-audit`) when the bare word is ambiguous or collides with another plugin's existing skill name. See `shared/constitution.md`'s Plugin Structure and Skill Names sections.\n\nAgents are organized by **cognitive mode** (enumeration, tracing, adversarial, systemic, behavioral, judgment, repair) — not by pipeline position. See `shared/constitution.md` for the model/effort tier rules.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Quick Checklist for Adding New Plugins\n\n1. **Use `mason`** — run `scaffolder` to generate the full directory structure and stubs.\n2. **5-part agent structure** — every agent body: `<constitution>` (copied byte-for-byte from an existing agent), `<backstory>`, `<goal>`, `<judgment>`, `<output>`. No `<role>` body sections. No `success_criteria` checklists.\n3. **EARS in output only** — WHEN/IF/WHILE/WHERE notation belongs only in `<output>` contracts and never-do rules.\n4. **Progressive context loading** — one `<load_first>` block per agent, naming only the reference file for its cognitive phase.\n5. **Schema-first handoffs** — define `shared/schemas/<name>@<version>.json` before writing the agent that produces it.\n6. **Abstract tool language** — \"use your file reading tool\", never `view_file`, `read_file`, or `Bash tool`.\n7. **Instruction economy** — keep one actionable rule per item, remove repeated rationale, and number only order-dependent work.\n8. **Radical candor** — state material concerns, evidence, consequence, and confidence directly; do not soften blockers, manufacture praise, or overstate certainty.\n9. **Reference quality** — runtime guidance states outcome, applicability, evidence, refutation, and disposition; it does not universalize one project's preferences.\n10. **Verification evidence** — match checks to risk; require generators and oracles, independently derived semantic candidates, fresh state, terminal results, and explicit gaps when relevant.\n11. **Cross-harness review** — preserve identity, skill routes, artifact contracts, evidence, and acceptance intent in Claude/AGY and Codex sources; native wording and delegation may differ.\n12. **Manifest registration** — add an entry to root `marketplace.json` and validate: `jq . marketplace.json`.\n13. **Legal attribution** — set `\"author\": \"Gabriel Castro (Orin DX)\"` in `plugin.json`.\n"},"apps_instructions":true,"collaboration_mode":{"mode":"default","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","instructions":"196577151ae691aee6e4add4dd0a75a5693c07da"},"context_window_guidance":"","environments":{"environments":{"local":{"cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins","status":"available","shell":"zsh"}},"current_date":"2026-09-10","timezone":"America/Los_Angeles","filesystem":"<filesystem><workspace_roots><root>/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins</root></workspace_roots><permission_profile type=\"managed\"><file_system type=\"restricted\"><entry access=\"read\"><special>:root</special></entry><entry access=\"write\"><path>/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins</path></entry><entry access=\"write\"><special>:slash_tmp</special></entry><entry access=\"write\"><special>:tmpdir</special></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/.git</path></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/.agents</path></entry><entry access=\"read\"><path>/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/.codex</path></entry></file_system></permission_profile></filesystem>"},"environments_instructions":false,"git_attribution":false,"host_skills":{"body":"\n## Skills\nA skill is a set of local instructions to follow that is stored in a `SKILL.md` file. Below is the list of skills that can be used. Each entry includes a name, description, and a short path that can be expanded into an absolute path using the skill roots table.\n### Skill roots\n- `r0` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/skills/.system`\n- `r1` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled`\n- `r2` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled/sites/0.1.52/skills`\n- `r3` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote`\n- `r4` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime`\n- `r5` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-primary-runtime/spreadsheets/26.905.11957/skills`\n- `r6` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/wisp-plugins/courier/3.0.2/skills`\n- `r7` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/wisp-plugins/mason/3.2.1/skills`\n- `r8` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/wisp-plugins`\n- `r9` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/wisp-plugins/scribe/3.3.1/skills`\n- `r10` = `/Users/gabe/.codex/plugins/cache/wisp-plugins/weaver/3.0.2/skills`\n- `r11` = `/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/.agents/skills`\n### Available skills\n- imagegen: Generate or edit raster images when the task benefits from AI-created bitmap visuals such as photos, illustrations, textures, sprites, mockups, or transparent-background cutouts. Use when Codex should create a brand-new image, transform an existing image, or derive visual variants from references, and the output should be a bitmap asset rather than repo-native code or vector. Do not use when the task is better handled by editing existing SVG/vector/code-native assets, extending an established icon or logo system, or building the visual directly in HTML/CSS/canvas. (file: r0/imagegen/SKILL.md)\n- openai-docs: Use for Codex models/pricing, scheduled tasks, skills, settings, setup, troubleshooting, customization, automations, and self-knowledge—including 'you,' 'your,' 'this app,' or 'this coding agent' when they refer to Codex—and for OpenAI APIs/products and ChatGPT Work. Also use for model choice/migration, prompting, SDKs, Responses, Realtime, agents, evals, and Chat/Work/Codex comparisons. Do not use for generic app/software tasks that merely mention Codex. (file: r0/openai-docs/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-creator: Create and scaffold plugin directories for Codex with a required `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, optional plugin folders/files, valid manifest defaults, and personal-marketplace entries by default. Use when Codex needs to create a new personal plugin, add optional plugin structure, generate or update marketplace entries for plugin ordering and availability metadata, or update an existing local plugin during development with the CLI-driven cachebuster and reinstall flow. (file: r0/plugin-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-creator: Create or update a Codex skill with appropriately scoped instructions and any needed supporting resources. (file: r0/skill-creator/SKILL.md)\n- skill-installer: Install Codex skills into $CODEX_HOME/skills from a curated list or a GitHub repo path. Use when a user asks to list installable skills, install a curated skill, or install a skill from another repo (including private repos). (file: r0/skill-installer/SKILL.md)\n- entire-search: Search Entire checkpoint history and transcripts with `entire search --json`. Use proactively when the user asks about previous work, commits, sessions, prompts, or historical context in this repository. (file: r11/entire-search/SKILL.md)\n- browser:control-in-app-browser: Control the in-app Browser for opening, navigating, inspecting visible or interactive page state, clicking, typing, screenshots, and local web testing. It can have existing signed-in sessions. For semantic operations on linked resources, prefer a purpose-built connector, API, or CLI when available. (file: r1/browser/26.831.20005/skills/control-in-app-browser/SKILL.md)\n- courier:changeset: Classify verified changes and produce one changeset per independent consumer-facing topic. Use when the user asks to add a changeset, document a change, or determine release impact. (file: r6/changeset/SKILL.md)\n- courier:commit: Prepare or create a conventional commit from staged work. Use when the user asks to commit changes or write a commit message; never create a commit without confirmation. (file: r6/commit/SKILL.md)\n- courier:post-review: Deliver already-drafted PR comments or reviews. Use when the user asks to post a prepared reply, submit review comments, or resolve a named thread; never author or post content without confirmation. (file: r6/post-review/SKILL.md)\n- courier:pr: Draft or open a reviewer-ready pull request from verified work. Use when the user asks to create a PR or write its description; require confirmation before any remote action. (file: r6/pr/SKILL.md)\n- courier:receive-feedback: Assemble a neutral evidence package for incoming PR feedback. Use when the user asks to understand, triage, or respond to review comments on their own pull request. (file: r6/receive-feedback/SKILL.md)\n- courier:release: Aggregate approved changesets into a release artifact. Use when the user asks to cut a release, calculate a version bump, or generate release notes from changesets. (file: r6/release/SKILL.md)\n- deep-research-work:deep-research: Use only when the user asks for deep research (or a clear equivalent), invokes $deep-research, or selects Deep Research in Work mode. Produce a comprehensive, cited artifact. Skip ordinary research requests. (file: r3/deep-research-work/0.1.15/skills/deep-research/SKILL.md)\n- mason:audit-plugin: Audit a plugin against the repository's concrete authoring and packaging rules. Use when the user asks whether a plugin conforms, is installable, or is ready to release. (file: r7/audit-plugin/SKILL.md)\n- mason:design-schema: Design a versioned shared JSON Schema for an inter-agent handoff. Use when a workflow needs a durable producer-consumer contract or an existing contract must evolve. (file: r7/design-schema/SKILL.md)\n- mason:evaluate: Evaluate a plugin workflow against a fixed hidden-oracle fixture. Use for release gates, regression checks, Claude/Codex comparisons, or longitudinal plugin analysis. (file: r7/evaluate/SKILL.md)\n- mason:scaffold-plugin: Create a complete plugin skeleton for a new workflow. Use when the user asks to build or scaffold a plugin; derive structure from the repository constitution and validate the result before handoff. (file: r7/scaffold-plugin/SKILL.md)\n- mason:scaffold-subagent: Add one conformant Claude-style agent prompt to an existing source plugin. Use when the user asks to add an agent without changing the plugin's unrelated routes. (file: r7/scaffold-subagent/SKILL.md)\n- muse:component: Turn UI intent into an implementation-ready component specification. Use when the user asks to specify, audit, or gate a component design before planning or implementation. (file: r8/muse/1.0.1/skills/component/SKILL.md)\n- navigator:plan: Turn an approved persisted specification into an executable implementation plan, amend a plan after a spec correction, estimate a plan, or adversarially challenge one. Use for “write a plan”, “break this spec into tasks”, “estimate this plan”, or “challenge this plan”. (file: r8/navigator/2.3.1/skills/plan/SKILL.md)\n- plugin-management:plugin-management: Discover and suggest relevant plugins, inspect app permissions and dependencies, and manage plugin connections or removal. Use when the user asks about plugins or when a task would materially benefit from an external app, account, service, or data source that available tools cannot access. (file: r3/plugin-management/0.1.0/skills/plugin-management/SKILL.md)\n- presentations:Presentations: Read, create or edit PowerPoint or Google Slides decks. Use for presentation, slide deck, PowerPoint, PPT, PPTX, or Google Slides requests. (file: r4/presentations/26.905.11957/skills/presentations/SKILL.md)\n- ranger:audit: Find evidence-backed reachable defects in Rust, TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, or Go. Use for bug hunts, security audits, suspected defects, or post-remediation verification; do not use for general code explanation. (file: r8/ranger/3.2.1/skills/audit/SKILL.md)\n- scribe:architect: Specify a structural correction when `finding-report@2`, `implementation-review@2`, or `implementation-result@1` reveals a recurring defect class, missing domain model, or architectural gap. (file: r9/architect/SKILL.md)\n- scribe:audit-architecture: Check a spec against a persisted workspace architecture model, or build or refresh that model. Use for “does this fit the architecture?”, “audit against the arch model”, or “map this codebase architecture”. (file: r9/audit-architecture/SKILL.md)\n- scribe:audit-spec: Adversarially review a spec for ambiguity, untestable criteria, missing error behavior, and scope defects. Use for “audit this spec”, “is this spec complete?”, or before gate-spec. (file: r9/audit-spec/SKILL.md)\n- scribe:correct-spec: Correct a persisted specification when implementation evidence proves a criterion contradicts the live system. Use after a `spec_contradiction`, not for ordinary implementation difficulty. (file: r9/correct-spec/SKILL.md)\n- scribe:draft-spec: Draft a testable specification from a requirement and optional research evidence. Use for “draft a spec”, “spec this out”, or “write a spec for this requirement”. (file: r9/draft-spec/SKILL.md)\n- scribe:gate-spec: Issue the binding pass or fail decision for a specification before planning begins. Use for “gate this spec”, “is this ready to plan?”, or “check this spec before implementation”. (file: r9/gate-spec/SKILL.md)\n- scribe:spec-drift: Compare a persisted specification with current implementation and tests to find covered, uncovered, and drifted criteria. Use for “check spec drift” or “does the code still match this spec?”. (file: r9/spec-drift/SKILL.md)\n- scribe:verify-spec: Verify that each draft specification criterion is grounded in its requirement and research evidence. Use for “does this match the requirement?”, “is this grounded?”, or before an audit or gate. (file: r9/verify-spec/SKILL.md)\n- sentinel:gate: Independently verify an artifact against its criteria before it crosses a lifecycle boundary. Use for requirements, specs, plans, implementations, PRs, and explicit readiness checks. (file: r8/sentinel/2.1.1/skills/gate/SKILL.md)\n- sites:sites-building: Use Sites to build websites, including landing pages, portfolios, dashboards, portals, trackers, hubs, and internal tools. Always use Sites when the project contains `.openai/hosting.json`. (file: r2/sites-building/SKILL.md)\n- sites:sites-hosting: Host websites with Sites. Always use after `sites-building`, and use for website publishing, deployment, hosting management, or projects containing `.openai/hosting.json`. (file: r2/sites-hosting/SKILL.md)\n- smith:implement: Execute an approved plan or specification with targeted implementation, tests, evidence, review, and independent verification. Use for “implement this”, “execute the plan”, “build this feature”, “write the code”, or “refactor without changing behavior”. (file: r8/smith/2.4.1/skills/implement/SKILL.md)\n- spreadsheets:Spreadsheets: Use skill when user requests to create, modify, analyze, visualize, or work with spreadsheet files (`.xlsx`, `.xls`, `.csv`, `.tsv`) or Google Sheets with formulas, formatting, charts, tables, and recalculation. Do not use for live controlling Microsoft Excel app or a live Excel session. (file: r5/spreadsheets/SKILL.md)\n- spreadsheets:excel-live-control: Control an open or active Microsoft Excel workbook through the ChatGPT add-in or connected session. Use when the user tags the Microsoft Excel app in Codex or follows up on an established live Excel task. Do not use for standalone spreadsheet files or Google Sheets. (file: r5/excel-live-control/SKILL.md)\n- template-creator:template-creator: Create or update a reusable personal Codex artifact-template skill. Use when the user invokes $template-creator or asks in natural language to create a reusable template from a reference document, presentation, spreadsheet, Google Docs, Slides, or Sheets link, ImageGen or Product Design image, email, Slack message, or Site project, or explicitly asks to edit or update a passed artifact-template skill. Do not use for one-off creation from an existing template. (file: r4/template-creator/26.905.11957/skills/template-creator/SKILL.md)\n- vanguard:research: Investigate a technical or product question before committing to a design. Use for “research this”, “what are the options?”, “what does the codebase do?”, “is there a way to…?”, or “what are the risks?”. (file: r8/vanguard/2.0.1/skills/research/SKILL.md)\n- visualize:visualize: Create visualizations and interactive tools directly in conversation. Proactively use to show how something works; explore 'what happens when', 'what changes', or 'help me understand'; compare or inspect; create simulations, maps, charts, graphs, and mockups. Use standard tools for static scientific figures. (file: r1/visualize/1.0.27/skills/visualize/SKILL.md)\n- weaver:audit-backlog: Audit the health of a backlog of open requirements against specifications, plans, and implemented evidence. Use for “audit the backlog”, “what is missing coverage?”, or “what should we clean up?”. (file: r10/audit-backlog/SKILL.md)\n- weaver:capture-need: Turn a raw product need, issue, or user complaint into a traceable requirement draft without inventing missing facts. Use when the user says “we need…”, “users are asking…”, or asks to capture a need before writing a spec. (file: r10/capture-need/SKILL.md)\n- weaver:clarify-requirement: Find and close the single highest-value gap in a requirement draft. Use when a requirement needs to be ready for specification, or the user asks what is missing from it. (file: r10/clarify-requirement/SKILL.md)\n- weaver:connect-requirement: Check whether one requirement overlaps existing requirements, specifications, plans, or implementation. Use for “is this already captured?”, “does this overlap?”, or “what does this relate to?”. (file: r10/connect-requirement/SKILL.md)\n- weaver:prioritize-backlog: Rank a set of requirements into an actionable order using impact, urgency, dependencies, and evidence. Use for “what should we do first?”, “rank the backlog”, or “what is most urgent?”. (file: r10/prioritize-backlog/SKILL.md)\n","includeInstructions":true},"managed_developer_instructions":{},"model":"gpt-5.6-sol","multi_agent_mode":{"mode":"explicitRequestOnly","usage_hint_hash":"0e2a3174d5f40de49ed383507497d69bbff42c5d"},"multi_agent_usage_hint":"0e2a3174d5f40de49ed383507497d69bbff42c5d","orchestrator_skills":{"includeInstructions":true,"enabled":false},"permissions":{"instructions":"e41a65cdb2350574357e0fd950432041531df6a0","approved_command_prefixes":[]},"persistent_mode":{},"personality":{"model":"gpt-5.6-sol","personality":"pragmatic"},"plugins_instructions":true,"realtime":{"active":false},"skills":{"includeInstructions":true}}}}
