6:Use your search tool to run each grep pattern from the loaded hazard reference against live files. For each match, use your file reading tool to read surrounding code (enough to populate excerpt and taxonomy fields). Do not scan dead_files.\nplugins/courier/README.md:56:| `changeset-analyzer` | Changeset Extractor | sonnet / medium | Classifies `consumer_impact` and `semver_impact` from a git diff per the decision table in `changesets.md`, then produces a `changeset@2` whose summary detail scales with that classification. |\nplugins/courier/README.md:105:- `shared/references/delivery/changesets.md` — changeset format, consumer/semver classification tables, voice\nplugins/courier/skills/release/SKILL.md:21:`shared/references/delivery/changesets.md` — the Semver Decision Guide (now machine-enforced via each changeset's `semver_impact`) and Release Notes Generation format.\nplugins/courier/skills/changeset/SKILL.md:4:  Trigger when the user asks to add a changeset or document a change: \"add a changeset\", \"document this change\", \"what changed here\". Extracts the consumer-facing meaning of a diff: classifies consumer_impact (behavior-change, new-capability, internal-only) and semver_impact (major, minor, patch, none) per shared/references/delivery/changesets.md's decision table, then writes a summary scaled to that classification. A diff spanning multiple independent topics — common on a backlog/catch-up run, not just a single PR — produces one changeset per topic, not one bundled entry. When the change came via smith, pass along implementer's criteria_evidence — changeset-analyzer uses those exact locations instead of reconstructing approximate ones from the diff.\nplugins/courier/skills/changeset/SKILL.md:21:`shared/references/delivery/changesets.md` — changeset format, the Consumer Impact Classification table, the Semver Decision Guide, and the voice standard for this artifact.\n"},"started_at_ms":1789065600980,"completed_at_ms":1789065600980}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T18:40:00.990Z","ordinal":9257,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a086d4-219f-74e1-8171-83f651770f25","turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-ce13c5f2-a533-4e55-87bd-d78a18579453","process_id":"83112","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","rtk python3 harnesses/codex/build.py --check"],"cwd":"file:///Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"rtk python3 harnesses/codex/build.py --check"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"failed","stdout":"/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/python3: can't open file '/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/harnesses/codex/build.py': [Errno 2] No such file or directory\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/python3: can't open file '/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/harnesses/codex/build.py': [Errno 2] No such file or directory\n","exit_code":2,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2208},"formatted_output":"/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/python3: can't open file '/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/harnesses/codex/build.py': [Errno 2] No such file or directory\n"},"started_at_ms":1789065600990,"completed_at_ms":1789065600990}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T18:40:01.030Z","ordinal":9258,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a08c9e-a006-7393-b4ed-87797fcd0ee4","call_id":"call_dDorhmUwNGDLvPUQ58BddM5U","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"exit_code\":0,\"output\":\"AGENTS.md:47:4. **Progressive context loading** — one `<load_first>` block per agent, naming only the reference file for its cognitive phase.\\nCONTRIBUTING.md:187:- [ ] `plugin.json` version bumped (see `shared/references/delivery/changesets.md`)\\nARCHITECTURE.md:244:**Progressive context loading** — each agent loads only the reference file for its cognitive concern.\\nARCHITECTURE.md:246:- A `<load_first>` block names one exact path. The first directory identifies the concern; a language filename narrows language-specific evidence.\\nshared/schemas/changeset@2.json:18:      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Whether this change is observable to someone outside this repo. See shared/references/changesets.md for classification rules.\\\"\\nshared/schemas/changeset@2.json:23:      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Version bump this change requires, per the Semver Decision Guide in shared/references/changesets.md. release-summarizer takes max(semver_impact) across a release's changesets rather than re-deriving it.\\\"\\nshared/constitution.md:72:WHEN an agent requires a shared reference file, it SHALL load only the file for its cognitive phase — not all reference files.\\nplugins/sentinel/skills/gate/SKILL.md:57:- [Verdict Schema](../../../shared/schemas/verdict@3.json)\\nplugins/mason/README.md:109:| `<load_first>` correctness | Present whenever an agent's goal implies a lookup it can't do from memory; its named reference file actually resolves |\\nplugins/courier/agents/release-summarizer.md:19:shared/references/delivery/changesets.md\\nplugins/courier/CHANGELOG.md:39:- `changeset-analyzer`'s topic-splitting judgment now checks `linked_spec`/`linked_plan`/`linked_requirement` first, then explicitly stated shared scope, falling back to diff-inferred shared cause only when neither applies — splitting on ambiguity alone is a rare fallback, not the default. Prompted by concern that the 2.1.0 split-by-topic change could over-fragment a deliberately-scoped effort (e.g. a themed feature batch or a testing push) into many tiny changesets. `shared/references/changesets.md` updated to match.\\nplugins/courier/CHANGELOG.md:55:- `docs-voice.md` reference — the repo's voice standard (banned words, sentence-length ceiling, inverted pyramid, Conventional Comments/Google review-label vocabulary), embedded into `conventional-commits.md`, `github.md`, and `changesets.md`.\\nplugins/courier/agents/changeset-analyzer.md:7:  Delegate to this subagent when you need changeset@2 artifacts produced from a git diff. Input is a git diff, optionally a linked spec@1, and optionally smith's criteria_evidence from the TDD run that produced this diff. A diff spanning multiple independent topics produces one changeset@2 per topic, never one bundled entry — see judgment for how topics are distinguished. For each topic, classifies consumer_impact (behavior-change, new-capability, internal-only) and semver_impact (major, minor, patch, none) per shared/references/delivery/changesets.md's decision table, identifies that topic's own changed files, detects breaking changes, and maps acceptance_criteria IDs. Uses smith's criteria_evidence directly when supplied; otherwise reconstructs best-effort file-level evidence from the diff. Summary detail scales with semver_impact — terse for patch/internal-only, full old-to-new-to-required-action detail for major. Output is one or more changeset@2 objects conforming to shared/schemas/changeset@2.json, routed to pr-narrator or release-summarizer.\\nplugins/courier/agents/changeset-analyzer.md:19:shared/references/delivery/changesets.md\\nplugins/courier/agents/changeset-analyzer.md:41:Beyond that: the changeset succeeds when each summary is written for a consumer assessing whether that specific change affects them, at the level of detail its semver_impact earns. Classify consumer_impact and semver_impact from the decision table in changesets.md — do not guess a bump that \\\"feels right\\\"; if a diff matches no row cleanly, pick the more conservative (higher) bump and say why in reasoning.\\nplugins/ranger/agents/adversary.md:20:- taxonomy is T7 or T10: load the matching `*-hazards-t7-t10.md`\\nplugins/ranger/agents/adversary.md:21:- any other taxonomy: load the matching `*-hazards.md`\\nplugins/ranger/CHANGELOG.md:54:- **Hazard reference split**: T7 and T10 — `boundary-tracer`'s entire scope — extracted into `rust-hazards-t7-t10.md`/`typescript-hazards-t7-t10.md`, both now under 120 lines (were 150/167, over the reference-file cap). `boundary-tracer` loads only the split file instead of the full ten-taxonomy set. `adversary` now branches on the candidate's `taxonomy` field to load only the matching file instead of the full set on every call — real savings on the agent invoked once per candidate, at the opus tier. `scanner` and `reviewer` (lambda) load both files where they need the complete set; behavior and taxonomy coverage are unchanged. Also removed a dead \\\"Workspace Discovery\\\" section neither file's consuming agent ever referenced — recon already provides the same information via its manifest.\\nplugins/ranger/agents/boundary-tracer.md:20:Load the matching `*-hazards-t7-t10.md` for the candidate language declared in\\nplugins/ranger/agents/scanner.md:34:Run every hazard taxonomy grep pattern from the loaded reference against every live file and emit a candidate@1 entry for each match, so the adversary has a complete set of candidates to work with.\\nplugins/ranger/agents/scanner.md:38:The scan is complete when every grep pattern from every applicable taxonomy has been run against every file in live_files, and every match has produced a candidate entry.\\nplugins/ranger/agents/scanner.md:46:Use your search tool to run each grep pattern from the loaded hazard reference against live files. For each match, use your file reading tool to read surrounding code (enough to populate excerpt and taxonomy fields). Do not scan dead_files.\\nplugins/courier/README.md:56:| `changeset-analyzer` | Changeset Extractor | sonnet / medium | Classifies `consumer_impact` and `semver_impact` from a git diff per the decision table in `changesets.md`, then produces a `changeset@2` whose summary detail scales with that classification. |\\nplugins/courier/README.md:105:- `shared/references/delivery/changesets.md` — changeset format, consumer/semver classification tables, voice\\nplugins/courier/skills/release/SKILL.md:21:`shared/references/delivery/changesets.md` — the Semver Decision Guide (now machine-enforced via each changeset's `semver_impact`) and Release Notes Generation format.\\nplugins/courier/skills/changeset/SKILL.md:4:  Trigger when the user asks to add a changeset or document a change: \\\"add a changeset\\\", \\\"document this change\\\", \\\"what changed here\\\". Extracts the consumer-facing meaning of a diff: classifies consumer_impact (behavior-change, new-capability, internal-only) and semver_impact (major, minor, patch, none) per shared/references/delivery/changesets.md's decision table, then writes a summary scaled to that classification. A diff spanning multiple independent topics — common on a backlog/catch-up run, not just a single PR — produces one changeset per topic, not one bundled entry. When the change came via smith, pass along implementer's criteria_evidence — changeset-analyzer uses those exact locations instead of reconstructing approximate ones from the diff.\\nplugins/courier/skills/changeset/SKILL.md:21:`shared/references/delivery/changesets.md` — changeset format, the Consumer Impact Classification table, the Semver Decision Guide, and the voice standard for this artifact.\\n\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"exit_code\":2,\"output\":\"/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/python3: can't open file '/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins/harnesses/codex/build.py': [Errno 2] No such file or directory\\n\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"exit_code\":0,\"output\":\"marketplace.json:67:      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Planning plugin. Decomposes specs into sequenced, bite-sized implementation plans with an implementation approach, exact code as a baseline, and the tests proving each criterion \\\\u2014 no write-test-first mandate. Tasks are sized to compilation boundaries, not a clock-time estimate. No TBDs. A developer with zero domain context can execute the plan mechanically.\\\"\\nshared/schemas/plan@1.json:5:  \\\"description\\\": \\\"A sequenced implementation plan. Every task is bite-sized, with an implementation approach, exact code, the tests proving each criterion, and a commit message.\\\",\\nshared/schemas/plan@1.json:46:            \\\"description\\\": \\\"Ordered steps. Each step is one action. Code steps include exact code. Test steps include exact command and expected output.\\\"\\nplugins/navigator/agents/planner.md:27:The plan succeeds if an implementer can work through every task in sequence, running exactly the specified commands, and arrive at a passing test suite that satisfies the spec's acceptance criteria — without needing to decide what to build or which criteria it must satisfy. The plan's exact code is a concrete baseline proving each task is achievable within its file targets and scope, not a shape the implementer is locked into — smith's implementer may adapt it, provided the same files, criteria, and tests are still satisfied.\\nplugins/navigator/agents/planner.md:30:- Any task says \\\"implement\\\" or \\\"add\\\" without specifying the exact code.\\nharnesses/codex/plugins/navigator/skills/plan/SKILL.md:41:- Do not invent exact code without inspecting the live APIs it touches.\\nplugins/navigator/skills/plan/SKILL.md:4:  Trigger this skill when the user says \\\"write a plan for X\\\", \\\"how do we implement this spec\\\", \\\"break this down into tasks\\\", \\\"what are the implementation steps\\\", \\\"create an implementation plan\\\", or \\\"plan this out\\\". Also activate when a spec (spec@1) exists and the user is ready to move to implementation but hasn't broken it down yet. Produces a plan@1 artifact where every task includes exact file paths, a brief implementation approach, exact implementation code, the exact tests proving each criterion, a conventional commit message, and covers_criteria linking the task to specific acceptance criteria. spec_file_path and linked_requirement are propagated from the source spec. No TBDs. No \\\"implement as appropriate.\\\" Every step is mechanically executable by a developer with zero domain context.\\nplugins/navigator/skills/plan/SKILL.md:11:Navigator decomposes a spec@1 into a sequenced, bite-sized plan@1 that a developer or agent can execute without making any design decisions. Every task is independently testable, time-bounded, and fully specified down to exact code and commit messages.\\nplugins/navigator/skills/plan/SKILL.md:87:Every step must show exact code. \\\"Implement the feature\\\", \\\"add appropriate error handling\\\", and \\\"write tests as needed\\\" are not steps — they are abdications. If the exact code is not known, it must be derived from the spec and documented as a concrete choice, not deferred as a TBD.\\nplugins/navigator/plugin.json:5:  \\\"description\\\": \\\"Planning plugin. Decomposes specs into sequenced, bite-sized implementation plans with an implementation approach, exact code as a baseline, and the tests proving each criterion — no write-test-first mandate. Tasks are sized to compilation boundaries, not a clock-time estimate. No TBDs. A developer with zero domain context can execute the plan mechanically.\\\",\\nplugins/navigator/CHANGELOG.md:100:- Spec decomposition into sequenced, TDD-ready implementation tasks with exact code and commit messages\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a08c9a-83b9-7301-a5a4-1fe2b35b276e","create_time":1789065601.030555}}}
