{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T19:15:16.371Z","ordinal":9350,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a086d4-219f-74e1-8171-83f651770f25","turn_id":"01a08cbd-ae2c-7ca0-9da8-fdd13eaa77fa","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-572ac6a7-579a-4a1b-b086-fd2fafd34f04","process_id":"88894","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","rtk bat --style=plain --number --line-range 230:265 ARCHITECTURE.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"rtk bat --style=plain --number --line-range 230:265 ARCHITECTURE.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":" 230 | :--- | :--- |\n 231 | **Constitution** | Ecosystem-wide invariants, byte-identical across every agent — the shared cache prefix. Never authored per-agent; copied verbatim. |\n 232 | **Backstory** | 2–4 sentences of experiential perspective. What has this agent been burned by? What does it value? Guides judgment in the open interior. |\n 233 | **Goal** | What the agent must produce and why. Intent, not steps. |\n 234 | **Judgment** | How to know if the goal was genuinely achieved vs. output that looks like it was. Names the key failure mode. |\n 235 | **Output** | Structured output shape, referencing a schema from `shared/schemas/` when the output flows to another agent. |\n 236 \n 237 Codex skills and role cards use native structures. Cross-harness parity applies to route names, artifacts, evidence, and acceptance intent—not prompt sections or delegation topology.\n 238 \n 239 **Cognitive mode dispatch** — agents are dispatched by the cognitive mode they require, not their pipeline position.\n 240 - A scanner (exhaustive pattern matching, no filtering) and an adversary (default-to-skepticism, requires a concrete failing scenario) cannot share a mental mode — combining them produces an agent worse at both\n 241 - Modes: enumeration (haiku/low), tracing/analysis/repair (sonnet/medium), adversarial judgment (opus/high)\n 242 - A plugin with distinct scanning, tracing, adversarial, and repair phases has a dedicated agent per mode\n 243 \n 244 **Progressive context loading** — each agent loads only the reference file for its cognitive concern.\n 245 \n 246 - A `<load_first>` block names one exact path. The first directory identifies the concern; a language filename narrows language-specific evidence.\n 247 - Never load all reference files into all agents — attention degrades when the context window contains material the agent won't use\n 248 \n 249 **Reference guidance** — runtime references improve judgment without replacing it.\n 250 - A heuristic is a candidate signal until live evidence confirms a reachable outcome\n 251 - Applicability, refutation, and disposition keep repository preferences from becoming universal defects or fixes\n 252 - Cross-language guidance covers each named target or records the unsupported target as a gap\n 253 \n 254 **Radical candor** — agents state material concerns, evidence, consequence, and confidence directly. Friendly language stays respectful without softening blockers, manufacturing praise, or overstating certainty.\n 255 \n 256 **EARS for edges only** — EARS notation (`WHEN`, `IF`, `WHILE`, `WHERE`) belongs in output contracts and never-do rules.\n 257 - Not in implementation steps or search strategies — those are the interior where agent judgment is the point\n 258 - Over-constraining the interior caps the agent at the level of the author's imagination\n 259 \n 260 **Schema-driven handoffs** — where one agent's output is another's input, both reference the same schema file from `shared/schemas/`. The schema is the contract; the prompt describes the intent.\n 261 \n 262 ---\n 263 \n 264 ## 8. Cross-Harness Distribution\n 265 \n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":" 230 | :--- | :--- |\n 231 | **Constitution** | Ecosystem-wide invariants, byte-identical across every agent — the shared cache prefix. Never authored per-agent; copied verbatim. |\n 232 | **Backstory** | 2–4 sentences of experiential perspective. What has this agent been burned by? What does it value? Guides judgment in the open interior. |\n 233 | **Goal** | What the agent must produce and why. Intent, not steps. |\n 234 | **Judgment** | How to know if the goal was genuinely achieved vs. output that looks like it was. Names the key failure mode. |\n 235 | **Output** | Structured output shape, referencing a schema from `shared/schemas/` when the output flows to another agent. |\n 236 \n 237 Codex skills and role cards use native structures. Cross-harness parity applies to route names, artifacts, evidence, and acceptance intent—not prompt sections or delegation topology.\n 238 \n 239 **Cognitive mode dispatch** — agents are dispatched by the cognitive mode they require, not their pipeline position.\n 240 - A scanner (exhaustive pattern matching, no filtering) and an adversary (default-to-skepticism, requires a concrete failing scenario) cannot share a mental mode — combining them produces an agent worse at both\n 241 - Modes: enumeration (haiku/low), tracing/analysis/repair (sonnet/medium), adversarial judgment (opus/high)\n 242 - A plugin with distinct scanning, tracing, adversarial, and repair phases has a dedicated agent per mode\n 243 \n 244 **Progressive context loading** — each agent loads only the reference file for its cognitive concern.\n 245 \n 246 - A `<load_first>` block names one exact path. The first directory identifies the concern; a language filename narrows language-specific evidence.\n 247 - Never load all reference files into all agents — attention degrades when the context window contains material the agent won't use\n 248 \n 249 **Reference guidance** — runtime references improve judgment without replacing it.\n 250 - A heuristic is a candidate signal until live evidence confirms a reachable outcome\n 251 - Applicability, refutation, and disposition keep repository preferences from becoming universal defects or fixes\n 252 - Cross-language guidance covers each named target or records the unsupported target as a gap\n 253 \n 254 **Radical candor** — agents state material concerns, evidence, consequence, and confidence directly. Friendly language stays respectful without softening blockers, manufacturing praise, or overstating certainty.\n 255 \n 256 **EARS for edges only** — EARS notation (`WHEN`, `IF`, `WHILE`, `WHERE`) belongs in output contracts and never-do rules.\n 257 - Not in implementation steps or search strategies — those are the interior where agent judgment is the point\n 258 - Over-constraining the interior caps the agent at the level of the author's imagination\n 259 \n 260 **Schema-driven handoffs** — where one agent's output is another's input, both reference the same schema file from `shared/schemas/`. The schema is the contract; the prompt describes the intent.\n 261 \n 262 ---\n 263 \n 264 ## 8. Cross-Harness Distribution\n 265 \n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":1167},"formatted_output":" 230 | :--- | :--- |\n 231 | **Constitution** | Ecosystem-wide invariants, byte-identical across every agent — the shared cache prefix. Never authored per-agent; copied verbatim. |\n 232 | **Backstory** | 2–4 sentences of experiential perspective. What has this agent been burned by? What does it value? Guides judgment in the open interior. |\n 233 | **Goal** | What the agent must produce and why. Intent, not steps. |\n 234 | **Judgment** | How to know if the goal was genuinely achieved vs. output that looks like it was. Names the key failure mode. |\n 235 | **Output** | Structured output shape, referencing a schema from `shared/schemas/` when the output flows to another agent. |\n 236 \n 237 Codex skills and role cards use native structures. Cross-harness parity applies to route names, artifacts, evidence, and acceptance intent—not prompt sections or delegation topology.\n 238 \n 239 **Cognitive mode dispatch** — agents are dispatched by the cognitive mode they require, not their pipeline position.\n 240 - A scanner (exhaustive pattern matching, no filtering) and an adversary (default-to-skepticism, requires a concrete failing scenario) cannot share a mental mode — combining them produces an agent worse at both\n 241 - Modes: enumeration (haiku/low), tracing/analysis/repair (sonnet/medium), adversarial judgment (opus/high)\n 242 - A plugin with distinct scanning, tracing, adversarial, and repair phases has a dedicated agent per mode\n 243 \n 244 **Progressive context loading** — each agent loads only the reference file for its cognitive concern.\n 245 \n 246 - A `<load_first>` block names one exact path. The first directory identifies the concern; a language filename narrows language-specific evidence.\n 247 - Never load all reference files into all agents — attention degrades when the context window contains material the agent won't use\n 248 \n 249 **Reference guidance** — runtime references improve judgment without replacing it.\n 250 - A heuristic is a candidate signal until live evidence confirms a reachable outcome\n 251 - Applicability, refutation, and disposition keep repository preferences from becoming universal defects or fixes\n 252 - Cross-language guidance covers each named target or records the unsupported target as a gap\n 253 \n 254 **Radical candor** — agents state material concerns, evidence, consequence, and confidence directly. Friendly language stays respectful without softening blockers, manufacturing praise, or overstating certainty.\n 255 \n 256 **EARS for edges only** — EARS notation (`WHEN`, `IF`, `WHILE`, `WHERE`) belongs in output contracts and never-do rules.\n 257 - Not in implementation steps or search strategies — those are the interior where agent judgment is the point\n 258 - Over-constraining the interior caps the agent at the level of the author's imagination\n 259 \n 260 **Schema-driven handoffs** — where one agent's output is another's input, both reference the same schema file from `shared/schemas/`. The schema is the contract; the prompt describes the intent.\n 261 \n 262 ---\n 263 \n 264 ## 8. Cross-Harness Distribution\n 265 \n"},"started_at_ms":1789067716371,"completed_at_ms":1789067716371}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T19:15:16.412Z","ordinal":9351,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a08cbe-e73c-7010-847d-583952c87439","call_id":"call_dpla27IPUA7uBF9rV9i35Q8t","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":" 230 | :--- | :--- |\n 231 | **Constitution** | Ecosystem-wide invariants, byte-identical across every agent — the shared cache prefix. Never authored per-agent; copied verbatim. |\n 232 | **Backstory** | 2–4 sentences of experiential perspective. What has this agent been burned by? What does it value? Guides judgment in the open interior. |\n 233 | **Goal** | What the agent must produce and why. Intent, not steps. |\n 234 | **Judgment** | How to know if the goal was genuinely achieved vs. output that looks like it was. Names the key failure mode. |\n 235 | **Output** | Structured output shape, referencing a schema from `shared/schemas/` when the output flows to another agent. |\n 236 \n 237 Codex skills and role cards use native structures. Cross-harness parity applies to route names, artifacts, evidence, and acceptance intent—not prompt sections or delegation topology.\n 238 \n 239 **Cognitive mode dispatch** — agents are dispatched by the cognitive mode they require, not their pipeline position.\n 240 - A scanner (exhaustive pattern matching, no filtering) and an adversary (default-to-skepticism, requires a concrete failing scenario) cannot share a mental mode — combining them produces an agent worse at both\n 241 - Modes: enumeration (haiku/low), tracing/analysis/repair (sonnet/medium), adversarial judgment (opus/high)\n 242 - A plugin with distinct scanning, tracing, adversarial, and repair phases has a dedicated agent per mode\n 243 \n 244 **Progressive context loading** — each agent loads only the reference file for its cognitive concern.\n 245 \n 246 - A `<load_first>` block names one exact path. The first directory identifies the concern; a language filename narrows language-specific evidence.\n 247 - Never load all reference files into all agents — attention degrades when the context window contains material the agent won't use\n 248 \n 249 **Reference guidance** — runtime references improve judgment without replacing it.\n 250 - A heuristic is a candidate signal until live evidence confirms a reachable outcome\n 251 - Applicability, refutation, and disposition keep repository preferences from becoming universal defects or fixes\n 252 - Cross-language guidance covers each named target or records the unsupported target as a gap\n 253 \n 254 **Radical candor** — agents state material concerns, evidence, consequence, and confidence directly. Friendly language stays respectful without softening blockers, manufacturing praise, or overstating certainty.\n 255 \n 256 **EARS for edges only** — EARS notation (`WHEN`, `IF`, `WHILE`, `WHERE`) belongs in output contracts and never-do rules.\n 257 - Not in implementation steps or search strategies — those are the interior where agent judgment is the point\n 258 - Over-constraining the interior caps the agent at the level of the author's imagination\n 259 \n 260 **Schema-driven handoffs** — where one agent's output is another's input, both reference the same schema file from `shared/schemas/`. The schema is the contract; the prompt describes the intent.\n 261 \n 262 ---\n 263 \n 264 ## 8. Cross-Harness Distribution\n 265 \n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"  85 - [ ] **Judgment** — how to tell if the goal was genuinely achieved vs. output that merely looks like it was. Name the key failure mode.\n  86 - [ ] **Output** — structured shape; reference a schema from `shared/schemas/` when output flows to another agent.\n  87 \n  88 No `<role>` section in the body — that's what `<backstory>` replaced. The frontmatter `role:` field is platform metadata (short display label for routing) and should be kept. No `success_criteria:` checklist. No filler: \"your job is to\", \"make sure to\", \"please ensure\".\n  89 \n  90 ### Instruction economy\n  91 \n  92 - [ ] Each paragraph or list item contains one independently actionable rule\n  93 - [ ] Sub-bullets express real branches, evidence, or attributes rather than split a long sentence cosmetically\n  94 - [ ] Numbered steps are reserved for operations whose order affects correctness\n  95 - [ ] Repeated rationale, process narration, filler, and synonymous restatement are removed\n  96 - [ ] Non-obvious context appears once, at the decision it changes\n  97 - [ ] Bodies over 300 words and list items over 40 words are reviewed, not automatically rejected\n  98 - [ ] Material concerns state evidence, consequence, and confidence directly; blockers are not softened and praise is not manufactured\n  99 \n 100 ### Progressive context loading\n 101 - [ ] Declares a `<load_first>` block naming the specific `shared/references/` file for this agent's phase\n 102 - [ ] Does not load reference files outside its cognitive mode (scanner loads hazards, not smells)\n 103 - [ ] Places references under the cognitive concern; uses language filenames only for language-specific evidence\n 104 - [ ] Keeps human indexes out of runtime load chains\n 105 - [ ] For Codex, every exact `shared/` path loaded directly or transitively appears in `harnesses/codex/catalog.json`; historical mentions omit the path when runtime access is unnecessary\n 106 \n 107 ### EARS placement\n 108 - [ ] EARS notation (`WHEN`, `IF`, `WHILE`, `WHERE`) used only in output contracts and never-do rules\n 109 - [ ] No EARS in implementation steps, search strategies, or reasoning guidance\n 110 \n 111 ### Model and effort\n 112 - [ ] `haiku` / `low` — deterministic enumeration only (manifest building, file inventory)\n 113 - [ ] `sonnet` / `medium` — analysis (scanning, drafting, planning, tracing)\n 114 - [ ] `opus` / `high` — judgment (adversarial reasoning, exit gates, final verdicts)\n 115 \n"},{"type":"input_text","text":" 275 | `finding-report@2` | ranger | scribe architect, humans |\n 276 | `field-survival-map@1` | boundary-tracer | adversary |\n 277 | `mutation-report@2` | smith mutator | reviewer, implementer |\n 278 | `evaluation-run@1` | mason evaluation runner | mason evaluation adjudicator |\n 279 | `harness-evaluation@2` | mason | release reviewers and longitudinal analysis |\n 280 | `arch-audit@1` | scribe | architecture-gate callers and humans |\n 281 | `arch-model@1` | scribe | scribe architecture checks and remediation |\n 282 | `release-artifact@2` | courier | humans |\n 283 \n 284 `verdict@1` remains Muse's component-spec gate contract. Other current gates use `verdict@3`; immutable schema versions are not rewritten in place.\n 285 \n 286 ---\n 287 \n 288 ## Shared References\n 289 \n 290 Runtime guides live in `shared/references/`. Paths start with the decision an agent is making; language is the filename only when the evidence is language-specific. Agents load exact files during execution, not this index at startup.\n 291 \n 292 | Concern | Supports |\n 293 | :--- | :--- |\n 294 | `hazards/` | Candidate defect signals and boundary tracing |\n 295 | `architecture/` | Semantic models, structural causes, and remediation |\n 296 | `verification/` | Evidence selection, project-native checks, review, and interface coverage |\n 297 | `delivery/` | Changesets, commits, pull requests, and review operations |\n 298 | `authoring/` | Reader-focused prose, comments, and diagrams |\n 299 | `evaluation/` | Hidden-oracle behavioral comparison |\n 300 | `workspace/` | Persisted artifact locations |\n 301 | `tooling/` | Repository tool preferences |\n 302 \n 303 See [`shared/references/README.md`](./shared/references/README.md) for the maintainer map.\n 304 \n 305 ---\n 306 \n 307 ## Repository Structure\n 308 \n 309 ```\n 310 agent-plugins/\n 311 ├── marketplace.json              ← Plugin registry\n 312 ├── .claude-plugin/               ← Claude marketplace metadata\n 313 ├── ARCHITECTURE.md               ← System architecture\n 314 ├── CONTRIBUTING.md               ← Plugin authoring guide\n 315 ├── harnesses/\n 316 │   └── codex/\n 317 │       ├── catalog.json          ← Marketplace order and runtime dependencies\n 318 │       └── plugins/<id>/         ← Authored native Codex plugins and skills\n 319 ├── shared/\n 320 │   ├── schemas/                  ← Versioned inter-agent JSON schemas\n 321 │   ├── references/               ← Runtime-pullable domain guides (split by concern)\n 322 │   └── agent-best-practices.md  ← Authoring-time principles\n 323 ├── plugins/\n 324     ├── weaver/                    ← Requirement capture\n 325     ├── vanguard/                  ← Research synthesis\n 326     ├── scribe/                    ← Specification drafting and gating\n 327     ├── muse/                      ← Component spec drafting and gating\n 328     ├── navigator/                 ← Implementation planning\n 329     ├── smith/                     ← Implementation and risk-matched verification\n 330     ├── sentinel/                  ← Verification gate\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"CONTRIBUTING.md:101:- [ ] Declares a `<load_first>` block naming the specific `shared/references/` file for this agent's phase\nCONTRIBUTING.md:180:- [ ] New references live under the reader's concern, have a real caller, and update `shared/references/README.md` when they introduce a concern\nCONTRIBUTING.md:187:- [ ] `plugin.json` version bumped (see `shared/references/delivery/changesets.md`)\nCONTRIBUTING.md:199:- **Pull, don't inject:** subagents load `shared/references/` on demand; the host does not preload them.\nCONTRIBUTING.md:204:- **Diagrams:** follow `shared/references/authoring/diagrams.md` when Mermaid materially clarifies a relationship.\nREADME.md:290:Runtime guides live in `shared/references/`. Paths start with the decision an agent is making; language is the filename only when the evidence is language-specific. Agents load exact files during execution, not this index at startup.\nREADME.md:303:See [`shared/references/README.md`](./shared/references/README.md) for the maintainer map.\nREADME.md:321:│   ├── references/               ← Runtime-pullable domain guides (split by concern)\nAGENTS.md:15:- [**`shared/references/tooling/cli.md`**](./shared/references/tooling/cli.md): Capability-aware preferences for modern CLI tools, with repository-native commands and safe fallbacks taking priority.\nAGENTS.md:47:4. **Progressive context loading** — one `<load_first>` block per agent, naming only the reference file for its cognitive phase.\nARCHITECTURE.md:246:- A `<load_first>` block names one exact path. The first directory identifies the concern; a language filename narrows language-specific evidence.\nARCHITECTURE.md:372:    └── shared/references/<concern>/*.md    # Materialized only when a native skill declares it\nshared/agent-best-practices.md:19:<load_first>\nshared/agent-best-practices.md:20:Read `shared/references/<concern>/<file>.md` before doing anything else.\nshared/agent-best-practices.md:69:<load_first>\nshared/agent-best-practices.md:70:Read `shared/references/hazards/rust.md` before scanning Rust code. Use its\nshared/agent-best-practices.md:269:- `shared/references/<concern>/*.md` — **runtime**. Agents load these on demand via `<load_first>`.\nplugins/mason/README.md:108:| 5-part body structure | Agent body has: constitution (byte-identical to the rest of the ecosystem), backstory, goal, judgment, output, in that order, with an optional `<load_first>` immediately after constitution — no success_criteria, no role sections, EARS only in constitution/output |\nplugins/mason/README.md:109:| `<load_first>` correctness | Present whenever an agent's goal implies a lookup it can't do from memory; its named reference file actually resolves |\nplugins/mason/README.md:159:- `shared/references/evaluation/behavioral.md` — hidden-oracle evaluation and provenance rules\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a08cbd-ae2c-7ca0-9da8-fdd13eaa77fa","create_time":1789067716.412528}}}
