{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T19:17:06.703Z","ordinal":9386,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0c3176283919dd68016aa3022f818487d0944aac74151a7815","status":"completed","call_id":"call_bM9A5wQQGI8UmLg1kxxE0lHI","name":"exec","input":"const rs = await Promise.all([\n  tools.exec_command({\n    cmd: \"rtk rg -n '2\\\\.1\\\\.0|sentinel' marketplace.json plugins/sentinel harnesses/codex/plugins/sentinel README.md CHANGELOG.md\",\n    workdir: \"/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins\",\n    yield_time_ms: 30000,\n    max_output_tokens: 10000\n  }),\n  tools.exec_command({\n    cmd: \"rtk bat --style=plain --number --line-range 1:35 plugins/sentinel/CHANGELOG.md plugins/sentinel/plugin.json plugins/sentinel/README.md harnesses/codex/plugins/sentinel/.codex-plugin/plugin.json\",\n    workdir: \"/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins\",\n    yield_time_ms: 30000,\n    max_output_tokens: 8000\n  }),\n  tools.exec_command({\n    cmd: \"rtk bat --style=plain --number harnesses/codex/plugins/sentinel/skills/gate/SKILL.md\",\n    workdir: \"/Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins\",\n    yield_time_ms: 30000,\n    max_output_tokens: 8000\n  })\n]);\nfor (const r of rs) text(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a08cbd-ae2c-7ca0-9da8-fdd13eaa77fa","create_time":1789067809.131652}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T19:17:06.740Z","ordinal":9387,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a086d4-219f-74e1-8171-83f651770f25","turn_id":"01a08cbd-ae2c-7ca0-9da8-fdd13eaa77fa","session_id":"01a086d4-219f-74e1-8171-83f651770f25","root_turn_id":"01a08cbd-ae2c-7ca0-9da8-fdd13eaa77fa","response_id":"resp_0c3176283919dd68016aa30220f5ec87d0aff956116002658c","usage":{"input_tokens":79058,"cached_input_tokens":73344,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":812,"reasoning_output_tokens":476,"total_tokens":79870},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":489954,"cached_input_tokens":443520,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":9255,"reasoning_output_tokens":4289,"total_tokens":499209},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":137792478,"cached_input_tokens":134883072,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":609800,"reasoning_output_tokens":204835,"total_tokens":138402278}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T19:17:06.828Z","ordinal":9388,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a086d4-219f-74e1-8171-83f651770f25","turn_id":"01a08cbd-ae2c-7ca0-9da8-fdd13eaa77fa","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-01fd5cce-8f4d-437e-b9ec-1717aceda8f7","process_id":"31465","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","rtk rg -n '2\\.1\\.0|sentinel' marketplace.json plugins/sentinel harnesses/codex/plugins/sentinel README.md CHANGELOG.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"rtk rg -n '2\\.1\\.0|sentinel' marketplace.json plugins/sentinel harnesses/codex/plugins/sentinel README.md CHANGELOG.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"CHANGELOG.md:29:- Smith 2.4.0 and Ranger 3.2.0 adopt the new contracts. Scribe 3.3.0 consumes their structural escalations. Sentinel 2.1.0 returns scoped verdicts.\nCHANGELOG.md:69:`smith` (2.1.0) and `navigator` (2.1.0): dropped the requirement that a task's test be written and confirmed failing before its implementation. Tasks now specify a brief implementation approach, the exact implementation, and the exact tests proving each criterion — test quality is verified by `smith`'s existing mutation-testing gate, not by write order. Rationale is a controlled comparison finding no quality advantage from write-test-first ordering for agent-written code, at several times the token cost, and that it suppressed upfront design work agents otherwise did well. See `docs/adr/008-drop-test-first-ordering.md`.\nCHANGELOG.md:83:| `axiom` | `sentinel` | 2.0.0 |\nCHANGELOG.md:88:Single-skill plugins also renamed their skill from the old plugin id to a literal action word: `proof/proof` → `ranger/audit`, `trace/trace` → `vanguard/research`, `vector/vector` → `navigator/plan`, `axiom/axiom` → `sentinel/gate`, `lambda/lambda` → `smith/implement`. Multi-skill plugins (`weaver`, `scribe`, `courier`, `mason`) kept their existing skill names — only the plugin-id prefix changed.\nCHANGELOG.md:122:- **`basis` (v2.1.0)**: `scaffolder` generates `<load_first>` and drafts SKILL.md routing entries; `auditor` gained `<load_first>`-correctness and orchestration-completeness checks, and now runs the repo's own validation scripts for what they already check deterministically instead of re-deriving it through reasoning.\nCHANGELOG.md:159:- **`canon` (v2.1.0)**: `auditor` gained a sixth check dimension, `unnecessary-prose`.\nCHANGELOG.md:175:- **`delta` (v2.1.0)**: `changeset-analyzer` now checks whether a diff contains multiple independent topics before classifying anything, and emits one `changeset@2` per topic instead of a single bundled entry. A single PR still produces exactly one changeset, unchanged — the new behavior only engages on a backlog/catch-up diff spanning several unrelated tracks since the last release. Prompted by a real incident in a sibling repo (`callisto`, a separate release-automation tool) where a large diff got bundled into one changeset covering unrelated tracks with package attribution guessed from a written summary instead of verified per-topic against the actual diff. `changeset-analyzer`'s output contract changes from a single object to an array of one-or-more; `delta/changeset`'s `<io>` section and `shared/references/changesets.md` updated to match, with the split rule stated as a named failure mode (bundling for convenience costs more than a spurious split).\nREADME.md:91:| **Verify** | [`sentinel`](./plugins/sentinel/) | Cross-cutting gate — confirms any artifact meets its criteria before the next stage begins | `verdict@3` |\nREADME.md:128:Add `muse` for component specifications, `sentinel` and `ranger` for verification, `courier` for shipping work, and `mason` for plugin authoring:\nREADME.md:132:/plugin install sentinel\nREADME.md:158:Other available selectors are `muse`, `sentinel`, `ranger`, `courier`, and `mason`. Inspect marketplace availability with:\nREADME.md:209:agy-plugin add sentinel@wisp-plugins\nREADME.md:266:| `spec@1` | scribe, muse | navigator, sentinel |\nREADME.md:272:| `changeset@2` | courier | courier release, sentinel |\nREADME.md:273:| `verdict@3` | scribe, smith, ranger, sentinel | gate consumers and humans |\nREADME.md:330:    ├── sentinel/                  ← Verification gate\nmarketplace.json:27:      \"id\": \"sentinel\",\nmarketplace.json:28:      \"name\": \"sentinel\",\nmarketplace.json:30:      \"path\": \"./plugins/sentinel\",\nplugins/sentinel/README.md:1:# sentinel — Verification Gate\nplugins/sentinel/README.md:27:/plugin install sentinel\nplugins/sentinel/README.md:32:**Codex** — see the [root Codex setup](../../README.md#codex), then run `codex plugin add sentinel@wisp-plugins`.\nplugins/sentinel/README.md:48:Sentinel is a single skill (`sentinel:sentinel` — the frontmatter `name: gate` is a cosmetic label, not the routing key; skills route by directory name) — not a set of per-artifact-type sub-skills. The same three-agent chain runs unchanged no matter what you hand it; `recon` is what determines the artifact type and derives its criteria, so nothing about the pipeline itself needs to vary by type.\nplugins/sentinel/agents/recon.md:7:  Delegate to this subagent at the start of the sentinel gate protocol when an artifact must be inventoried before verification begins. Input is an artifact path or description. The agent determines the artifact type, locates it in the workspace, enumerates the criteria it must be verified against (derived from a linked spec or requirement, or from the artifact's own acceptance criteria section if none is linked), and identifies source files to cross-check during verification. This is purely mechanical discovery — the agent produces no pass/fail opinion. Output is a JSON manifest with artifact_type, artifact_path, criteria array, source_files array, and a reasoning scratchpad. Do not delegate here for judgment; route the manifest to verifier for criterion cross-reference.\nplugins/sentinel/plugin.json:2:  \"id\": \"sentinel\",\nplugins/sentinel/plugin.json:3:  \"name\": \"sentinel\",\nplugins/sentinel/plugin.json:5:  \"description\": \"Cross-cutting verification gate. Confirms any stage artifact meets its criteria before the next stage begins. Implements the sentinel protocol: recon \\u2192 verify \\u2192 exit-gate with retry-with-feedback.\",\nplugins/sentinel/CHANGELOG.md:3:— sentinel\nplugins/sentinel/CHANGELOG.md:10:## [2.1.0] - 2026-09-09\nplugins/sentinel/CHANGELOG.md:19:- **BREAKING**: Plugin ID renamed from `axiom` to `sentinel` as part of the ecosystem-wide Wisp persona-naming rename — abstract math-noun plugin ids became hard to recall as the ecosystem grew past 9 plugins. This is the verification gate plugin; its skill also renamed from `axiom/axiom` to `sentinel/gate`. See `docs/adr/007-wisp-persona-naming.md` for the full rationale and old→new mapping. `plugin.json` `id`/`name` updated; every cross-plugin reference across the ecosystem updated to match.\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"CHANGELOG.md:29:- Smith 2.4.0 and Ranger 3.2.0 adopt the new contracts. Scribe 3.3.0 consumes their structural escalations. Sentinel 2.1.0 returns scoped verdicts.\nCHANGELOG.md:69:`smith` (2.1.0) and `navigator` (2.1.0): dropped the requirement that a task's test be written and confirmed failing before its implementation. Tasks now specify a brief implementation approach, the exact implementation, and the exact tests proving each criterion — test quality is verified by `smith`'s existing mutation-testing gate, not by write order. Rationale is a controlled comparison finding no quality advantage from write-test-first ordering for agent-written code, at several times the token cost, and that it suppressed upfront design work agents otherwise did well. See `docs/adr/008-drop-test-first-ordering.md`.\nCHANGELOG.md:83:| `axiom` | `sentinel` | 2.0.0 |\nCHANGELOG.md:88:Single-skill plugins also renamed their skill from the old plugin id to a literal action word: `proof/proof` → `ranger/audit`, `trace/trace` → `vanguard/research`, `vector/vector` → `navigator/plan`, `axiom/axiom` → `sentinel/gate`, `lambda/lambda` → `smith/implement`. Multi-skill plugins (`weaver`, `scribe`, `courier`, `mason`) kept their existing skill names — only the plugin-id prefix changed.\nCHANGELOG.md:122:- **`basis` (v2.1.0)**: `scaffolder` generates `<load_first>` and drafts SKILL.md routing entries; `auditor` gained `<load_first>`-correctness and orchestration-completeness checks, and now runs the repo's own validation scripts for what they already check deterministically instead of re-deriving it through reasoning.\nCHANGELOG.md:159:- **`canon` (v2.1.0)**: `auditor` gained a sixth check dimension, `unnecessary-prose`.\nCHANGELOG.md:175:- **`delta` (v2.1.0)**: `changeset-analyzer` now checks whether a diff contains multiple independent topics before classifying anything, and emits one `changeset@2` per topic instead of a single bundled entry. A single PR still produces exactly one changeset, unchanged — the new behavior only engages on a backlog/catch-up diff spanning several unrelated tracks since the last release. Prompted by a real incident in a sibling repo (`callisto`, a separate release-automation tool) where a large diff got bundled into one changeset covering unrelated tracks with package attribution guessed from a written summary instead of verified per-topic against the actual diff. `changeset-analyzer`'s output contract changes from a single object to an array of one-or-more; `delta/changeset`'s `<io>` section and `shared/references/changesets.md` updated to match, with the split rule stated as a named failure mode (bundling for convenience costs more than a spurious split).\nREADME.md:91:| **Verify** | [`sentinel`](./plugins/sentinel/) | Cross-cutting gate — confirms any artifact meets its criteria before the next stage begins | `verdict@3` |\nREADME.md:128:Add `muse` for component specifications, `sentinel` and `ranger` for verification, `courier` for shipping work, and `mason` for plugin authoring:\nREADME.md:132:/plugin install sentinel\nREADME.md:158:Other available selectors are `muse`, `sentinel`, `ranger`, `courier`, and `mason`. Inspect marketplace availability with:\nREADME.md:209:agy-plugin add sentinel@wisp-plugins\nREADME.md:266:| `spec@1` | scribe, muse | navigator, sentinel |\nREADME.md:272:| `changeset@2` | courier | courier release, sentinel |\nREADME.md:273:| `verdict@3` | scribe, smith, ranger, sentinel | gate consumers and humans |\nREADME.md:330:    ├── sentinel/                  ← Verification gate\nmarketplace.json:27:      \"id\": \"sentinel\",\nmarketplace.json:28:      \"name\": \"sentinel\",\nmarketplace.json:30:      \"path\": \"./plugins/sentinel\",\nplugins/sentinel/README.md:1:# sentinel — Verification Gate\nplugins/sentinel/README.md:27:/plugin install sentinel\nplugins/sentinel/README.md:32:**Codex** — see the [root Codex setup](../../README.md#codex), then run `codex plugin add sentinel@wisp-plugins`.\nplugins/sentinel/README.md:48:Sentinel is a single skill (`sentinel:sentinel` — the frontmatter `name: gate` is a cosmetic label, not the routing key; skills route by directory name) — not a set of per-artifact-type sub-skills. The same three-agent chain runs unchanged no matter what you hand it; `recon` is what determines the artifact type and derives its criteria, so nothing about the pipeline itself needs to vary by type.\nplugins/sentinel/agents/recon.md:7:  Delegate to this subagent at the start of the sentinel gate protocol when an artifact must be inventoried before verification begins. Input is an artifact path or description. The agent determines the artifact type, locates it in the workspace, enumerates the criteria it must be verified against (derived from a linked spec or requirement, or from the artifact's own acceptance criteria section if none is linked), and identifies source files to cross-check during verification. This is purely mechanical discovery — the agent produces no pass/fail opinion. Output is a JSON manifest with artifact_type, artifact_path, criteria array, source_files array, and a reasoning scratchpad. Do not delegate here for judgment; route the manifest to verifier for criterion cross-reference.\nplugins/sentinel/plugin.json:2:  \"id\": \"sentinel\",\nplugins/sentinel/plugin.json:3:  \"name\": \"sentinel\",\nplugins/sentinel/plugin.json:5:  \"description\": \"Cross-cutting verification gate. Confirms any stage artifact meets its criteria before the next stage begins. Implements the sentinel protocol: recon \\u2192 verify \\u2192 exit-gate with retry-with-feedback.\",\nplugins/sentinel/CHANGELOG.md:3:— sentinel\nplugins/sentinel/CHANGELOG.md:10:## [2.1.0] - 2026-09-09\nplugins/sentinel/CHANGELOG.md:19:- **BREAKING**: Plugin ID renamed from `axiom` to `sentinel` as part of the ecosystem-wide Wisp persona-naming rename — abstract math-noun plugin ids became hard to recall as the ecosystem grew past 9 plugins. This is the verification gate plugin; its skill also renamed from `axiom/axiom` to `sentinel/gate`. See `docs/adr/007-wisp-persona-naming.md` for the full rationale and old→new mapping. `plugin.json` `id`/`name` updated; every cross-plugin reference across the ecosystem updated to match.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2916},"formatted_output":"CHANGELOG.md:29:- Smith 2.4.0 and Ranger 3.2.0 adopt the new contracts. Scribe 3.3.0 consumes their structural escalations. Sentinel 2.1.0 returns scoped verdicts.\nCHANGELOG.md:69:`smith` (2.1.0) and `navigator` (2.1.0): dropped the requirement that a task's test be written and confirmed failing before its implementation. Tasks now specify a brief implementation approach, the exact implementation, and the exact tests proving each criterion — test quality is verified by `smith`'s existing mutation-testing gate, not by write order. Rationale is a controlled comparison finding no quality advantage from write-test-first ordering for agent-written code, at several times the token cost, and that it suppressed upfront design work agents otherwise did well. See `docs/adr/008-drop-test-first-ordering.md`.\nCHANGELOG.md:83:| `axiom` | `sentinel` | 2.0.0 |\nCHANGELOG.md:88:Single-skill plugins also renamed their skill from the old plugin id to a literal action word: `proof/proof` → `ranger/audit`, `trace/trace` → `vanguard/research`, `vector/vector` → `navigator/plan`, `axiom/axiom` → `sentinel/gate`, `lambda/lambda` → `smith/implement`. Multi-skill plugins (`weaver`, `scribe`, `courier`, `mason`) kept their existing skill names — only the plugin-id prefix changed.\nCHANGELOG.md:122:- **`basis` (v2.1.0)**: `scaffolder` generates `<load_first>` and drafts SKILL.md routing entries; `auditor` gained `<load_first>`-correctness and orchestration-completeness checks, and now runs the repo's own validation scripts for what they already check deterministically instead of re-deriving it through reasoning.\nCHANGELOG.md:159:- **`canon` (v2.1.0)**: `auditor` gained a sixth check dimension, `unnecessary-prose`.\nCHANGELOG.md:175:- **`delta` (v2.1.0)**: `changeset-analyzer` now checks whether a diff contains multiple independent topics before classifying anything, and emits one `changeset@2` per topic instead of a single bundled entry. A single PR still produces exactly one changeset, unchanged — the new behavior only engages on a backlog/catch-up diff spanning several unrelated tracks since the last release. Prompted by a real incident in a sibling repo (`callisto`, a separate release-automation tool) where a large diff got bundled into one changeset covering unrelated tracks with package attribution guessed from a written summary instead of verified per-topic against the actual diff. `changeset-analyzer`'s output contract changes from a single object to an array of one-or-more; `delta/changeset`'s `<io>` section and `shared/references/changesets.md` updated to match, with the split rule stated as a named failure mode (bundling for convenience costs more than a spurious split).\nREADME.md:91:| **Verify** | [`sentinel`](./plugins/sentinel/) | Cross-cutting gate — confirms any artifact meets its criteria before the next stage begins | `verdict@3` |\nREADME.md:128:Add `muse` for component specifications, `sentinel` and `ranger` for verification, `courier` for shipping work, and `mason` for plugin authoring:\nREADME.md:132:/plugin install sentinel\nREADME.md:158:Other available selectors are `muse`, `sentinel`, `ranger`, `courier`, and `mason`. Inspect marketplace availability with:\nREADME.md:209:agy-plugin add sentinel@wisp-plugins\nREADME.md:266:| `spec@1` | scribe, muse | navigator, sentinel |\nREADME.md:272:| `changeset@2` | courier | courier release, sentinel |\nREADME.md:273:| `verdict@3` | scribe, smith, ranger, sentinel | gate consumers and humans |\nREADME.md:330:    ├── sentinel/                  ← Verification gate\nmarketplace.json:27:      \"id\": \"sentinel\",\nmarketplace.json:28:      \"name\": \"sentinel\",\nmarketplace.json:30:      \"path\": \"./plugins/sentinel\",\nplugins/sentinel/README.md:1:# sentinel — Verification Gate\nplugins/sentinel/README.md:27:/plugin install sentinel\nplugins/sentinel/README.md:32:**Codex** — see the [root Codex setup](../../README.md#codex), then run `codex plugin add sentinel@wisp-plugins`.\nplugins/sentinel/README.md:48:Sentinel is a single skill (`sentinel:sentinel` — the frontmatter `name: gate` is a cosmetic label, not the routing key; skills route by directory name) — not a set of per-artifact-type sub-skills. The same three-agent chain runs unchanged no matter what you hand it; `recon` is what determines the artifact type and derives its criteria, so nothing about the pipeline itself needs to vary by type.\nplugins/sentinel/agents/recon.md:7:  Delegate to this subagent at the start of the sentinel gate protocol when an artifact must be inventoried before verification begins. Input is an artifact path or description. The agent determines the artifact type, locates it in the workspace, enumerates the criteria it must be verified against (derived from a linked spec or requirement, or from the artifact's own acceptance criteria section if none is linked), and identifies source files to cross-check during verification. This is purely mechanical discovery — the agent produces no pass/fail opinion. Output is a JSON manifest with artifact_type, artifact_path, criteria array, source_files array, and a reasoning scratchpad. Do not delegate here for judgment; route the manifest to verifier for criterion cross-reference.\nplugins/sentinel/plugin.json:2:  \"id\": \"sentinel\",\nplugins/sentinel/plugin.json:3:  \"name\": \"sentinel\",\nplugins/sentinel/plugin.json:5:  \"description\": \"Cross-cutting verification gate. Confirms any stage artifact meets its criteria before the next stage begins. Implements the sentinel protocol: recon \\u2192 verify \\u2192 exit-gate with retry-with-feedback.\",\nplugins/sentinel/CHANGELOG.md:3:— sentinel\nplugins/sentinel/CHANGELOG.md:10:## [2.1.0] - 2026-09-09\nplugins/sentinel/CHANGELOG.md:19:- **BREAKING**: Plugin ID renamed from `axiom` to `sentinel` as part of the ecosystem-wide Wisp persona-naming rename — abstract math-noun plugin ids became hard to recall as the ecosystem grew past 9 plugins. This is the verification gate plugin; its skill also renamed from `axiom/axiom` to `sentinel/gate`. See `docs/adr/007-wisp-persona-naming.md` for the full rationale and old→new mapping. `plugin.json` `id`/`name` updated; every cross-plugin reference across the ecosystem updated to match.\n"},"started_at_ms":1789067826828,"completed_at_ms":1789067826828}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T19:17:06.831Z","ordinal":9389,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a086d4-219f-74e1-8171-83f651770f25","turn_id":"01a08cbd-ae2c-7ca0-9da8-fdd13eaa77fa","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-b4e136d8-ce3a-4afc-a6bc-e0ea3d4d9c75","process_id":"64662","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","rtk bat --style=plain --number harnesses/codex/plugins/sentinel/skills/gate/SKILL.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"rtk bat --style=plain --number harnesses/codex/plugins/sentinel/skills/gate/SKILL.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"   1 ---\n   2 name: gate\n   3 description: Independently verify an artifact against its criteria before it crosses a lifecycle boundary. Use for requirements, specs, plans, implementations, PRs, and explicit readiness checks.\n   4 ---\n   5 \n   6 State material concerns, evidence, consequence, and confidence directly. Do not soften blockers, manufacture praise, or claim certainty beyond the evidence.\n   7 \n   8 # Gate a lifecycle artifact with evidence\n   9 \n  10 Sentinel judges an existing artifact; it does not author a replacement. Begin by identifying what the artifact claims, where it is persisted, and which criteria actually govern it.\n  11 \n  12 ## Workflow\n  13 \n  14 1. Identify `artifact_type`, `artifact_path`, the authoritative criteria, linked artifacts, and source files that can prove or disprove each criterion.\n  15 2. Read persisted files when a path is present rather than relying on conversation summaries.\n  16 3. Build an evidence table that classifies each criterion as verified, failed, or unverifiable, with exact paths and test or inspection evidence.\n  17 4. Produce `verdict@3` conforming to `shared/schemas/verdict@3.json`. Name the verified scope, blockers, coverage gaps, and pending checks.\n  18 5. Pass only when every applicable criterion is verified. On failure, return specific blockers that the producing workflow can act on.\n  19 \n  20 ## Retry protocol\n  21 \n  22 On a retry, inspect only the revised artifact and the previous blockers. Do not reopen unrelated review scope. Set `retry_count` accurately and escalate to the caller after the third failed retry rather than looping indefinitely.\n  23 \n  24 ## Evidence and safety\n  25 \n  26 - Do not treat a passing test alone as proof of a criterion it does not exercise.\n  27 - Mark evidence unverifiable when the relevant source, environment, or criterion is absent; do not convert uncertainty into a pass.\n  28 - Treat a required check that has not finished as pending and fail the gate.\n  29 - Do not edit the artifact, create a PR, or publish a verdict externally unless the user asks for a separate action.\n  30 \n  31 ## Team use\n  32 \n  33 Before delegating, read `agent-roles/README.md`; use `recon` for fixed evidence collection, but reserve the binding decision for the primary `judge`.\n  34 \n  35 When teams are available, split criterion verification only across independent subsystems after the artifact and criteria are fixed. The owner reconciles coverage and delivers one binding verdict. Without teams, verify each criterion sequentially with the same standard.\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"   1 ---\n   2 name: gate\n   3 description: Independently verify an artifact against its criteria before it crosses a lifecycle boundary. Use for requirements, specs, plans, implementations, PRs, and explicit readiness checks.\n   4 ---\n   5 \n   6 State material concerns, evidence, consequence, and confidence directly. Do not soften blockers, manufacture praise, or claim certainty beyond the evidence.\n   7 \n   8 # Gate a lifecycle artifact with evidence\n   9 \n  10 Sentinel judges an existing artifact; it does not author a replacement. Begin by identifying what the artifact claims, where it is persisted, and which criteria actually govern it.\n  11 \n  12 ## Workflow\n  13 \n  14 1. Identify `artifact_type`, `artifact_path`, the authoritative criteria, linked artifacts, and source files that can prove or disprove each criterion.\n  15 2. Read persisted files when a path is present rather than relying on conversation summaries.\n  16 3. Build an evidence table that classifies each criterion as verified, failed, or unverifiable, with exact paths and test or inspection evidence.\n  17 4. Produce `verdict@3` conforming to `shared/schemas/verdict@3.json`. Name the verified scope, blockers, coverage gaps, and pending checks.\n  18 5. Pass only when every applicable criterion is verified. On failure, return specific blockers that the producing workflow can act on.\n  19 \n  20 ## Retry protocol\n  21 \n  22 On a retry, inspect only the revised artifact and the previous blockers. Do not reopen unrelated review scope. Set `retry_count` accurately and escalate to the caller after the third failed retry rather than looping indefinitely.\n  23 \n  24 ## Evidence and safety\n  25 \n  26 - Do not treat a passing test alone as proof of a criterion it does not exercise.\n  27 - Mark evidence unverifiable when the relevant source, environment, or criterion is absent; do not convert uncertainty into a pass.\n  28 - Treat a required check that has not finished as pending and fail the gate.\n  29 - Do not edit the artifact, create a PR, or publish a verdict externally unless the user asks for a separate action.\n  30 \n  31 ## Team use\n  32 \n  33 Before delegating, read `agent-roles/README.md`; use `recon` for fixed evidence collection, but reserve the binding decision for the primary `judge`.\n  34 \n  35 When teams are available, split criterion verification only across independent subsystems after the artifact and criteria are fixed. The owner reconciles coverage and delivers one binding verdict. Without teams, verify each criterion sequentially with the same standard.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":958},"formatted_output":"   1 ---\n   2 name: gate\n   3 description: Independently verify an artifact against its criteria before it crosses a lifecycle boundary. Use for requirements, specs, plans, implementations, PRs, and explicit readiness checks.\n   4 ---\n   5 \n   6 State material concerns, evidence, consequence, and confidence directly. Do not soften blockers, manufacture praise, or claim certainty beyond the evidence.\n   7 \n   8 # Gate a lifecycle artifact with evidence\n   9 \n  10 Sentinel judges an existing artifact; it does not author a replacement. Begin by identifying what the artifact claims, where it is persisted, and which criteria actually govern it.\n  11 \n  12 ## Workflow\n  13 \n  14 1. Identify `artifact_type`, `artifact_path`, the authoritative criteria, linked artifacts, and source files that can prove or disprove each criterion.\n  15 2. Read persisted files when a path is present rather than relying on conversation summaries.\n  16 3. Build an evidence table that classifies each criterion as verified, failed, or unverifiable, with exact paths and test or inspection evidence.\n  17 4. Produce `verdict@3` conforming to `shared/schemas/verdict@3.json`. Name the verified scope, blockers, coverage gaps, and pending checks.\n  18 5. Pass only when every applicable criterion is verified. On failure, return specific blockers that the producing workflow can act on.\n  19 \n  20 ## Retry protocol\n  21 \n  22 On a retry, inspect only the revised artifact and the previous blockers. Do not reopen unrelated review scope. Set `retry_count` accurately and escalate to the caller after the third failed retry rather than looping indefinitely.\n  23 \n  24 ## Evidence and safety\n  25 \n  26 - Do not treat a passing test alone as proof of a criterion it does not exercise.\n  27 - Mark evidence unverifiable when the relevant source, environment, or criterion is absent; do not convert uncertainty into a pass.\n  28 - Treat a required check that has not finished as pending and fail the gate.\n  29 - Do not edit the artifact, create a PR, or publish a verdict externally unless the user asks for a separate action.\n  30 \n  31 ## Team use\n  32 \n  33 Before delegating, read `agent-roles/README.md`; use `recon` for fixed evidence collection, but reserve the binding decision for the primary `judge`.\n  34 \n  35 When teams are available, split criterion verification only across independent subsystems after the artifact and criteria are fixed. The owner reconciles coverage and delivers one binding verdict. Without teams, verify each criterion sequentially with the same standard.\n"},"started_at_ms":1789067826831,"completed_at_ms":1789067826831}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T19:17:06.838Z","ordinal":9390,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a086d4-219f-74e1-8171-83f651770f25","turn_id":"01a08cbd-ae2c-7ca0-9da8-fdd13eaa77fa","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-21ceb4c1-2cbf-42c9-8b96-64b1e877781f","process_id":"30824","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","rtk bat --style=plain --number --line-range 1:35 plugins/sentinel/CHANGELOG.md plugins/sentinel/plugin.json plugins/sentinel/README.md harnesses/codex/plugins/sentinel/.codex-plugin/plugin.json"],"cwd":"file:///Users/gabe/Projects/agent-plugins","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"rtk bat --style=plain --number --line-range 1:35 plugins/sentinel/CHANGELOG.md plugins/sentinel/plugin.json plugins/sentinel/README.md harnesses/codex/plugins/sentinel/.codex-plugin/plugin.json"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"   1 # Changelog\n   2 \n   3 — sentinel\n   4 \n   5 ## [2.1.1] - 2026-09-09\n   6 \n   7 ### Changed\n   8 - Agents now report blockers and material concerns directly with evidence, consequence, and calibrated confidence.\n   9 \n  10 ## [2.1.0] - 2026-09-09\n  11 \n  12 ### Changed\n  13 - Exit-gate returns `verdict@3`, separating blockers, coverage gaps, and unfinished checks.\n  14 - Mutable external evidence is refreshed near the verdict; started checks must reach a terminal result before pass.\n  15 \n  16 ## [2.0.0] - 2026-08-27\n  17 \n  18 ### Changed\n  19 - **BREAKING**: Plugin ID renamed from `axiom` to `sentinel` as part of the ecosystem-wide Wisp persona-naming rename — abstract math-noun plugin ids became hard to recall as the ecosystem grew past 9 plugins. This is the verification gate plugin; its skill also renamed from `axiom/axiom` to `sentinel/gate`. See `docs/adr/007-wisp-persona-naming.md` for the full rationale and old→new mapping. `plugin.json` `id`/`name` updated; every cross-plugin reference across the ecosystem updated to match.\n  20 \n  21 ## [1.2.3] - 2026-08-23\n  22 \n  23 ### Fixed\n  24 - `skills/axiom/SKILL.md` linked `shared/agent-best-practices.md` — an authoring-time-only guide with no legitimate runtime purpose for a verification gate, unlike `basis`'s scaffolding skills where it's the actual job. Copy-paste leftover from scaffolding. Found by an adversarial audit of the one plugin untouched all session.\n  25 \n  26 ## [1.2.2] - 2026-08-22\n  27 \n  28 ### Changed\n  29 - `recon`'s `reasoning` scratchpad capped to 1-2 sentences — discarded, not read by a human, mechanical enumeration doesn't need more.\n  30 - All agents now carry a `<constitution>` section — see root CHANGELOG and ADR-006. No routing or output behavior changed.\n  31 \n  32 ## [1.2.1] - 2026-08-21\n  33 \n  34 ### Fixed\n  35 - `SKILL.md` and `README.md` each documented a \"sub-skills\" table, and the two disagreed with each other — `SKILL.md` listed six (`verify-requirement`, `verify-spec`, `verify-plan`, `verify-implementation`, `verify-pr`, `exit-gate`), `README.md` listed two (`axiom/gate`, `axiom/verify`). Both were fiction: axiom has exactly one skill directory and one three-agent pipeline (`recon` → `verifier` → `exit-gate`), reused unchanged against whatever artifact type it's handed. Replaced both with one consistent description.\n   1 {\n   2   \"id\": \"sentinel\",\n   3   \"name\": \"sentinel\",\n   4   \"version\": \"2.1.1\",\n   5   \"description\": \"Cross-cutting verification gate. Confirms any stage artifact meets its criteria before the next stage begins. Implements the sentinel protocol: recon \\u2192 verify \\u2192 exit-gate with retry-with-feedback.\",\n   6   \"author\": {\n   7     \"name\": \"Gabriel Castro\",\n   8     \"email\": \"gabe@techworx.dev\"\n   9   },\n  10   \"skills\": \"./skills/\",\n  11   \"agents\": [\n  12     \"./agents/exit-gate.md\",\n  13     \"./agents/recon.md\",\n  14     \"./agents/verifier.md\"\n  15   ],\n  16   \"consumes\": [],\n  17   \"produces\": [\n  18     \"verdict@3\"\n  19   ]\n  20 }\n   1 # sentinel — Verification Gate\n   2 \n   3 **Stage:** Gate · **Output:** `verdict@3` · **Version:** 2.1.1\n   4 \n   5 One skill, one artifact-agnostic pipeline. Sentinel returns a scoped `verdict@3`: pass or fail, with blockers, coverage gaps, and pending checks. Unverifiable required criteria fail unless explicitly waived.\n   6 \n   7 Sentinel is standalone. Its `plugin.json` declares `consumes: []`, and nothing invokes it automatically. Scribe and Smith have dedicated exit gates for their own artifacts; neither calls Sentinel's agents. Install Sentinel for an on-demand independent gate against any artifact.\n   8 \n   9 ---\n  10 \n  11 ## When to Use\n  12 \n  13 - You want to verify a spec, plan, or changeset against a defined set of criteria\n  14 - You want an independent second opinion on whether an implementation meets its spec\n  15 - You need a formal pass/fail verdict with specific blockers before shipping\n  16 - You want to check a finding report for completeness before handing it off\n  17 \n  18 **Invoke with:** `\"Gate this spec\"`, `\"Verify this implementation against the spec\"`, `\"Run the verification gate\"`, `\"Check whether this changeset meets all acceptance criteria\"`, `\"Is this finding report complete?\"`\n  19 \n  20 ---\n  21 \n  22 ## Install\n  23 \n  24 **Claude Code** — add the marketplace once, then install by ID:\n  25 ```\n  26 /plugin marketplace add orin-dx/agent-plugins\n  27 /plugin install sentinel\n  28 ```\n  29 \n  30 **AGY** — installs the full repo; see the [root README](../../README.md#install) for instructions.\n  31 \n  32 **Codex** — see the [root Codex setup](../../README.md#codex), then run `codex plugin add sentinel@wisp-plugins`.\n  33 \n  34 ---\n  35 \n   1 {\n   2   \"name\": \"sentinel\",\n   3   \"version\": \"2.1.1\",\n   4   \"description\": \"Independently gate lifecycle artifacts against explicit criteria before they cross a workflow boundary.\",\n   5   \"author\": {\n   6     \"name\": \"Gabriel Castro\",\n   7     \"email\": \"gabe@techworx.dev\"\n   8   },\n   9   \"homepage\": \"https://github.com/orin-dx/agent-plugins\",\n  10   \"repository\": \"https://github.com/orin-dx/agent-plugins\",\n  11   \"license\": \"MIT\",\n  12   \"keywords\": [\"verification\", \"quality-gate\", \"specification\", \"review\", \"evidence\"],\n  13   \"skills\": \"./skills/\",\n  14   \"interface\": {\n  15     \"displayName\": \"Sentinel\",\n  16     \"shortDescription\": \"Gate work with evidence\",\n  17     \"longDescription\": \"Verify requirements, specs, plans, implementations, and pull requests against their stated criteria before the next lifecycle stage.\",\n  18     \"developerName\": \"Gabriel Castro (Orin DX)\",\n  19     \"category\": \"Developer Tools\",\n  20     \"capabilities\": [\"Interactive\", \"Read\"],\n  21     \"websiteURL\": \"https://github.com/orin-dx/agent-plugins\",\n  22     \"defaultPrompt\": [\"Gate this spec before implementation\", \"Verify this PR against its criteria\", \"Is this artifact ready for the next stage?\"]\n  23   }\n  24 }\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"   1 # Changelog\n   2 \n   3 — sentinel\n   4 \n   5 ## [2.1.1] - 2026-09-09\n   6 \n   7 ### Changed\n   8 - Agents now report blockers and material concerns directly with evidence, consequence, and calibrated confidence.\n   9 \n  10 ## [2.1.0] - 2026-09-09\n  11 \n  12 ### Changed\n  13 - Exit-gate returns `verdict@3`, separating blockers, coverage gaps, and unfinished checks.\n  14 - Mutable external evidence is refreshed near the verdict; started checks must reach a terminal result before pass.\n  15 \n  16 ## [2.0.0] - 2026-08-27\n  17 \n  18 ### Changed\n  19 - **BREAKING**: Plugin ID renamed from `axiom` to `sentinel` as part of the ecosystem-wide Wisp persona-naming rename — abstract math-noun plugin ids became hard to recall as the ecosystem grew past 9 plugins. This is the verification gate plugin; its skill also renamed from `axiom/axiom` to `sentinel/gate`. See `docs/adr/007-wisp-persona-naming.md` for the full rationale and old→new mapping. `plugin.json` `id`/`name` updated; every cross-plugin reference across the ecosystem updated to match.\n  20 \n  21 ## [1.2.3] - 2026-08-23\n  22 \n  23 ### Fixed\n  24 - `skills/axiom/SKILL.md` linked `shared/agent-best-practices.md` — an authoring-time-only guide with no legitimate runtime purpose for a verification gate, unlike `basis`'s scaffolding skills where it's the actual job. Copy-paste leftover from scaffolding. Found by an adversarial audit of the one plugin untouched all session.\n  25 \n  26 ## [1.2.2] - 2026-08-22\n  27 \n  28 ### Changed\n  29 - `recon`'s `reasoning` scratchpad capped to 1-2 sentences — discarded, not read by a human, mechanical enumeration doesn't need more.\n  30 - All agents now carry a `<constitution>` section — see root CHANGELOG and ADR-006. No routing or output behavior changed.\n  31 \n  32 ## [1.2.1] - 2026-08-21\n  33 \n  34 ### Fixed\n  35 - `SKILL.md` and `README.md` each documented a \"sub-skills\" table, and the two disagreed with each other — `SKILL.md` listed six (`verify-requirement`, `verify-spec`, `verify-plan`, `verify-implementation`, `verify-pr`, `exit-gate`), `README.md` listed two (`axiom/gate`, `axiom/verify`). Both were fiction: axiom has exactly one skill directory and one three-agent pipeline (`recon` → `verifier` → `exit-gate`), reused unchanged against whatever artifact type it's handed. Replaced both with one consistent description.\n   1 {\n   2   \"id\": \"sentinel\",\n   3   \"name\": \"sentinel\",\n   4   \"version\": \"2.1.1\",\n   5   \"description\": \"Cross-cutting verification gate. Confirms any stage artifact meets its criteria before the next stage begins. Implements the sentinel protocol: recon \\u2192 verify \\u2192 exit-gate with retry-with-feedback.\",\n   6   \"author\": {\n   7     \"name\": \"Gabriel Castro\",\n   8     \"email\": \"gabe@techworx.dev\"\n   9   },\n  10   \"skills\": \"./skills/\",\n  11   \"agents\": [\n  12     \"./agents/exit-gate.md\",\n  13     \"./agents/recon.md\",\n  14     \"./agents/verifier.md\"\n  15   ],\n  16   \"consumes\": [],\n  17   \"produces\": [\n  18     \"verdict@3\"\n  19   ]\n  20 }\n   1 # sentinel — Verification Gate\n   2 \n   3 **Stage:** Gate · **Output:** `verdict@3` · **Version:** 2.1.1\n   4 \n   5 One skill, one artifact-agnostic pipeline. Sentinel returns a scoped `verdict@3`: pass or fail, with blockers, coverage gaps, and pending checks. Unverifiable required criteria fail unless explicitly waived.\n   6 \n   7 Sentinel is standalone. Its `plugin.json` declares `consumes: []`, and nothing invokes it automatically. Scribe and Smith have dedicated exit gates for their own artifacts; neither calls Sentinel's agents. Install Sentinel for an on-demand independent gate against any artifact.\n   8 \n   9 ---\n  10 \n  11 ## When to Use\n  12 \n  13 - You want to verify a spec, plan, or changeset against a defined set of criteria\n  14 - You want an independent second opinion on whether an implementation meets its spec\n  15 - You need a formal pass/fail verdict with specific blockers before shipping\n  16 - You want to check a finding report for completeness before handing it off\n  17 \n  18 **Invoke with:** `\"Gate this spec\"`, `\"Verify this implementation against the spec\"`, `\"Run the verification gate\"`, `\"Check whether this changeset meets all acceptance criteria\"`, `\"Is this finding report complete?\"`\n  19 \n  20 ---\n  21 \n  22 ## Install\n  23 \n  24 **Claude Code** — add the marketplace once, then install by ID:\n  25 ```\n  26 /plugin marketplace add orin-dx/agent-plugins\n  27 /plugin install sentinel\n  28 ```\n  29 \n  30 **AGY** — installs the full repo; see the [root README](../../README.md#install) for instructions.\n  31 \n  32 **Codex** — see the [root Codex setup](../../README.md#codex), then run `codex plugin add sentinel@wisp-plugins`.\n  33 \n  34 ---\n  35 \n   1 {\n   2   \"name\": \"sentinel\",\n   3   \"version\": \"2.1.1\",\n   4   \"description\": \"Independently gate lifecycle artifacts against explicit criteria before they cross a workflow boundary.\",\n   5   \"author\": {\n   6     \"name\": \"Gabriel Castro\",\n   7     \"email\": \"gabe@techworx.dev\"\n   8   },\n   9   \"homepage\": \"https://github.com/orin-dx/agent-plugins\",\n  10   \"repository\": \"https://github.com/orin-dx/agent-plugins\",\n  11   \"license\": \"MIT\",\n  12   \"keywords\": [\"verification\", \"quality-gate\", \"specification\", \"review\", \"evidence\"],\n  13   \"skills\": \"./skills/\",\n  14   \"interface\": {\n  15     \"displayName\": \"Sentinel\",\n  16     \"shortDescription\": \"Gate work with evidence\",\n  17     \"longDescription\": \"Verify requirements, specs, plans, implementations, and pull requests against their stated criteria before the next lifecycle stage.\",\n  18     \"developerName\": \"Gabriel Castro (Orin DX)\",\n  19     \"category\": \"Developer Tools\",\n  20     \"capabilities\": [\"Interactive\", \"Read\"],\n  21     \"websiteURL\": \"https://github.com/orin-dx/agent-plugins\",\n  22     \"defaultPrompt\": [\"Gate this spec before implementation\", \"Verify this PR against its criteria\", \"Is this artifact ready for the next stage?\"]\n  23   }\n  24 }\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2167},"formatted_output":"   1 # Changelog\n   2 \n   3 — sentinel\n   4 \n   5 ## [2.1.1] - 2026-09-09\n   6 \n   7 ### Changed\n   8 - Agents now report blockers and material concerns directly with evidence, consequence, and calibrated confidence.\n   9 \n  10 ## [2.1.0] - 2026-09-09\n  11 \n  12 ### Changed\n  13 - Exit-gate returns `verdict@3`, separating blockers, coverage gaps, and unfinished checks.\n  14 - Mutable external evidence is refreshed near the verdict; started checks must reach a terminal result before pass.\n  15 \n  16 ## [2.0.0] - 2026-08-27\n  17 \n  18 ### Changed\n  19 - **BREAKING**: Plugin ID renamed from `axiom` to `sentinel` as part of the ecosystem-wide Wisp persona-naming rename — abstract math-noun plugin ids became hard to recall as the ecosystem grew past 9 plugins. This is the verification gate plugin; its skill also renamed from `axiom/axiom` to `sentinel/gate`. See `docs/adr/007-wisp-persona-naming.md` for the full rationale and old→new mapping. `plugin.json` `id`/`name` updated; every cross-plugin reference across the ecosystem updated to match.\n  20 \n  21 ## [1.2.3] - 2026-08-23\n  22 \n  23 ### Fixed\n  24 - `skills/axiom/SKILL.md` linked `shared/agent-best-practices.md` — an authoring-time-only guide with no legitimate runtime purpose for a verification gate, unlike `basis`'s scaffolding skills where it's the actual job. Copy-paste leftover from scaffolding. Found by an adversarial audit of the one plugin untouched all session.\n  25 \n  26 ## [1.2.2] - 2026-08-22\n  27 \n  28 ### Changed\n  29 - `recon`'s `reasoning` scratchpad capped to 1-2 sentences — discarded, not read by a human, mechanical enumeration doesn't need more.\n  30 - All agents now carry a `<constitution>` section — see root CHANGELOG and ADR-006. No routing or output behavior changed.\n  31 \n  32 ## [1.2.1] - 2026-08-21\n  33 \n  34 ### Fixed\n  35 - `SKILL.md` and `README.md` each documented a \"sub-skills\" table, and the two disagreed with each other — `SKILL.md` listed six (`verify-requirement`, `verify-spec`, `verify-plan`, `verify-implementation`, `verify-pr`, `exit-gate`), `README.md` listed two (`axiom/gate`, `axiom/verify`). Both were fiction: axiom has exactly one skill directory and one three-agent pipeline (`recon` → `verifier` → `exit-gate`), reused unchanged against whatever artifact type it's handed. Replaced both with one consistent description.\n   1 {\n   2   \"id\": \"sentinel\",\n   3   \"name\": \"sentinel\",\n   4   \"version\": \"2.1.1\",\n   5   \"description\": \"Cross-cutting verification gate. Confirms any stage artifact meets its criteria before the next stage begins. Implements the sentinel protocol: recon \\u2192 verify \\u2192 exit-gate with retry-with-feedback.\",\n   6   \"author\": {\n   7     \"name\": \"Gabriel Castro\",\n   8     \"email\": \"gabe@techworx.dev\"\n   9   },\n  10   \"skills\": \"./skills/\",\n  11   \"agents\": [\n  12     \"./agents/exit-gate.md\",\n  13     \"./agents/recon.md\",\n  14     \"./agents/verifier.md\"\n  15   ],\n  16   \"consumes\": [],\n  17   \"produces\": [\n  18     \"verdict@3\"\n  19   ]\n  20 }\n   1 # sentinel — Verification Gate\n   2 \n   3 **Stage:** Gate · **Output:** `verdict@3` · **Version:** 2.1.1\n   4 \n   5 One skill, one artifact-agnostic pipeline. Sentinel returns a scoped `verdict@3`: pass or fail, with blockers, coverage gaps, and pending checks. Unverifiable required criteria fail unless explicitly waived.\n   6 \n   7 Sentinel is standalone. Its `plugin.json` declares `consumes: []`, and nothing invokes it automatically. Scribe and Smith have dedicated exit gates for their own artifacts; neither calls Sentinel's agents. Install Sentinel for an on-demand independent gate against any artifact.\n   8 \n   9 ---\n  10 \n  11 ## When to Use\n  12 \n  13 - You want to verify a spec, plan, or changeset against a defined set of criteria\n  14 - You want an independent second opinion on whether an implementation meets its spec\n  15 - You need a formal pass/fail verdict with specific blockers before shipping\n  16 - You want to check a finding report for completeness before handing it off\n  17 \n  18 **Invoke with:** `\"Gate this spec\"`, `\"Verify this implementation against the spec\"`, `\"Run the verification gate\"`, `\"Check whether this changeset meets all acceptance criteria\"`, `\"Is this finding report complete?\"`\n  19 \n  20 ---\n  21 \n  22 ## Install\n  23 \n  24 **Claude Code** — add the marketplace once, then install by ID:\n  25 ```\n  26 /plugin marketplace add orin-dx/agent-plugins\n  27 /plugin install sentinel\n  28 ```\n  29 \n  30 **AGY** — installs the full repo; see the [root README](../../README.md#install) for instructions.\n  31 \n  32 **Codex** — see the [root Codex setup](../../README.md#codex), then run `codex plugin add sentinel@wisp-plugins`.\n  33 \n  34 ---\n  35 \n   1 {\n   2   \"name\": \"sentinel\",\n   3   \"version\": \"2.1.1\",\n   4   \"description\": \"Independently gate lifecycle artifacts against explicit criteria before they cross a workflow boundary.\",\n   5   \"author\": {\n   6     \"name\": \"Gabriel Castro\",\n   7     \"email\": \"gabe@techworx.dev\"\n   8   },\n   9   \"homepage\": \"https://github.com/orin-dx/agent-plugins\",\n  10   \"repository\": \"https://github.com/orin-dx/agent-plugins\",\n  11   \"license\": \"MIT\",\n  12   \"keywords\": [\"verification\", \"quality-gate\", \"specification\", \"review\", \"evidence\"],\n  13   \"skills\": \"./skills/\",\n  14   \"interface\": {\n  15     \"displayName\": \"Sentinel\",\n  16     \"shortDescription\": \"Gate work with evidence\",\n  17     \"longDescription\": \"Verify requirements, specs, plans, implementations, and pull requests against their stated criteria before the next lifecycle stage.\",\n  18     \"developerName\": \"Gabriel Castro (Orin DX)\",\n  19     \"category\": \"Developer Tools\",\n  20     \"capabilities\": [\"Interactive\", \"Read\"],\n  21     \"websiteURL\": \"https://github.com/orin-dx/agent-plugins\",\n  22     \"defaultPrompt\": [\"Gate this spec before implementation\", \"Verify this PR against its criteria\", \"Is this artifact ready for the next stage?\"]\n  23   }\n  24 }\n"},"started_at_ms":1789067826838,"completed_at_ms":1789067826838}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-10T19:17:06.878Z","ordinal":9391,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a08cc0-96be-70f1-89a0-85481139afee","call_id":"call_bM9A5wQQGI8UmLg1kxxE0lHI","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"CHANGELOG.md:29:- Smith 2.4.0 and Ranger 3.2.0 adopt the new contracts. Scribe 3.3.0 consumes their structural escalations. Sentinel 2.1.0 returns scoped verdicts.\nCHANGELOG.md:69:`smith` (2.1.0) and `navigator` (2.1.0): dropped the requirement that a task's test be written and confirmed failing before its implementation. Tasks now specify a brief implementation approach, the exact implementation, and the exact tests proving each criterion — test quality is verified by `smith`'s existing mutation-testing gate, not by write order. Rationale is a controlled comparison finding no quality advantage from write-test-first ordering for agent-written code, at several times the token cost, and that it suppressed upfront design work agents otherwise did well. See `docs/adr/008-drop-test-first-ordering.md`.\nCHANGELOG.md:83:| `axiom` | `sentinel` | 2.0.0 |\nCHANGELOG.md:88:Single-skill plugins also renamed their skill from the old plugin id to a literal action word: `proof/proof` → `ranger/audit`, `trace/trace` → `vanguard/research`, `vector/vector` → `navigator/plan`, `axiom/axiom` → `sentinel/gate`, `lambda/lambda` → `smith/implement`. Multi-skill plugins (`weaver`, `scribe`, `courier`, `mason`) kept their existing skill names — only the plugin-id prefix changed.\nCHANGELOG.md:122:- **`basis` (v2.1.0)**: `scaffolder` generates `<load_first>` and drafts SKILL.md routing entries; `auditor` gained `<load_first>`-correctness and orchestration-completeness checks, and now runs the repo's own validation scripts for what they already check deterministically instead of re-deriving it through reasoning.\nCHANGELOG.md:159:- **`canon` (v2.1.0)**: `auditor` gained a sixth check dimension, `unnecessary-prose`.\nCHANGELOG.md:175:- **`delta` (v2.1.0)**: `changeset-analyzer` now checks whether a diff contains multiple independent topics before classifying anything, and emits one `changeset@2` per topic instead of a single bundled entry. A single PR still produces exactly one changeset, unchanged — the new behavior only engages on a backlog/catch-up diff spanning several unrelated tracks since the last release. Prompted by a real incident in a sibling repo (`callisto`, a separate release-automation tool) where a large diff got bundled into one changeset covering unrelated tracks with package attribution guessed from a written summary instead of verified per-topic against the actual diff. `changeset-analyzer`'s output contract changes from a single object to an array of one-or-more; `delta/changeset`'s `<io>` section and `shared/references/changesets.md` updated to match, with the split rule stated as a named failure mode (bundling for convenience costs more than a spurious split).\nREADME.md:91:| **Verify** | [`sentinel`](./plugins/sentinel/) | Cross-cutting gate — confirms any artifact meets its criteria before the next stage begins | `verdict@3` |\nREADME.md:128:Add `muse` for component specifications, `sentinel` and `ranger` for verification, `courier` for shipping work, and `mason` for plugin authoring:\nREADME.md:132:/plugin install sentinel\nREADME.md:158:Other available selectors are `muse`, `sentinel`, `ranger`, `courier`, and `mason`. Inspect marketplace availability with:\nREADME.md:209:agy-plugin add sentinel@wisp-plugins\nREADME.md:266:| `spec@1` | scribe, muse | navigator, sentinel |\nREADME.md:272:| `changeset@2` | courier | courier release, sentinel |\nREADME.md:273:| `verdict@3` | scribe, smith, ranger, sentinel | gate consumers and humans |\nREADME.md:330:    ├── sentinel/                  ← Verification gate\nmarketplace.json:27:      \"id\": \"sentinel\",\nmarketplace.json:28:      \"name\": \"sentinel\",\nmarketplace.json:30:      \"path\": \"./plugins/sentinel\",\nplugins/sentinel/README.md:1:# sentinel — Verification Gate\nplugins/sentinel/README.md:27:/plugin install sentinel\nplugins/sentinel/README.md:32:**Codex** — see the [root Codex setup](../../README.md#codex), then run `codex plugin add sentinel@wisp-plugins`.\nplugins/sentinel/README.md:48:Sentinel is a single skill (`sentinel:sentinel` — the frontmatter `name: gate` is a cosmetic label, not the routing key; skills route by directory name) — not a set of per-artifact-type sub-skills. The same three-agent chain runs unchanged no matter what you hand it; `recon` is what determines the artifact type and derives its criteria, so nothing about the pipeline itself needs to vary by type.\nplugins/sentinel/agents/recon.md:7:  Delegate to this subagent at the start of the sentinel gate protocol when an artifact must be inventoried before verification begins. Input is an artifact path or description. The agent determines the artifact type, locates it in the workspace, enumerates the criteria it must be verified against (derived from a linked spec or requirement, or from the artifact's own acceptance criteria section if none is linked), and identifies source files to cross-check during verification. This is purely mechanical discovery — the agent produces no pass/fail opinion. Output is a JSON manifest with artifact_type, artifact_path, criteria array, source_files array, and a reasoning scratchpad. Do not delegate here for judgment; route the manifest to verifier for criterion cross-reference.\nplugins/sentinel/plugin.json:2:  \"id\": \"sentinel\",\nplugins/sentinel/plugin.json:3:  \"name\": \"sentinel\",\nplugins/sentinel/plugin.json:5:  \"description\": \"Cross-cutting verification gate. Confirms any stage artifact meets its criteria before the next stage begins. Implements the sentinel protocol: recon \\u2192 verify \\u2192 exit-gate with retry-with-feedback.\",\nplugins/sentinel/CHANGELOG.md:3:— sentinel\nplugins/sentinel/CHANGELOG.md:10:## [2.1.0] - 2026-09-09\nplugins/sentinel/CHANGELOG.md:19:- **BREAKING**: Plugin ID renamed from `axiom` to `sentinel` as part of the ecosystem-wide Wisp persona-naming rename — abstract math-noun plugin ids became hard to recall as the ecosystem grew past 9 plugins. This is the verification gate plugin; its skill also renamed from `axiom/axiom` to `sentinel/gate`. See `docs/adr/007-wisp-persona-naming.md` for the full rationale and old→new mapping. `plugin.json` `id`/`name` updated; every cross-plugin reference across the ecosystem updated to match.\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"   1 # Changelog\n   2 \n   3 — sentinel\n   4 \n   5 ## [2.1.1] - 2026-09-09\n   6 \n   7 ### Changed\n   8 - Agents now report blockers and material concerns directly with evidence, consequence, and calibrated confidence.\n   9 \n  10 ## [2.1.0] - 2026-09-09\n  11 \n  12 ### Changed\n  13 - Exit-gate returns `verdict@3`, separating blockers, coverage gaps, and unfinished checks.\n  14 - Mutable external evidence is refreshed near the verdict; started checks must reach a terminal result before pass.\n  15 \n  16 ## [2.0.0] - 2026-08-27\n  17 \n  18 ### Changed\n  19 - **BREAKING**: Plugin ID renamed from `axiom` to `sentinel` as part of the ecosystem-wide Wisp persona-naming rename — abstract math-noun plugin ids became hard to recall as the ecosystem grew past 9 plugins. This is the verification gate plugin; its skill also renamed from `axiom/axiom` to `sentinel/gate`. See `docs/adr/007-wisp-persona-naming.md` for the full rationale and old→new mapping. `plugin.json` `id`/`name` updated; every cross-plugin reference across the ecosystem updated to match.\n  20 \n  21 ## [1.2.3] - 2026-08-23\n  22 \n  23 ### Fixed\n  24 - `skills/axiom/SKILL.md` linked `shared/agent-best-practices.md` — an authoring-time-only guide with no legitimate runtime purpose for a verification gate, unlike `basis`'s scaffolding skills where it's the actual job. Copy-paste leftover from scaffolding. Found by an adversarial audit of the one plugin untouched all session.\n  25 \n  26 ## [1.2.2] - 2026-08-22\n  27 \n  28 ### Changed\n  29 - `recon`'s `reasoning` scratchpad capped to 1-2 sentences — discarded, not read by a human, mechanical enumeration doesn't need more.\n  30 - All agents now carry a `<constitution>` section — see root CHANGELOG and ADR-006. No routing or output behavior changed.\n  31 \n  32 ## [1.2.1] - 2026-08-21\n  33 \n  34 ### Fixed\n  35 - `SKILL.md` and `README.md` each documented a \"sub-skills\" table, and the two disagreed with each other — `SKILL.md` listed six (`verify-requirement`, `verify-spec`, `verify-plan`, `verify-implementation`, `verify-pr`, `exit-gate`), `README.md` listed two (`axiom/gate`, `axiom/verify`). Both were fiction: axiom has exactly one skill directory and one three-agent pipeline (`recon` → `verifier` → `exit-gate`), reused unchanged against whatever artifact type it's handed. Replaced both with one consistent description.\n   1 {\n   2   \"id\": \"sentinel\",\n   3   \"name\": \"sentinel\",\n   4   \"version\": \"2.1.1\",\n   5   \"description\": \"Cross-cutting verification gate. Confirms any stage artifact meets its criteria before the next stage begins. Implements the sentinel protocol: recon \\u2192 verify \\u2192 exit-gate with retry-with-feedback.\",\n   6   \"author\": {\n   7     \"name\": \"Gabriel Castro\",\n   8     \"email\": \"gabe@techworx.dev\"\n   9   },\n  10   \"skills\": \"./skills/\",\n  11   \"agents\": [\n  12     \"./agents/exit-gate.md\",\n  13     \"./agents/recon.md\",\n  14     \"./agents/verifier.md\"\n  15   ],\n  16   \"consumes\": [],\n  17   \"produces\": [\n  18     \"verdict@3\"\n  19   ]\n  20 }\n   1 # sentinel — Verification Gate\n   2 \n   3 **Stage:** Gate · **Output:** `verdict@3` · **Version:** 2.1.1\n   4 \n   5 One skill, one artifact-agnostic pipeline. Sentinel returns a scoped `verdict@3`: pass or fail, with blockers, coverage gaps, and pending checks. Unverifiable required criteria fail unless explicitly waived.\n   6 \n   7 Sentinel is standalone. Its `plugin.json` declares `consumes: []`, and nothing invokes it automatically. Scribe and Smith have dedicated exit gates for their own artifacts; neither calls Sentinel's agents. Install Sentinel for an on-demand independent gate against any artifact.\n   8 \n   9 ---\n  10 \n  11 ## When to Use\n  12 \n  13 - You want to verify a spec, plan, or changeset against a defined set of criteria\n  14 - You want an independent second opinion on whether an implementation meets its spec\n  15 - You need a formal pass/fail verdict with specific blockers before shipping\n  16 - You want to check a finding report for completeness before handing it off\n  17 \n  18 **Invoke with:** `\"Gate this spec\"`, `\"Verify this implementation against the spec\"`, `\"Run the verification gate\"`, `\"Check whether this changeset meets all acceptance criteria\"`, `\"Is this finding report complete?\"`\n  19 \n  20 ---\n  21 \n  22 ## Install\n  23 \n  24 **Claude Code** — add the marketplace once, then install by ID:\n  25 ```\n  26 /plugin marketplace add orin-dx/agent-plugins\n  27 /plugin install sentinel\n  28 ```\n  29 \n  30 **AGY** — installs the full repo; see the [root README](../../README.md#install) for instructions.\n  31 \n  32 **Codex** — see the [root Codex setup](../../README.md#codex), then run `codex plugin add sentinel@wisp-plugins`.\n  33 \n  34 ---\n  35 \n   1 {\n   2   \"name\": \"sentinel\",\n   3   \"version\": \"2.1.1\",\n   4   \"description\": \"Independently gate lifecycle artifacts against explicit criteria before they cross a workflow boundary.\",\n   5   \"author\": {\n   6     \"name\": \"Gabriel Castro\",\n   7     \"email\": \"gabe@techworx.dev\"\n   8   },\n   9   \"homepage\": \"https://github.com/orin-dx/agent-plugins\",\n  10   \"repository\": \"https://github.com/orin-dx/agent-plugins\",\n  11   \"license\": \"MIT\",\n  12   \"keywords\": [\"verification\", \"quality-gate\", \"specification\", \"review\", \"evidence\"],\n  13   \"skills\": \"./skills/\",\n  14   \"interface\": {\n  15     \"displayName\": \"Sentinel\",\n  16     \"shortDescription\": \"Gate work with evidence\",\n  17     \"longDescription\": \"Verify requirements, specs, plans, implementations, and pull requests against their stated criteria before the next lifecycle stage.\",\n  18     \"developerName\": \"Gabriel Castro (Orin DX)\",\n  19     \"category\": \"Developer Tools\",\n  20     \"capabilities\": [\"Interactive\", \"Read\"],\n  21     \"websiteURL\": \"https://github.com/orin-dx/agent-plugins\",\n  22     \"defaultPrompt\": [\"Gate this spec before implementation\", \"Verify this PR against its criteria\", \"Is this artifact ready for the next stage?\"]\n  23   }\n  24 }\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"   1 ---\n   2 name: gate\n   3 description: Independently verify an artifact against its criteria before it crosses a lifecycle boundary. Use for requirements, specs, plans, implementations, PRs, and explicit readiness checks.\n   4 ---\n   5 \n   6 State material concerns, evidence, consequence, and confidence directly. Do not soften blockers, manufacture praise, or claim certainty beyond the evidence.\n   7 \n   8 # Gate a lifecycle artifact with evidence\n   9 \n  10 Sentinel judges an existing artifact; it does not author a replacement. Begin by identifying what the artifact claims, where it is persisted, and which criteria actually govern it.\n  11 \n  12 ## Workflow\n  13 \n  14 1. Identify `artifact_type`, `artifact_path`, the authoritative criteria, linked artifacts, and source files that can prove or disprove each criterion.\n  15 2. Read persisted files when a path is present rather than relying on conversation summaries.\n  16 3. Build an evidence table that classifies each criterion as verified, failed, or unverifiable, with exact paths and test or inspection evidence.\n  17 4. Produce `verdict@3` conforming to `shared/schemas/verdict@3.json`. Name the verified scope, blockers, coverage gaps, and pending checks.\n  18 5. Pass only when every applicable criterion is verified. On failure, return specific blockers that the producing workflow can act on.\n  19 \n  20 ## Retry protocol\n  21 \n  22 On a retry, inspect only the revised artifact and the previous blockers. Do not reopen unrelated review scope. Set `retry_count` accurately and escalate to the caller after the third failed retry rather than looping indefinitely.\n  23 \n  24 ## Evidence and safety\n  25 \n  26 - Do not treat a passing test alone as proof of a criterion it does not exercise.\n  27 - Mark evidence unverifiable when the relevant source, environment, or criterion is absent; do not convert uncertainty into a pass.\n  28 - Treat a required check that has not finished as pending and fail the gate.\n  29 - Do not edit the artifact, create a PR, or publish a verdict externally unless the user asks for a separate action.\n  30 \n  31 ## Team use\n  32 \n  33 Before delegating, read `agent-roles/README.md`; use `recon` for fixed evidence collection, but reserve the binding decision for the primary `judge`.\n  34 \n  35 When teams are available, split criterion verification only across independent subsystems after the artifact and criteria are fixed. The owner reconciles coverage and delivers one binding verdict. Without teams, verify each criterion sequentially with the same standard.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a08cbd-ae2c-7ca0-9da8-fdd13eaa77fa","create_time":1789067826.87842}}}
